<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-marble="1">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<meta name="marble:capabilities" content="storage; net=none">
<title>Research Vision</title>
<style>
  /* Research Vision — one pool of ideas, seen many ways.

     v1 held six representations and wrote the same idea into all of them.
     World Interface Models existed as a thesis bullet, a project card, a map
     node, a principle, three questions and a triage tile: eight copies, none
     of which knew about the others. That is why the views drifted. This
     document replaced it; v1 lives in Archive/ beside it, as an older version
     rather than a second source of truth.

     Here every idea is exactly one <article class="item"> in one .pool. A
     "view" is not another copy, it is a different CSS layout over those same
     elements — so editing a title in the map edits it in the board, because
     there is nothing else to edit.

     Three attributes on .stage decide what you see:
       data-arrange  how the pool is laid out (thesis, form, term, when, map, list, focus)
       data-show     which parts of each item are worth reading right now
       data-focus    which single thesis "focus" narrows the list to
     A preset button writes arrange and show at once; the focus dial writes
     arrange and focus the same way. Nothing else is stored: the rail
     highlight is derived from those attributes, never written down.

     The band above the pool is a reading of the pool, not a second home for
     a card. Being current work is data-now on the pool card; the band shows
     a reference. Dragging into the band pins that attribute. The card stays
     in its thesis column. */
  :root {
    color-scheme: light dark;
    --ink: #1b1f23;
    --ink-2: #3b444d;
    --muted: #5b6670;
    --faint: #8a949e;
    --line: #e3e5e6;
    --hair: rgba(27, 31, 35, .09);
    --paper: #fbfaf8;
    --card: #ffffff;
    --accent: #2f6f5b;
    --accent-soft: #ecf3ef;
    --accent-ink: #26594a;
    /* the mark families, one hue each; every shade is mixed from it */
    --m-cool: #8a949e;
    --m-warm: #b0762a;
    --m-live: #2f6f5b;
    --m-done: #3f6cae;
    --r: 8px; --r-2: 5px;
    /* three spine columns at their widest, their gutters, and the thesis rail */
    --ui-max: 1636px;
    --serif: ui-serif, "Iowan Old Style", "Palatino", Georgia, serif;
    --sans: system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
  }
  /* Dark is the same paper unlit, not a second theme.

     Two wrong answers came first, and both were about temperature. This block
     began as a cool slate — hsl(210 12% 9%), the default blue you get by
     reaching for "a neutral dark" — which fought the warm --paper above. The
     correction for that overshot into brown, because warmth is not symmetric
     near black: the same 6-7 points of R-B that pass unnoticed as slate read
     as mud the moment you flip them warm.

     So this ramp has no temperature at all. Every neutral below is R=G=B, and
     each is the exact luminance of the cool value it replaced — #171717 sits
     where #15181b sat, #1e1e1e where #1b1f23 did. Light mode keeps its warm
     paper and dark simply declines to answer it: a grey that is not trying to
     be anything is the one neither cast can spoil.

     The accent and the four mark families keep their hues. They are marks on
     the paper, not the paper — and --m-cool stays equal to --faint here exactly
     as it does in light mode, so "dormant" is the page's own grey in both. */
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root {
      --ink: #e8e8e8;
      --ink-2: #cacaca;
      --muted: #a2a2a2;
      --faint: #767676;
      --line: #303030;
      --hair: rgba(255, 255, 255, .10);
      --paper: #171717;
      --card: #1e1e1e;
      --accent: #6fbfa2;
      --accent-soft: #1f2a26;
      --accent-ink: #8fd0ba;
      --m-cool: #767676;
      --m-warm: #d29a5c;
      --m-live: #6fbfa2;
      --m-done: #83a6dc;
    }
  }
  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  /* Every width answer below is asked of this element, not of the window. A
     host that docks a panel beside the page shrinks <html> and leaves the
     viewport exactly where it was, so a media query would go on insisting
     there is room that is no longer there. */
  html { container: doc / inline-size; }
  body {
    margin: 0; background: var(--paper); color: var(--ink);
    font: 15px/1.6 var(--sans);
    -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
    display: flex; min-height: 100vh;
  }
  h1, h2, h3, h4 { margin: 0; font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -.012em; }
  p { margin: 0; }
  .kicker { display: block; font: 600 10px/1 var(--sans); letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); }

  /* ── rail ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .rail {
    width: 232px; min-width: 232px; padding: 26px 20px 40px;
    position: sticky; top: 0; height: 100vh; overflow-y: auto;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--hair);
  }
  .rail-title { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 19px; line-height: 1.22; font-weight: 600; margin: 9px 0 4px; }
  .rail-sub { font-size: 12px; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.5; margin-bottom: 22px; }

  /* rail-versions holds no fact of its own — its content is read fresh from
     marble.docs() every time it paints, never written into the file, because
     "which documents are in this folder" is the folder's answer, not this
     one's to keep a copy of. */
  .rail-versions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 5px; margin: -10px 0 20px; }
  .rail-versions[hidden] { display: none; }
  .version-pill {
    font: 500 11px/1 var(--sans); padding: 4px 9px; border-radius: 999px;
    border: 1px solid var(--hair); color: var(--muted); text-decoration: none;
  }
  a.version-pill:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
  .version-pill.is-current { background: var(--accent-soft); border-color: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent-ink); font-weight: 600; }

  .rail-group { font: 600 10px/1 var(--sans); letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--faint); margin: 18px 0 9px; }
  .rail-group:first-of-type { margin-top: 0; }
  .nav { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .nav button {
    display: flex; gap: 9px; align-items: baseline; width: 100%; text-align: left;
    padding: 6px 0 6px 11px; border: 0; border-left: 1px solid var(--hair); background: none;
    font: inherit; font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted); cursor: pointer;
    transition: color .16s, border-color .16s;
  }
  .nav button:hover { color: var(--ink-2); border-left-color: var(--faint); }
  .nav button.marble-on { color: var(--accent); border-left: 1px solid var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }
  .nav .n { font: 500 10px/1 var(--sans); color: var(--faint); min-width: 12px; }

  /* the two dials, underneath the presets — the same two attributes the
     presets write, exposed so a combination nobody named is still reachable */
  .dial { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 4px; }
  .dial button {
    font: inherit; font-size: 11px; color: var(--muted); cursor: pointer;
    padding: 3px 7px; border: 1px solid var(--hair); border-radius: 999px;
    background: none; transition: border-color .16s, color .16s;
  }
  .dial button:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
  .dial button.marble-on { background: var(--accent-soft); border-color: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent-ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .rail-note { font-size: 11px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--faint); padding-top: 14px; margin-top: 18px; border-top: 1px solid var(--hair); }

  /* ── stage ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .stage { flex: 1; min-width: 0; padding: 0 0 200px; max-width: var(--stage-max, 1340px); }
  /* The arrangements that stand cards side by side are worth the whole site:
     what keeps a line of prose readable is the width of a column, not the
     width of the page, so each one caps its own columns instead and the
     stage is free to take everything it is given. */
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"],
  .stage[data-arrange="form"],
  .stage[data-arrange="when"],
  .stage[data-arrange="term"],
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] { --stage-max: none; }
  .stage-in { padding: 0 34px; }
  .vhead { margin: 26px 0 18px; max-width: 62em; }
  .vhead h2 { font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.18; margin: 6px 0 0; }
  .vhead .sub { color: var(--ink-2); margin: 9px 0 0; max-width: 48em; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.55; }

  /* The north star is the whole document's premise, so it reads in the two
     arrangements meant for reading and stands down in the working views. */
  marble-alt.northstar { display: none; margin: 0 0 26px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] marble-alt.northstar,
  .stage[data-arrange="list"] marble-alt.northstar { display: block; }
  marble-alt.northstar > [data-marble-alt] {
    display: none; font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 18.5px; line-height: 1.42;
    font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: -.012em; color: var(--ink); max-width: 44em;
  }
  marble-alt.northstar[data-marble-active="v1"] > [data-marble-alt="v1"],
  marble-alt.northstar[data-marble-active="v2"] > [data-marble-alt="v2"],
  marble-alt.northstar[data-marble-active="v3"] > [data-marble-alt="v3"],
  marble-alt.northstar[data-marble-active="v4"] > [data-marble-alt="v4"],
  marble-alt.northstar[data-marble-active="v5"] > [data-marble-alt="v5"],
  marble-alt.northstar[data-marble-active="v6"] > [data-marble-alt="v6"] { display: block; }
  marble-alt.northstar:not([data-marble-active]) > [data-marble-alt]:first-child { display: block; }

  .bar { display: flex; gap: 8px; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .btn {
    font: inherit; font-size: 12px; color: var(--muted); cursor: pointer;
    padding: 5px 10px; border: 1px solid var(--hair); border-radius: var(--r-2); background: none;
    transition: border-color .16s, color .16s;
  }
  .btn:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
  .btn[aria-pressed="true"] { background: var(--accent-soft); border-color: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent-ink); }
  .count { font-size: 11px; color: var(--faint); margin-left: auto; }
  .pool-actions {
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 25;
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 7px; align-items: center;
    margin: 0 0 12px; padding: 10px 0 11px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 90%, var(--card));
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hair);
  }
  .pool-actions .count { margin-left: auto; }
  /* The stage is free to take the whole site now; a row read left to right is
     not. The toolbar, the band and its heading stop where the widest
     arrangement stops — three spine columns, their gutters and the thesis
     rail — so the count never ends up a screenful away from the buttons. */
  .pool-actions, .band-head, .band { max-width: var(--ui-max); }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .add-fallback { display: none; }

  .col-add {
    display: none; position: absolute; z-index: 3; box-sizing: border-box;
    margin: 0; padding: 7px 10px;
    font: inherit; font-size: 12px; color: var(--faint); cursor: pointer;
    background: transparent; border: 1px dashed var(--hair); border-radius: var(--r-2);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .col-add { display: block; }
  .col-add:hover, .col-add:focus-visible {
    color: var(--ink); border-color: var(--muted); background: var(--card); outline: none;
  }
  .col-add-thesis.is-rail {
    writing-mode: vertical-rl;
    letter-spacing: .1em;
    padding: 14px 0;
  }

  /* ── editing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  [data-marble-editable] { outline: none; border-radius: 3px; transition: background .14s; }
  [data-marble-editable]:hover { background: var(--hair); }
  [data-marble-editable]:focus { background: var(--hair); outline: 1px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }
  [data-marble-editable]:empty::after,
  [data-marble-editable].marble-empty::after { content: attr(data-ph); color: var(--faint); }
  [data-marble-editable].marble-empty > .marble-grip { display: none; }

  /* ── the band: current work, constant across every view ──────────────── */
  .band-wrap {
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 30;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 88%, var(--card));
    backdrop-filter: blur(8px);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hair);
    padding: 11px 34px 12px;
  }
  .band-head { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 9px; }
  .band-head h2 { font: 600 10px/1 var(--sans); letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); }
  .band-head .count { margin-left: auto; }
  /* Its layout never answers to data-arrange. That is the whole point of it:
     flip every view you like, this row does not move. */
  .band {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none;
    min-height: 52px; border-radius: var(--r);
    outline: 1px dashed transparent; outline-offset: 4px;
    transition: outline-color .14s ease, background .14s ease;
  }
  /* Emptiness has to be asked for, not matched: the band is written across
     several lines, so the whitespace between its cards means :empty is false
     the moment the last card leaves. The script says so with a class, and an
     empty band that says nothing is a band nobody discovers. */
  .band.is-empty::after {
    content: "Drag an idea up here to pin it — it stays in its column";
    font-size: 12px; color: var(--faint); font-style: italic; padding: 14px 2px;
  }
  /* While a card is in the air the band admits that it is somewhere to put
     it — otherwise it is a strip of nothing at the top of the page. */
  body.carrying .band,
  body.marble-dragging-now .band {
    outline-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 45%, transparent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 5%, transparent);
  }
  body.carrying .band.marble-target,
  body.marble-dragging-now .band.marble-target {
    outline-color: var(--accent); outline-style: solid;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 11%, transparent);
  }
  .now-ref {
    position: relative;
    flex: 0 1 auto; max-width: 270px; padding: 9px 28px 9px 12px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--r);
    background: var(--card); color: inherit;
    font: inherit; text-align: left; cursor: pointer;
  }
  .now-ref:hover, .now-ref:focus-visible {
    border-color: var(--accent); outline: none;
  }
  .now-ref-title {
    display: block; font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.32; padding-right: 3.2rem;
  }
  .now-ref-gloss {
    display: block; font-size: 11.5px; line-height: 1.35; color: var(--faint); margin-top: 2px;
  }
  .now-ref-gloss:empty { display: none; }
  .now-ref-form {
    position: absolute; right: 26px; top: 9px;
    font: 600 8px/1 var(--sans); letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 3px 6px; border-radius: 999px; border: 1px solid var(--hair);
    color: var(--muted); background: var(--card);
  }
  .now-ref-form:empty { display: none; }
  .now-ref[data-form="abstract"] > .now-ref-form {
    color: var(--m-warm); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--m-warm) 40%, transparent);
  }
  .now-ref[data-form="concrete"] > .now-ref-form,
  .now-ref[data-form="complete"] > .now-ref-form {
    color: var(--m-live); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--m-live) 45%, transparent);
    background: var(--accent-soft);
  }
  .now-ref[data-form="complete"] > .now-ref-form { color: #fff; background: var(--m-live); border-color: var(--m-live); }
  .now-ref-x {
    position: absolute; right: 5px; top: 5px;
    width: 18px; height: 18px; padding: 0; border: 0; border-radius: 4px;
    background: none; color: var(--faint); cursor: pointer;
    font: 600 13px/18px var(--sans);
  }
  .now-ref-x:hover, .now-ref-x:focus-visible { color: var(--accent-ink); background: var(--accent-soft); outline: none; }
  .pool > .item[data-now] {
    box-shadow: inset 3px 0 0 var(--accent);
  }
  .pool > .item.marble-now-flash {
    outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* ── the pool ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .pool { margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; position: relative; }

  /* list — everything in one column, longest reading */
  .stage[data-arrange="list"] .pool { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; }

  /* focus — one thesis, one column. data-focus on .stage says which; its own
     thesis card floats to the top as the heading, everything not carrying
     that thesis drops out, so reading one line of the agenda never means
     scrolling past the other two. */
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"] .pool { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"] .pool > .item[data-kind="thesis"] { order: -1; }
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-focus="t1"] .pool > .item:not([data-thesis~="t1"]),
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-focus="t2"] .pool > .item:not([data-thesis~="t2"]),
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-focus="t3"] .pool > .item:not([data-thesis~="t3"]) { display: none; }
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"] .pool > .item[data-kind="target"] { display: none; }

  /* focus, compact — the same list, collapsed to a title, its note, and (if
     it has one) the venue it's aimed at. Everything a card carries beyond
     that — the form/state chips, the claim, the open questions, the add
     link — folds away rather than a second copy of the card being kept. */
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item {
    padding-top: 10px; padding-bottom: 10px;
    transition: padding .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"] .pool > .item > .gloss,
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"] .pool > .item > .claim,
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"] .pool > .item > .beats,
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"] .pool > .item > .addlink {
    overflow: hidden; max-height: 80rem;
    transition:
      max-height .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1),
      opacity .24s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1),
      margin .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"] .pool > .item > .chip {
    transition: opacity .24s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .gloss,
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .claim,
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .beats,
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .addlink {
    max-height: 0; opacity: 0; margin: 0; pointer-events: none;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .chip {
    padding: 3px 6px;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > h3 { font-size: 13px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > h3::before { display: none; }
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .say { margin-top: 2px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="focus"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .meta { margin-top: 4px; }

  /* thesis — three independent stacks, packed in script. A CSS grid shares
     row tracks across columns, so a tall card in one thesis leaves a hole in
     the others. Each card's --spine-x/--spine-y is derived, never filed. */
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool {
    display: block; position: relative;
    min-height: var(--spine-h, 0px);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item {
    position: absolute;
    left: var(--spine-x, 0px) !important;
    top: var(--spine-y, 0px) !important;
    width: var(--spine-w, calc((100% - 24px) / 3));
    box-sizing: border-box;
    border-radius: 0;
    margin: 0;
    padding-top: 14px; padding-bottom: 14px;
    transition:
      padding .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item.marble-col-start {
    border-radius: var(--r) var(--r) 0 0; z-index: 1;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item.marble-col-end {
    border-radius: 0 0 var(--r) var(--r);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item.marble-col-start.marble-col-end {
    border-radius: var(--r);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-kind="thesis"] {
    min-height: var(--spine-th, 0px);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool.marble-measuring > .item[data-kind="thesis"] {
    min-height: 0;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-kind="thesis"] > h3::before {
    width: 0; height: 0;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > h3 {
    transition: font-size .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > h3::before {
    transition: width .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1), height .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .say,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .claim,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .beats,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .addlink,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .meta {
    overflow: hidden;
    max-height: 80rem;
    transition:
      max-height .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1),
      opacity .24s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1),
      margin .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1),
      padding .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .chip {
    transition: opacity .24s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .gloss {
    display: block; overflow: hidden; max-height: 0; opacity: 0; margin: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    transition:
      max-height .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1),
      opacity .24s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1),
      margin .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1);
  }
  @supports (interpolate-size: allow-keywords) {
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] { interpolate-size: allow-keywords; }
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .say,
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .claim,
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .beats,
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .addlink,
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .meta,
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .gloss {
      height: auto; max-height: none;
      transition:
        height .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1),
        opacity .24s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1),
        margin .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1),
        padding .32s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1);
    }
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .gloss { height: 0; }
  }

  /* thesis, compact — title, gloss, and the idea's status chip. */
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item {
    padding: 9px 12px 9px 24px;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .claim,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .beats,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .addlink,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .meta,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item:has(.gloss) > .say {
    max-height: 0; opacity: 0; margin: 0; padding: 0; pointer-events: none; border: 0;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .chip {
    padding: 3px 6px; font-size: 8px; letter-spacing: .07em;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .chip.form { right: 28px; top: 9px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .chip.pub { right: 28px; top: 28px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .field-btn { top: 7px; right: 6px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > h3 { font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.35; }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > h3::before { width: 76px; height: 20px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-form="concrete"] > h3::before,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-form="complete"] > h3::before { height: 40px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-kind="thesis"] > h3::before {
    width: 0; height: 0;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .gloss {
    max-height: 4em; opacity: 1; margin-top: 4px; pointer-events: auto;
    font-size: 11.5px; line-height: 1.4;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item:not(:has(.gloss)) > .say {
    display: -webkit-box; -webkit-box-orient: vertical; -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
    overflow: hidden; margin-top: 4px; font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.45;
    max-height: 3.5em; opacity: 1;
  }
  @supports (interpolate-size: allow-keywords) {
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .claim,
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .beats,
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .addlink,
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .meta,
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item:has(.gloss) > .say {
      height: 0; max-height: none;
    }
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .gloss { height: 0; max-height: none; }
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item > .gloss { height: auto; }
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item:not(:has(.gloss)) > .say { height: auto; }
  }

  /* the grid arrangements: form, when, term. Lanes are real editable
     elements pinned to row 1; the items place themselves into a column by
     attribute and pack upward from row 2. */
  .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool,
  .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool {
    display: grid; gap: 10px; align-items: start;
    grid-auto-flow: row dense;
  }
  /* The caps are the cards': four lanes stop growing at ~360px a card, six at
     ~300px, which is as wide as a title and a gloss want to be. */
  .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); max-width: 1500px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); max-width: 1500px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool { grid-template-columns: repeat(6, minmax(0, 1fr)); max-width: 1860px; }

  .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool > [data-form="rough"]    { grid-column: 1; }
  .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool > [data-form="abstract"] { grid-column: 2; }
  .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool > [data-form="concrete"] { grid-column: 3; }
  .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool > [data-form="complete"] { grid-column: 4; }

  .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool > [data-when="now"]   { grid-column: 1; }
  .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool > [data-when="next"]  { grid-column: 2; }
  .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool > [data-when="later"] { grid-column: 3; }
  .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool > .item:not([data-when]) { grid-column: 4; }

  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > [data-term="f26"]    { grid-column: 1; }
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > [data-term="w27"]    { grid-column: 2; }
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > [data-term="sp27"]   { grid-column: 3; }
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > [data-term="su27"]   { grid-column: 4; }
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > [data-term="f27"]    { grid-column: 5; }
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > [data-term="beyond"] { grid-column: 6; }
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item:not([data-term]) { grid-column: 6; }

  /* map — the item's own coordinates, which every other arrangement ignores
     for free because a static box does not read left or top */
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool {
    display: block; min-height: 1199px; overflow: auto;
    border: 1px dashed var(--hair); border-radius: var(--r);
    background:
      linear-gradient(var(--hair) 1px, transparent 1px) 0 0 / 100% 28px,
      linear-gradient(90deg, var(--hair) 1px, transparent 1px) 0 0 / 28px 100%;
    background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 60%, var(--card));
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item {
    position: absolute; max-width: 216px; padding: 9px 11px;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, .05);
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool[data-marble-canvas="off"] > .item { cursor: text; }
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool:not([data-marble-canvas="off"]) > .item { cursor: grab; }

  /* ── lanes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .lane { display: none; grid-row: 1; padding: 0 0 2px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool > .lane[data-lane^="form:"],
  .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool > .lane[data-lane^="when:"],
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .lane[data-lane^="term:"] { display: block; }
  .lane[data-lane="form:rough"],    .lane[data-lane="when:now"],   .lane[data-lane="term:f26"]  { grid-column: 1; }
  .lane[data-lane="form:abstract"], .lane[data-lane="when:next"],  .lane[data-lane="term:w27"]  { grid-column: 2; }
  .lane[data-lane="form:concrete"], .lane[data-lane="when:later"], .lane[data-lane="term:sp27"] { grid-column: 3; }
  .lane[data-lane="form:complete"], .lane[data-lane="when:none"],  .lane[data-lane="term:su27"] { grid-column: 4; }
  .lane[data-lane="term:f27"]    { grid-column: 5; }
  .lane[data-lane="term:beyond"] { grid-column: 6; }
  .lane > h4 {
    font: 600 10px/1 var(--sans); letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--faint); margin: 0 0 4px; padding-bottom: 7px; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--hair);
  }
  .lane > .lane-why { font-size: 11px; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--faint); margin: 5px 0 0; }
  /* A lane lights up while a card is over its column. The pointer may be
     anywhere in the column, not just on the header, so the script measures
     the header's horizontal span and lights the one the pointer is inside. */
  .lane.marble-target > h4 { color: var(--accent); border-bottom-color: var(--accent); }
  /* In the spine there are no lane elements: the thesis card is its own
     column's heading, so it is the thing that lights. */
  .item[data-kind="thesis"].marble-target {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 35%, transparent);
  }

  /* ── what a drag looks like ──────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* A column is CSS placement rather than a box, so there is nothing on the
     page to light up and nothing to slide aside. The script draws both: a
     wash over the column the card would land in, and a line at the place it
     would take. Both are page-only, positioned in script and thrown away on
     drop, so nothing about a drag in progress is ever written to the file. */
  .dropzone, .dropline { position: absolute; pointer-events: none; z-index: 4; }
  .dropzone {
    border-radius: var(--r);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 6%, transparent);
    outline: 1px dashed color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 38%, transparent);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }
  .dropline {
    height: 2px; border-radius: 2px; background: var(--accent); z-index: 6;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 16%, transparent);
  }
  /* The card being carried stays legible — the carrier's own .4 opacity on
     top of a wash reads as a rendering fault rather than a held thing. */
  .item.marble-dragging { opacity: .5; outline: 1px solid var(--accent); }
  body.carrying { cursor: grabbing; }

  /* venue deadlines, on the same axis as the work. Core venues read at full
     contrast; the field you are watching rather than aiming at stays faded. */
  .venues { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 3px 5px; margin: 6px 0 0; }
  .venue {
    font: 600 9px/1 var(--sans); letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 3px 6px; border-radius: 999px; border: 1px solid var(--hair); color: var(--faint);
  }
  .venue[data-tier="core"] {
    color: var(--m-warm); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--m-warm) 45%, transparent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--m-warm) 9%, transparent);
  }
  .venue[data-tier="background"] { opacity: .55; font-weight: 500; }

  /* ── the item ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .item {
    position: relative; padding: 14px 15px; border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r); background: var(--card);
  }
  /* A card carries its map coordinates permanently, in the same inline style
     the canvas affordance writes. The card is also relatively positioned, so
     the chips have something to hang off — which means every arrangement that
     is not the map has to say out loud that it is not listening, or a card
     gets shunted by a left and top it never asked for. An inline style
     outranks any selector, so this is the one place the override has to
     shout. */
  .stage:not([data-arrange="map"]):not([data-arrange="thesis"]) .pool > .item,
  .band > .item { left: auto !important; top: auto !important; }
  /* ── taking hold of a card ───────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* The carrier hangs its drag handle a rem and a half clear of the item's
     left edge. That is right for a document that is one column of rows, and
     wrong for a board: across a twelve pixel gutter, the handle that moves
     this card is drawn on top of the card beside it, so the thing you reach
     for belongs to something else. The handle comes inside instead and
     becomes a rail down the card's own left edge — the full height of the
     card, always faintly there, so moving a card is a gesture you can make
     without aiming and without discovering it first. */
  .item, .band .item { padding-left: 26px; }
  .item > .marble-grip {
    left: 0; top: 0; bottom: 0; width: 18px;
    align-items: center; gap: 0; opacity: 1;
    border-radius: var(--r) 0 0 var(--r);
    border-right: 1px solid transparent;
    transition: background .12s ease, border-color .12s ease;
  }
  .item > .marble-grip:hover {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 8%, transparent);
    border-right-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 30%, transparent);
  }
  .item > .marble-grip > .marble-handle {
    width: 18px; height: 24px; line-height: 24px; border-radius: 0; opacity: .28;
  }
  .item:hover > .marble-grip > .marble-handle { opacity: .55; }
  .item > .marble-grip > .marble-handle:hover,
  .item > .marble-grip > .marble-handle:focus-visible { opacity: 1; background: none; }
  /* Throwing a card away is not a thing to do by accident, so that button
     keeps the carrier's manners and waits for the pointer. */
  .item > .marble-grip > .marble-remove { width: 18px; opacity: 0; }
  .item:hover > .marble-grip > .marble-remove { opacity: .4; }
  .item > .marble-grip > .marble-remove:hover { opacity: 1; }
  /* On the map a card is dragged by its face, so a rail would be a second
     way to do the same thing, in a view with the least room for it. */
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item { padding-left: 11px; }
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item > .marble-grip { display: none; }

  .item > h3 { font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.32; padding-right: 0; }
  /* The chips sit in the card's top corner, so a title has to leave room for
     them — but only on the lines they actually occupy. A floated spacer does
     that: the first line or two wrap short and every line below runs the full
     width of the card, which the fixed indent this replaces never gave back.
     It is sized to the widest label a chip can hold, which is "submitted". */
  .item > h3::before { content: ""; float: right; width: 86px; height: 22px; }
  .item[data-form="concrete"] > h3::before,
  .item[data-form="complete"] > h3::before { height: 46px; }
  .item > .say { color: var(--ink-2); margin: 5px 0 0; font-size: 12.5px; line-height: 1.5; }
  .item[data-kind="thesis"] {
    border-color: var(--accent); background: var(--accent-soft);
  }
  .item[data-kind="thesis"] > h3 { font-size: 15px; color: var(--accent-ink); padding-right: 28px; }
  .item[data-kind="target"] { border-style: dashed; }
  /* The one-line reading of a card. The full pitch is too much for a map node
     and a bare title is too little, so the short gloss is what those views
     show — the same sentence, not a second copy of it. */
  .gloss { display: none; font-size: 11.5px; line-height: 1.4; color: var(--faint); margin-top: 3px; }
  .band .item > .gloss,
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item > .gloss,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item > .gloss,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item > .gloss { display: block; }
  /* Those four are where a gloss is worth reading by default. A card that
     names it in data-parts has been asked for it, which is the one answer no
     view gets to talk over — the same rule the questions and the claim get
     further down. */
  .stage .pool > .item[data-parts~="gloss"] > .gloss { display: block; }
  /* A link is an annotation about two other cards rather than a card of its
     own, so it has meaning only where the two sit next to each other. */
  .item[data-kind="link"] { display: none; }
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item[data-kind="link"] {
    display: block; border: 0; background: none; box-shadow: none; padding: 4px 0; max-width: 158px;
  }
  .item[data-kind="link"] > h3 {
    font: italic 400 12px/1.45 var(--serif); color: var(--faint); padding-right: 0;
  }

  /* An idea that serves two theses is in one column and says so here, derived
     from the same attribute the column came from — never written twice. It is
     an explanation of where the card was put, so it is only worth saying in
     the arrangement that put it there. */
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-thesis~="t1"][data-thesis~="t2"] > h3::after { content: " · also T1"; }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-thesis~="t1"][data-thesis~="t3"] > h3::after { content: " · also T1"; }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-thesis~="t2"][data-thesis~="t3"] > h3::after { content: " · also T2"; }
  .item > h3::after { font: 500 9.5px/1 var(--sans); letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--faint); vertical-align: middle; }

  .meta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 3px 6px; align-items: baseline; font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--ink-2); margin-top: 9px; }
  .meta .k { color: var(--faint); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .07em; font-size: 10px; }
  .meta .k:not(:first-child) { margin-left: 8px; }

  .claim { position: relative; margin: 10px 0 0; padding: 9px 11px 9px 12px; border-left: 2px solid var(--accent); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-soft) 60%, transparent); border-radius: 0 var(--r-2) var(--r-2) 0; }
  .claim > h4 { font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.35; padding-right: 3.6rem; }
  .claim > p { font-size: 11.5px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink-2); margin-top: 4px; }
  .claim > .chip { position: absolute; right: 9px; top: 8px; }

  .beats { list-style: none; margin: 10px 0 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 3px; }
  .beat { position: relative; padding: 2px 3.4rem 2px 14px; }
  .beat > p { font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.5; }
  .beat::before { content: ""; position: absolute; left: 3px; top: 10px; width: 4px; height: 4px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--faint); }
  /* A question's own handle tucks into the indent its bullet was using,
     rather than reaching out past the card and landing on the card's rail. */
  .beat > .marble-grip { left: -2px; top: -1px; width: 13px; align-items: center; }
  .beat:hover::before, .beat:focus-within::before { opacity: 0; }
  .beat > .marble-grip > .marble-btn { width: 13px; height: 14px; line-height: 14px; font-size: 9px; }
  .beat > .chip { position: absolute; right: 0; top: 2px; }
  /* A heat chip on every question would be noise on thirty-five of them, so an
     unset one waits for the pointer — and for the keyboard, which is why the
     focus-within twin is not optional. */
  .beat:not([data-mark]) > .chip { opacity: 0; transition: opacity .14s; }
  .beat:not([data-mark]):hover > .chip,
  .beat:not([data-mark]):focus-within > .chip { opacity: 1; }
  @media (hover: none) { .beat:not([data-mark]) > .chip { opacity: .6; } }

  .addlink { font: inherit; font-size: 12px; color: var(--faint); background: none; border: 0; border-radius: var(--r-2); padding: 4px 8px; cursor: pointer; margin-top: 6px; }
  .addlink:hover { color: var(--accent); background: var(--accent-soft); }

  /* ── chips ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .chip {
    font: 600 9px/1 var(--sans); letter-spacing: .09em; text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 4px 7px; border-radius: 999px; border: 1px solid var(--hair);
    color: var(--muted); background: var(--card); cursor: pointer; user-select: none; white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .item > .chip.form { position: absolute; right: 30px; top: 12px; }
  .item > .chip.pub { position: absolute; right: 30px; top: 36px; }
  /* A dial is shown when the card has the thing it dials. A rough idea with
     no submission state simply has no data-mark, and gets the status field
     the moment it is asked for one — which used to be decided a second time
     here, by form, so a card could be told the field was on and then not show
     it the moment you closed the picker. */
  .item:not([data-mark]) > .chip.pub,
  .item:not([data-form]) > .chip.form { display: none; }
  /* A target is a date, not an idea, so it has no form to run through — but it
     does have a plan, and that chip is the only one it wears. */
  .item[data-kind="target"] > .chip.pub { top: 12px; }
  .item[data-kind="target"] > .chip.form { display: none; }
  /* A thesis is a claim, not a paper: there is no form for it to run through
     and nothing to submit, so it wears neither dial. Which parts each kind of
     card is offered is decided once, in KIND_PARTS; these rules are that
     answer spelled where the host is not running. */
  .item[data-kind="thesis"] > .chip.form,
  .item[data-kind="thesis"] > .chip.pub { display: none; }

  [data-form="rough"] > .chip.form { color: var(--faint); }
  [data-form="abstract"] > .chip.form {
    color: var(--m-warm); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--m-warm) 40%, transparent);
  }
  [data-form="concrete"] > .chip.form {
    color: var(--m-live); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--m-live) 45%, transparent);
    background: var(--accent-soft);
  }
  [data-form="complete"] > .chip.form { color: #fff; background: var(--m-live); border-color: var(--m-live); }

  [data-mark="seed"] > .chip, [data-mark="someday"] > .chip,
  [data-mark="cold"] > .chip, [data-mark="hunch"] > .chip { color: var(--faint); }
  [data-mark="planned"] > .chip, [data-mark="warm"] > .chip, [data-mark="forming"] > .chip {
    color: var(--m-warm); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--m-warm) 40%, transparent);
  }
  [data-mark="active"] > .chip, [data-mark="drafting"] > .chip,
  [data-mark="hot"] > .chip, [data-mark="firm"] > .chip {
    color: var(--m-live); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--m-live) 45%, transparent);
    background: var(--accent-soft);
  }
  [data-mark="submitted"] > .chip, [data-mark="done"] > .chip {
    color: #fff; background: var(--m-live); border-color: var(--m-live);
  }

  /* ── on-demand fields: the card itself is the picker ─────────────────── */
  .field-btn {
    position: absolute; right: 4px; top: 4px; z-index: 8;
    width: 20px; height: 20px; padding: 0;
    display: grid; place-items: center;
    border: 1px solid var(--hair); border-radius: 5px;
    background: var(--card); color: var(--faint); cursor: pointer;
    opacity: .4; transition: opacity .14s, border-color .14s, color .14s, background .14s;
  }
  .field-btn svg { width: 11px; height: 11px; display: block; }
  .item:hover > .field-btn,
  .item:focus-within > .field-btn,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .field-btn { opacity: 1; }
  .field-btn:hover, .field-btn:focus-visible {
    border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-ink); background: var(--accent-soft); outline: none;
  }
  .item[data-fields-open] > .field-btn {
    border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-ink); background: var(--accent-soft);
  }
  @media (hover: none) { .item > .field-btn { opacity: 1; } }

  .item[data-fields-open] {
    z-index: 7;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 38%, transparent);
  }
  .item[data-fields-open] [data-marble-editable] { caret-color: transparent; }
  .item[data-fields-open] > .chip,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .gloss,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .say,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .claim,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .meta,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .beats,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost { cursor: pointer; }

  .part-ghost {
    display: block; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;
    margin: 0; padding: 0; border: 0; border-radius: 5px;
    background: none; color: inherit; font: inherit; text-align: left;
  }
  .part-ghost.chip { width: auto; }
  .part-ghost.claim .part-ghost-h { display: block; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.35; }
  .part-ghost.claim .part-ghost-p { display: block; font-size: 11.5px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink-2); margin-top: 4px; }
  .part-ghost.beats { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 3px; }
  .part-ghost-beat {
    display: block; position: relative; padding: 2px 0 2px 14px;
    font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .part-ghost-beat::before {
    content: ""; position: absolute; left: 3px; top: 10px; width: 4px; height: 4px;
    border-radius: 50%; background: var(--faint);
  }

  /* A part that is off is not shown. This has to outrank the arrangement
     rules above — a gloss switched off was still appearing on the spine,
     because "the spine shows glosses" was written one selector deeper than
     "this card has no gloss" — so it is spelled at the same depth they are.
     Inside the picker it stands down: there every slot shows, the off ones
     dashed, which is the whole point of opening it. */
  .stage .pool > .item[data-parts]:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="form"]) > .chip.form,
  .stage .pool > .item[data-parts]:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="state"]) > .chip.pub,
  .stage .pool > .item[data-parts]:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="gloss"]) > .gloss,
  .stage .pool > .item[data-parts]:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="say"]) > .say,
  .stage .pool > .item[data-parts]:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="claim"]) > .claim,
  .stage .pool > .item[data-parts]:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="meta"]) > .meta,
  .stage .pool > .item[data-parts]:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="beats"]) > .beats,
  .stage .pool > .item[data-parts]:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="beats"]) > .addlink { display: none; }

  /* Compose mode: every slot sits in its real place. On = the live field.
     Off = the same block, dashed and transparent. */
  .item[data-fields-open] > .chip.form,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .chip.pub,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .gloss,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .say,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .claim,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .meta,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .beats,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost {
    display: block;
    height: auto; max-height: none; overflow: visible;
    pointer-events: auto; -webkit-line-clamp: unset;
  }
  .item[data-fields-open] > .meta,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost.meta { display: flex; }
  .item[data-kind="target"][data-fields-open] > .chip.form,
  .item[data-kind="target"][data-fields-open] > .part-ghost[data-part="form"],
  .item[data-kind="thesis"][data-fields-open] > .chip.form,
  .item[data-kind="thesis"][data-fields-open] > .chip.pub,
  .item[data-kind="thesis"][data-fields-open] > .part-ghost[data-part="form"],
  .item[data-kind="thesis"][data-fields-open] > .part-ghost[data-part="state"] { display: none; }

  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .gloss,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .say,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .claim,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .beats,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .meta,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .gloss,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .say,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .claim,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .beats,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .meta,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost {
    display: block; height: auto; max-height: none;
    margin-top: 6px; padding: revert; pointer-events: auto;
    overflow: visible; border-width: revert; -webkit-line-clamp: unset;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .chip,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .chip {
    margin-top: 0; padding: 3px 6px;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .meta,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .meta,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost.meta,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost.meta {
    display: flex;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .gloss:not(.marble-part-off):not(.part-ghost),
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .say:not(.marble-part-off):not(.part-ghost),
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .claim:not(.marble-part-off):not(.part-ghost),
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .beats:not(.marble-part-off):not(.part-ghost),
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .meta:not(.marble-part-off):not(.part-ghost),
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open][data-parts~="gloss"] > .gloss,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open][data-parts~="say"] > .say,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open][data-parts~="claim"] > .claim,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open][data-parts~="meta"] > .meta,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open][data-parts~="beats"] > .beats {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .item[data-fields-open] > .marble-part-off.chip,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost.chip {
    background: transparent; color: var(--faint); border-style: dashed; opacity: .55;
  }
  .item[data-fields-open] > .marble-part-off:not(.chip),
  .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost:not(.chip),
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .marble-part-off:not(.chip),
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost:not(.chip),
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .marble-part-off:not(.chip),
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"][data-density="compact"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost:not(.chip) {
    opacity: .42; background: transparent;
    outline: 1px dashed var(--hair); outline-offset: 0;
  }
  .item[data-fields-open] > .marble-part-off.claim,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost.claim {
    border-left-color: var(--hair);
  }
  .item[data-fields-open] > .gloss:hover,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .say:hover,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .claim:hover,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .meta:hover,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .beats:hover,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost:hover,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .chip:hover {
    outline-color: var(--accent);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 55%, var(--hair));
  }
  .item[data-fields-open] > .part-ghost:focus-visible,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .chip:focus-visible,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .gloss:focus-visible,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .say:focus-visible,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .claim:focus-visible,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .meta:focus-visible,
  .item[data-fields-open] > .beats:focus-visible {
    outline: 1px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* ── density: what is worth reading in each arrangement ──────────────── */
  /* The compact views want a name and nothing else. Hiding is a rendering
     decision, so it is spelled here and never by deleting anything.

     Every rule in this section stands down for a card whose picker is open.
     Which fields a card can wear is a fact about the card; standing a field
     down to save room is a reading of the view. A picker that offered three
     of seven fields here and all seven one view over would be answering the
     second question when it was asked the first.

     These two are the one place a view still outranks what a card says it
     shows, and it is not density: on the map and the timeline a card is a
     node the size of its name, placed against its neighbours. A pitch and
     five questions inside one would not be a fuller card, it would be a card
     lying across three others. The card keeps every field; this is the view
     where none of them is read. */
  .band .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .say, .band .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .claim, .band .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .meta,
  .band .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .beats, .band .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .addlink,
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .say,
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .claim,
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .meta,
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .beats,
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .addlink,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .say,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .claim,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .meta,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .beats,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .addlink { display: none; }

  /* A map node is a label, not a paragraph. A thesis states itself in a full
     sentence, which is right on the spine and far too much at this size, so
     the compact views show the head of it and the gloss underneath carries
     the meaning. The whole sentence is one view away. */
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item > h3,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item > h3 {
    font-size: 12px; padding-right: 0;
    display: -webkit-box; -webkit-box-orient: vertical; -webkit-line-clamp: 3; overflow: hidden;
  }
  /* A card this narrow has no corner to spare: a word like "submitted" is
     wider than the gutter a floating chip could sit in, so here the chip stops
     floating and takes a line of its own under the gloss. One chip is all that
     fits and all that is wanted — for an idea that is how formed it is, for a
     target it is the plan. The other is one view away. */
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item > h3,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item > h3 { order: 1; }
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item > .gloss,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item > .gloss { order: 2; }
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item > .chip,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item > .chip {
    order: 3; position: static; margin: 8px 0 0;
  }
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item[data-kind="idea"]:not([data-fields-open]) > .chip.pub,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item[data-kind="idea"]:not([data-fields-open]) > .chip.pub { display: none; }
  /* With the chip out of the corner there is nothing to leave room for, and
     the spacer that did so would read as a hole bitten out of the heading. */
  .item[data-kind="link"] > h3::before,
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item > h3::before,
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item > h3::before { display: none; }

  /* The spine is the argument — a thesis and what would carry it. The
     questions hanging off each idea are a reading of their own and have a view
     of their own, so here they stand down; "list" is the arrangement that
     still shows everything at once.

     Unless the card says otherwise. A view standing a part down is a default
     for a card nobody has composed; `data-parts` is somebody having sat with
     that card and said what it shows. Switching Questions on and watching them
     vanish the moment the picker closed is the default quietly overruling the
     decision, so from here down a part the card names outranks the view.
     (The map and the timeline below are the exception, and say why.) */
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool[data-show="all"] > .item:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="beats"]) > .beats,
  .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool[data-show="all"] > .item:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="beats"]) > .addlink { display: none; }

  /* the board and the agenda want the pitch but not the whole file */
  .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="claim"]) > .claim,
  .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="beats"]) > .beats,
  .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="beats"]) > .addlink,
  .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="claim"]) > .claim,
  .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="beats"]) > .beats,
  .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool > .item:not([data-fields-open]):not([data-parts~="beats"]) > .addlink { display: none; }

  /* ── who belongs in which arrangement ────────────────────────────────── */
  /* Form, when and term are questions you can ask of an idea. A thesis has no
     answer to any of them, so it stands out of those three rather than being
     filed under a column that means nothing to it; a target has a date and a
     plan but no form, so it sits out of the board alone. These come after the
     layout rules on purpose — not being here at all has to beat any rule
     about how a card would have been laid out if it were. */
  .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool > .item[data-kind="thesis"],
  .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool > .item[data-kind="thesis"],
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item[data-kind="thesis"],
  .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool > .item[data-kind="target"] { display: none; }
  /* A connector says something about two cards being near each other, which is
     only true on the map. */
  .stage:not([data-arrange="map"]) .pool > .item[data-kind="link"] { display: none; }

  /* On the map and the board a card is a label whose title and gloss are
     ordered by hand, so the slots the picker unfolds take their place after
     them rather than landing above the name of the thing. */
  .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > :where(.say, .claim, .meta, .beats, .addlink, .part-ghost),
  .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .item[data-fields-open] > :where(.say, .claim, .meta, .beats, .addlink, .part-ghost) { order: 4; }

  /* ── the show dial ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .pool[data-show="claims"] .item:not(:has(.claim)) { display: none; }
  .pool[data-show="claims"] .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .say,
  .pool[data-show="claims"] .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .meta,
  .pool[data-show="claims"] .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .beats,
  .pool[data-show="claims"] .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .addlink { display: none; }
  .pool[data-show="claims"] .item > h3 { font-size: 12px; color: var(--faint); font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: .04em; }
  .pool[data-show="claims"] .item > .claim { margin-top: 7px; }

  .pool[data-show="questions"] .item:not(:has(.beat)) { display: none; }
  .pool[data-show="questions"] .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .say,
  .pool[data-show="questions"] .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .claim,
  .pool[data-show="questions"] .item:not([data-fields-open]) > .meta { display: none; }
  .pool[data-show="questions"] .item > h3 { font-size: 12px; color: var(--faint); font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: .04em; }
  .pool[data-show="questions"] .item > .beats { margin-top: 7px; }

  .pool[data-show="targets"] .item:not([data-kind="target"]) { display: none; }

  /* The band is a pointer list, not a second copy of the cards, so the
     claims and questions readings do not unpack the real fields here. */

  /* ── the framings, kept whole ────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .framings { display: none; }
  .stage[data-arrange="framings"] .framings { display: block; }
  .stage[data-arrange="framings"] .pool,
  .stage[data-arrange="framings"] .bar,
  .stage[data-arrange="framings"] .vhead,
  .stage[data-arrange="framings"] .band-wrap { display: none; }
  .stage[data-arrange="framings"] .framings .vhead { display: block; }

  /* Narrowing these three cannot mean fewer tracks. A card is placed in a
     lane by its own attribute, so a sixth term asked to live in a three
     column grid takes an implicit track outside the template and leaves the
     first three at zero width with their cards still in them. The lane is
     the whole reading here, so the board keeps every one of them at a width
     a card can be read at, and scrolls sideways instead. */
  @container doc (max-width: 1100px) {
    .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(6, 236px);
      max-width: none; overflow-x: auto; overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
      padding-bottom: 6px;
    }
  }
  @container doc (max-width: 900px) {
    body { flex-direction: column; }
    .rail { width: auto; min-width: 0; height: auto; position: static; border-right: 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hair); }
    /* the grip hangs 1.5rem into the left margin; without the gutter it hangs
       off the page instead and the whole document scrolls sideways */
    .stage-in, .band-wrap { padding-left: 30px; padding-right: 20px; }
    .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool,
    .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 248px);
      max-width: none; overflow-x: auto; overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
      padding-bottom: 6px;
    }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    * { transition: none !important; }
  }
</style>
<style data-marble-id="viz-style">
  /* 01½ · vision, visualized — one claim, ten framings.
     The marble-alt below holds the ten diagrams as siblings and its switch shows
     one. Every diagram speaks through the shared classes here, so each fill is
     read from the theme and never written into the file a second time. */
  .viz-defs { position: absolute; width: 0; height: 0; }

  marble-alt.vizgallery { display: block; margin: 6px 0 0; }
  marble-alt.vizgallery > .viz { display: none; }
  marble-alt.vizgallery[data-marble-active="v1"]  > [data-marble-alt="v1"],
  marble-alt.vizgallery[data-marble-active="v2"]  > [data-marble-alt="v2"],
  marble-alt.vizgallery[data-marble-active="v3"]  > [data-marble-alt="v3"],
  marble-alt.vizgallery[data-marble-active="v4"]  > [data-marble-alt="v4"],
  marble-alt.vizgallery[data-marble-active="v5"]  > [data-marble-alt="v5"],
  marble-alt.vizgallery[data-marble-active="v6"]  > [data-marble-alt="v6"],
  marble-alt.vizgallery[data-marble-active="v7"]  > [data-marble-alt="v7"],
  marble-alt.vizgallery[data-marble-active="v8"]  > [data-marble-alt="v8"],
  marble-alt.vizgallery[data-marble-active="v9"]  > [data-marble-alt="v9"],
  marble-alt.vizgallery[data-marble-active="v10"] > [data-marble-alt="v10"] { display: block; }
  marble-alt.vizgallery:not([data-marble-active]) > .viz:first-child { display: block; }
  .stage[data-view="gallery"] .view[data-view~="gallery"] { display: block; }

  .viz { margin: 0; }
  .viz > h4 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0; display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: .55em; line-height: 1.3; }
  .viz > h4 .n { font: 500 10px/1 var(--sans); color: var(--faint); }
  .viz-svg {
    display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 12px 0 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--hair); border-radius: var(--r);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 55%, var(--card));
  }
  .viz > figcaption { color: var(--ink-2); font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.55; margin: 12px 0 0; max-width: 52em; }

  /* the shared diagram vocabulary — colour follows the theme */
  .viz-svg text { font-family: var(--sans); fill: var(--ink); }
  .viz-svg .d-serif { font-family: var(--serif); }
  .viz-svg .d-mut { fill: var(--muted); }
  .viz-svg .d-faint { fill: var(--faint); }
  .viz-svg .d-acc { fill: var(--accent); }
  .viz-svg .d-kick { fill: var(--faint); letter-spacing: .14em; }
  .viz-svg .s-box { fill: var(--card); stroke: var(--line); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .viz-svg .s-acc { fill: var(--accent-soft); stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .viz-svg .s-line { fill: none; stroke: var(--line); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .viz-svg .s-accline { fill: none; stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .viz-svg .s-dash { fill: none; stroke: var(--faint); stroke-width: 1.5; stroke-dasharray: 4 5; }
  .viz-svg .d-lens { fill: var(--accent); fill-opacity: .10; stroke: var(--line); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .viz-svg .d-lens.is-phd { fill-opacity: .2; stroke: var(--accent); }
  .viz-svg .d-fill { fill: var(--faint); fill-opacity: .11; stroke: none; }
  .viz-svg .d-fill-acc { fill: var(--accent); fill-opacity: .12; stroke: none; }
  .viz-defs .mk { fill: var(--faint); }
  .viz-defs .mk-acc { fill: var(--accent); }
</style>
<style data-marble-id="phone-style">
  /* The document on a phone.

     Everything above this block describes a wide two-pane workspace: a rail
     that is always there, a band pinned across the top, a pool of three to six
     columns side by side. A phone has room for exactly one of those things at
     a time, so none of it is deleted here — it is re-laid-out, at one
     breakpoint, over the same elements.

     The breakpoint is asked of the container, not the window, for the same
     reason the rest of this file does it: a host that docks a panel beside the
     page shrinks <html> and leaves the viewport where it was, and a media
     query would go on insisting there is room that is no longer there.

     One rule underneath all of it: on a phone, content is the page and chrome
     is summoned. The eight views are this document's main verb, so they are
     the one piece of chrome that stays on screen — under the thumb, where
     tapping one visibly changes what you are reading. Everything else is a
     second-order dial and lives behind a disclosure. */

  /* ── shell ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* ── the phone reading of the rail ──────────────────────────────────────────
  
     On a wide screen the rail is a wall you stand beside: 232px of navigation
     next to the page, costing nothing because the page was never going to use
     that column anyway. On a phone the same markup stacks, and a wall you stand
     beside becomes a wall you stand behind — 850px of chrome on an 844px screen,
     so the document opens on one full page of controls and no ideas at all.
  
     Nothing here adds a control. Every rule below takes the rail apart and puts
     the same elements somewhere a thumb can reach them:
  
       the eight views  →  a bar pinned to the bottom of the screen
       everything else  →  a sheet that slides up over the page when asked for
  
     The one structural fact to hold on to: the bar is a *child* of the sheet, and
     it has to stay put while the sheet moves. That rules out `transform` on the
     rail — a transformed ancestor becomes the containing block for any fixed
     descendant, and the bar would ride down with the sheet it is supposed to
     outlive. So the rail is a fixed box anchored to the bottom edge whose height
     is what animates, and the bar is `position: sticky; bottom: 0` inside it.
     Closed, the box is exactly bar-high and the bar fills it. Open, the box grows
     upward and the bar stays welded to its bottom edge. One property animates and
     nothing has to escape anything.
  
     The question is asked of `doc` — the container on <html> — and never of the
     window, because a host that docks a panel beside this document shrinks <html>
     and leaves the viewport exactly where it was.                              */
  
  
  /* Page-owned chrome. These four elements are written by part.js and carry
     data-marble-transient, so they belong to the page and never to the file.
     They are declared invisible here and summoned inside the phone query, which
     is the only place they mean anything. */
  .pm-grab, .pm-scrim, .pm-head { display: none; }
  .rail .nav > .pm-opts { display: none; }
  
  
  @container doc (max-width: 720px) {
  
    /* 100vh on iOS is the viewport with the toolbars retracted — a height you
       cannot see while you are looking at it, and part of why the rail measured
       850px on an 844px screen. Every height below asks for the small viewport
       instead: svh never moves, where dvh reflows every time the toolbar hides
       and would make a bottom-anchored sheet twitch as you scroll. The bar itself
       asks for neither; `position: fixed; bottom: 0` plus the safe-area inset is
       handled by the visual viewport, which is more correct than either unit. */
    body {
      --pm-bar-h: 56px;
      --pm-safe-b: env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px);
      --pm-bar-total: calc(var(--pm-bar-h) + var(--pm-safe-b));
      /* the plain vh stays first, as the fallback for anything without svh */
      min-height: 100vh;
      min-height: 100svh;
    }
  
    /* ── 1 · the rail stops being a wall ───────────────────────────────────
       Out of flow entirely, so the first idea is the first thing on the page.
       Closed it is bar-high; open it is a sheet. `overflow: hidden` while closed
       means the sheet's contents are there but unreachable, which is what lets
       the sticky bar sit over them without a second element to hold it. */
    .rail {
      position: fixed; z-index: 50;
      left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0; top: auto;
      width: auto; min-width: 0; height: auto;
      max-height: var(--pm-bar-total);
      padding: 0;
      display: flex; flex-direction: column;
      overflow: hidden;
      overscroll-behavior: contain;
      background: var(--paper);
      /* No border on the rail itself: it is a border-box, so a 1px top border
         is 1px the sticky bar cannot occupy, and the bar ends up a hairline
         below the fold. The bar carries its own top edge; open, the sheet is a
         raised card on a dimmed page and does not need a line to say so. */
      border: 0;
      transition: max-height .32s cubic-bezier(.32, .72, 0, 1),
                  border-radius .32s cubic-bezier(.32, .72, 0, 1),
                  box-shadow .32s ease;
    }
    .rail.marble-pm-open {
      max-height: 86svh;
      overflow-y: auto;
      background: var(--card);
      border-radius: 16px 16px 0 0;
      /* --hair is the document's own word for an edge and it flips with the
         theme, so the sheet's lip reads in both. */
      box-shadow: 0 -1px 0 var(--hair),
                  0 -22px 44px -22px color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 26%, transparent);
    }
  
    /* DOM order puts the view list in the middle of the rail; on the phone it is
       the floor. `order` says so without moving a single node. */
    .rail > * { order: 1; flex: 0 0 auto; }
    .rail > .pm-grab { order: 0; }
    .rail > .nav { order: 2; }
  
    /* The sheet's contents are hidden from the keyboard and the screen reader
       while it is shut — clipped is not the same as absent. The zero-length
       visibility transition is delayed on the way out so the content does not
       blink away before the sheet has finished closing over it. */
    .rail > :not(.nav) {
      padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px;
      visibility: hidden;
      transition: visibility 0s linear .32s;
    }
    .rail.marble-pm-open > :not(.nav) { visibility: visible; transition-delay: 0s; }
  
    .rail > .kicker { padding-top: 4px; }
    .rail-title { font-size: 20px; margin: 6px 0 4px; }
    .rail-sub { font-size: 12.5px; margin-bottom: 4px; }
    .rail-versions { margin: 10px 0 0; }
    .rail-group { margin: 18px 0 9px; }
    /* the bar below is the view list; naming it twice would be furniture */
    .rail-group:has(+ .nav) { display: none; }
  
    /* The grab handle. It is the sheet's own edge made visible and, because a
       real button is cheaper than a gesture, it is also how you shut it. */
    .rail > .pm-grab {
      display: block; width: 100%;
      position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 2;
      padding: 14px 20px 12px;
      border: 0; background: var(--card); cursor: pointer;
    }
    .pm-grab::after {
      content: ""; display: block; margin: 0 auto;
      width: 38px; height: 4px; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--line);
    }
    /* 30px of handle, 44px of target. The expander grows downward into the
       kicker underneath rather than upward past the sheet's edge, where the
       rail's own overflow would clip it — a clipped hit area is not a hit area. */
    .pm-grab::before { content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 0 0 -14px; }
  
    /* ── 2 · the view list becomes the bar ─────────────────────────────────
       Eight views are this document's main verb, and on a phone the main verb
       belongs under the thumb. These are the file's own buttons, repositioned —
       not copies. The document derives `marble-on` from data-arrange for every
       [data-preset] it can find, so a second bar would be a second source of
       truth that never lit up. */
    .rail > .nav {
      position: sticky; bottom: 0; z-index: 3;
      visibility: visible;
      height: var(--pm-bar-total);
      /* No margin on the bar, ever. A bottom-sticky element may only be shifted
         until its *margin* box reaches the top of its containing block, so a
         16px margin here is 16px the bar cannot rise — and 16px of pills left
         hanging below the fold while the sheet is shut. The air above the bar is
         spent by the last thing in the sheet instead. */
      margin: 0;
      /* no left padding on the strip: a sticky child's `left: 0` is the
         scrollport's padding edge, so any padding here is a gap the pills show
         through beside the button that is supposed to be masking them. The
         toggle carries that inset itself. */
      /* The trailing inset is not decoration. A phone's lower-right corner is
         already spoken for — iOS floats its own controls there, and a host may
         float an agent button over the page — so the strip keeps enough room at
         the end that its last pill can never come to rest under one. It doubles
         as the peek that says the strip scrolls. */
      padding: 5px max(48px, calc(12px + env(safe-area-inset-right, 0px)))
               calc(5px + var(--pm-safe-b)) 0;
      flex-direction: row; align-items: stretch; gap: 6px;
      background: var(--paper);
      border-top: 1px solid var(--hair);
      overflow-x: auto; overflow-y: hidden;
      overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
      scroll-snap-type: x proximity;
      scrollbar-width: none;
    }
    .rail.marble-pm-open > .nav { background: var(--card); }
    .rail > .nav::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }
    .rail > .nav > li { flex: 0 0 auto; display: flex; scroll-snap-align: center; }
  
    .rail .nav button {
      position: relative;
      width: auto; text-align: center; align-items: center;
      padding: 12px 15px;
      border: 0; border-radius: 999px;
      font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.25; white-space: nowrap;
      color: var(--muted); background: none;
      transition: color .16s, background-color .16s;
    }
    /* The visual pill stays the size the typography wants; the *target* is
       grown past it by an expander nobody can see. Vertical only — every label
       is already wider than 44px, and a sideways expander would have adjacent
       pills fighting over the same pixels. */
    .rail .nav button::before { content: ""; position: absolute; inset: -2px 0; }
    .rail .nav button.marble-on {
      border-left: 0;
      color: var(--accent-ink); background: var(--accent-soft); font-weight: 600;
    }
    /* 01, 02, 03 … are ornament for a vertical list. On the bar they are eight
       labels' worth of width spent saying nothing. */
    .rail .nav .n { display: none; }
  
    /* The one new control: the rest of the rail, on request. It sits at the
       *left* end and stays there while the pills scroll under it, because the
       lower-right corner of a phone already belongs to the browser — iOS Safari
       floats its own controls there. */
    .rail .nav > .pm-opts {
      display: flex; position: sticky; left: 0; z-index: 2;
      background: inherit; margin-right: 1px;
      padding-left: max(8px, env(safe-area-inset-left, 0px)); padding-right: 7px;
      /* the pills pass underneath it, and should look like they are passing
         underneath it */
      box-shadow: 7px 0 7px -7px color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 34%, transparent);
    }
    .pm-opts button {
      width: 44px; min-width: 44px; padding: 0;
      display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
      border: 1px solid var(--hair); border-radius: 999px;
      background: none; color: var(--muted);
      font: 600 17px/1 var(--sans); cursor: pointer;
      transition: color .16s, background-color .16s, border-color .16s;
    }
    .pm-opts button[aria-expanded="true"] {
      color: var(--accent-ink); background: var(--accent-soft); border-color: var(--accent-soft);
    }
  
    /* ── 3 · the dials, inside the sheet ───────────────────────────────────
       Same expander trick, plus enough gap that the invisible halves of two
       neighbouring targets never overlap. */
    .rail .dial { gap: 9px 8px; }
    .rail .dial button {
      position: relative; font-size: 12px; padding: 8px 13px;
    }
    .rail .dial button::before { content: ""; position: absolute; inset: -5px -2px; }
  
    /* ── 9 · the note is written for a mouse ───────────────────────────────
       Grips, drag, drop, Shift-drop, arrow keys, Mod+Z: 240 words about gestures
       a phone does not have, currently the first thing a phone reader meets. It
       stays in the file — desktop needs every word — but here it is the last
       thing in the sheet, and under a touch pointer it is clamped to its first
       line and faded out. Touching it opens it, which on a live host is the same
       gesture that lets you edit it. */
    .rail-note {
      font-size: 11px; margin-top: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px; padding-top: 12px;
    }
    @media (hover: none) {
      .rail-note {
        max-height: 3.2em; overflow: hidden; cursor: pointer;
        -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, currentColor 38%, transparent);
                mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, currentColor 38%, transparent);
        transition: max-height .3s ease;
      }
      .rail-note:focus-within, .rail-note:active {
        max-height: 60em;
        -webkit-mask-image: none; mask-image: none;
      }
    }
  
    /* The scrim. It is what "tap outside to dismiss" is made of, and
       `touch-action: none` is what stops a drag on it from scrolling the page
       behind the open sheet — a job usually done by writing to body.style, which
       here would mean writing an inline style onto an element of the file. */
    .pm-scrim {
      display: block; position: fixed; inset: 0; z-index: 45;
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 34%, transparent);
      opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; touch-action: none;
      transition: opacity .32s ease;
    }
    /* A scrim made of --ink is a scrim that inverts: in dark mode --ink is the
       near-white the text is set in, so a wash of it *lights* the page instead
       of pushing it back. Brightness asks the right question in both themes —
       dim what is behind, whatever colour it happens to be — and needs no colour
       of its own. The ink wash above stays as the answer for anything that
       cannot do it, where light mode is at least right. */
    @supports ((backdrop-filter: brightness(1)) or (-webkit-backdrop-filter: brightness(1))) {
      .pm-scrim {
        background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 7%, transparent);
        -webkit-backdrop-filter: brightness(.6) saturate(.9);
                backdrop-filter: brightness(.6) saturate(.9);
      }
    }
    .pm-scrim.marble-pm-on { opacity: 1; pointer-events: auto; }
  
    /* ── 4 · a slim page header ────────────────────────────────────────────
       The document's title went into the sheet with the rest of the rail, and a
       document you cannot name is a document you are lost inside. This row hands
       it back, together with the name of the view the bar is lit on — so the
       bar's state is legible as a word and not only as a highlight. It scrolls
       away with everything else; only the bar is allowed to be permanent. */
    .stage > .pm-head {
      display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 12px;
      padding: 13px 20px 11px;
      background: var(--paper);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hair);
    }
    .pm-head-doc {
      font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: -.012em; color: var(--ink);
      white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
    }
    .pm-head-view {
      margin-left: auto; flex: 0 0 auto;
      font: 600 10px/1 var(--sans); letter-spacing: .15em; text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--accent);
    }
  
    /* ── 6 · the content gutter ────────────────────────────────────────────
       34px, then 30/20 at the tablet step — the asymmetry reserves a left margin
       for a drag grip that has no meaning under a finger. Even gutters here, and
       bottom room for the bar plus the browser's own floating toolbar, which on
       iOS lies over the last thing on the page rather than beside it. */
    .stage { padding-bottom: calc(var(--pm-bar-total) + 96px); }
    .stage-in { padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px; }
  
    .vhead { margin: 18px 0 14px; }
    .vhead h2 { font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.22; }
    .vhead .sub { font-size: 13px; margin-top: 7px; }
    marble-alt.northstar { margin-bottom: 20px; }
    marble-alt.northstar > [data-marble-alt] { font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.4; }
  }

  /* ── band ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     1 · The form chip stops floating over the title — at every width
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  
     This one section is deliberately unscoped. The chip overlapping the title is
     not a phone bug that a narrow screen exposes; it is an arithmetic mistake
     that has always been in the band. The chip is positioned from the card's
     right edge and the title reserves a flat 3.2rem for it — but the chip is
     64px wide and sits 27px in, so it occupies 68px of the title's line and the
     title only steps aside for 51px of that. Any title long enough to reach the
     end of its first line runs underneath it. At 1440 that is "Generating Design
     Pattern·s"; at 390 it is all four cards.
  
     More padding would be the same mistake again with a different number, because
     the chip's width is its text — ABSTRACT and COMPLETE are not the same size,
     and neither is whatever word this vocabulary grows next. So the card stops
     guessing and starts measuring: it becomes a two-column grid, the chip takes
     a real column, and the gap between them is a gap rather than a hope.
  
     The chip does not move. In a grid whose first column is 1fr, an auto second
     column lands its content against the right edge of the card's content box —
     which is within a couple of pixels of where `right: 26px` was putting it, and
     the negative margin below spends those two pixels. The × is left exactly as
     it was, still absolute in the corner, still clear of the chip.
  
     What does change is that titles now wrap at the width they actually have
     rather than 13px more than they have. On a wide screen that costs nothing —
     the band's height is set by the three-line gloss underneath, which has room
     to spare. Between roughly 600 and 900px, where the band packs two cards to a
     row, one title gains a line and the band grows by that line. That is the true
     cost of the space the chip was taking without asking for it. */
  
  .now-ref {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    align-items: start;
    /* The cards in a row are all as tall as the tallest, and a grid handed spare
       height spreads it between its rows by default — which would push a short
       card's gloss into the middle of nowhere. The rows stay at the top and the
       spare height stays at the bottom, where it was before any of this. */
    align-content: start;
    column-gap: 8px;
  }
  /* A card with no form yet has no chip, so it should not be charged the gap. */
  .now-ref:not([data-form]) { column-gap: 0; }
  
  .now-ref-title {
    grid-area: 1 / 1 / 2 / 2;
    padding-right: 0;   /* the grid reserves the chip's column now */
  }
  .now-ref-form {
    position: static;
    grid-area: 1 / 2 / 2 / 3;
    margin-right: -2px; /* land on the pixel `right: 26px` was landing on */
  }
  .now-ref-gloss {
    grid-area: 2 / 1 / 3 / 3;
  }
  
  
  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     2 · On a phone the band is a strip you scroll, not a wall you scroll past
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  
     Everything below is phone-only, and the gate is the container and not the
     viewport: <html> is the `doc` container, so a host that docks a panel beside
     the document narrows the container and gets the phone layout, which is what
     a 400px-wide document wants whatever the screen behind it measures.
  
     The band is sticky because it is a drop target: a card you are dragging has
     to be able to reach it from anywhere in a very long page. On a touch screen
     there is no dragging and therefore no reason to pin it, and the cost of
     pinning it here is enormous — four cards at 270px wrap onto four rows, so the
     band stands 421px tall on an 844px screen and takes half of every screenful
     of the document, forever, in every view. It gives that back and becomes one
     row you push sideways. */
  
  @container doc (max-width: 720px) {
  
    .band-wrap {
      /* Not sticky. The pinned cards are still marked where they live — the accent
         rule down the left edge of a data-now card in the pool says so — so the
         fact survives losing its permanent readout, and the band is one flick from
         the top of the document when you want the index itself. It stays above the
         content rather than below it: it is the shortest thing on the page that
         answers "what am I in the middle of", and arriving at four titles before
         25 ideas is orientation, where arriving at it after them is burial. */
      position: static;
      /* nothing passes behind it any more, and a blur that frosts nothing is a
         compositor layer for no reason */
      backdrop-filter: none;
      /* The strip runs edge to edge, so the wrap keeps no side padding of its own
         and hands the inset to the head and to the scroller's own padding. */
      padding: 9px 0 10px;
    }
  
    /* The head keeps the document's left margin so "WORKING ON NOW" lines up with
       the prose underneath it, and the strip starts on the same line. */
    .band-head {
      padding-left: 30px;
      padding-right: 20px;
      margin-bottom: 8px;
    }
  
    .band {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: nowrap;
      max-width: none;
      min-height: 0;
      gap: 10px;
      /* Full bleed on the right: the strip's own padding holds the first card off
         the margin, and nothing holds the last one off the screen edge, so a card
         is always cut by the edge when there is more to the right. A cut card is
         the only honest promise of sideways — a fade or an arrow is a picture of
         one. */
      padding: 0 20px 0 30px;
      overflow-x: auto;
      overflow-y: hidden;
      overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
      scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
      scroll-padding-left: 30px;
      -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
      /* The scrollbar says nothing the cut edge has not already said, and on a
         short strip it is mostly a grey line under the cards. The strip stays
         reachable from a keyboard — every card in it is a button, and part.js
         makes the scroller itself a tab stop whenever it actually overflows. */
      scrollbar-width: none;
      /* the dashed drop outline is drawn outside the box, which a scroller clips */
      outline-offset: -3px;
    }
    .band::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }
    .band:focus-visible {
      outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
      outline-offset: -2px;
    }
  
    .now-ref {
      /* Wide enough to read a title and a line of gloss, narrow enough that the
         next card is always visibly there. cqw keeps the peek when the container
         is narrower than a phone — a docked panel, say — instead of letting one
         card fill it. */
      flex: 0 0 auto;
      width: min(256px, 72cqw);
      max-width: none;
      scroll-snap-align: start;
      /* 36px on the right is not decoration: it is the strip the × lives in, and
         it is exactly wide enough that the ×'s 44px tap area (below) can reach its
         full size without ever crossing into the title. */
      padding: 10px 36px 11px 12px;
      /* The title takes the whole first line here. At this width the chip beside
         it would leave about 120px for a serif title, which is not a title, it is
         a column of words — so the chip drops to the second line and introduces
         the gloss instead, which is the sentence it was always a label for. */
      grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
      row-gap: 3px;
    }
    .now-ref-title {
      grid-area: 1 / 1 / 2 / 3;
      /* Two lines is the card's share of the strip. A title that needs a third is
         telling you to open it, and the whole card is the button that does. */
      display: -webkit-box;
      -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
      -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
      overflow: hidden;
    }
    .now-ref-form {
      grid-area: 2 / 1 / 3 / 2;
      align-self: start;
      margin-top: 1px;
      margin-right: 8px;
    }
    .now-ref-gloss {
      grid-area: 2 / 2 / 3 / 3;
      margin-top: 0;
      /* The band is an index, not a reading surface. Two lines, and the card is
         one tap from the paragraph it came from. */
      display: -webkit-box;
      -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
      -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
      overflow: hidden;
    }
  
    /* A finger is not a cursor. The × keeps its 18px glyph — this document is its
       typography and a chunky button would be a different document — and grows a
       44px tap area underneath it instead. The expander reaches out into the
       card's right strip and the gap beside it, where there is nothing to hit; it
       stops at the title's edge, so the two things this card does (unpin it, go
       to it) never have to guess which one you meant. */
    .now-ref-x {
      /* it is already absolute, which already makes it the containing block the
         expander below measures from — it only moves in a little */
      top: 6px;
      right: 6px;
    }
    .now-ref-x::before {
      content: "";
      position: absolute;
      inset: -13px;
    }
  
    /* An empty band that says nothing is a band nobody discovers — but what it
       said was "drag an idea up here", and there is no dragging on a touch screen,
       so on a touch screen it says what is true instead of what is impossible.
       A narrow window with a mouse can still drag, and still reads the original. */
    .band.is-empty::after {
      flex: 1 1 auto;
      min-width: 0;
      padding: 12px 0;
    }
    @media (hover: none) {
      .band.is-empty::after {
        content: "Nothing pinned. Ideas you mark as current work collect here — they stay in their column.";
      }
    }
  
    /* Snapping is a settle, and a settle is motion. Asked for less of it, the
       strip still snaps where it is going but never takes the scroll out of your
       hand. Nothing here adds a smooth scroll to disable; this is the one piece
       of movement the strip introduces, and this is where it is given back. */
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      .band { scroll-behavior: auto; scroll-snap-type: x proximity; }
    }
  }
  
  /* Below about 380px the caption stops fitting on the heading's line and starts
     costing the band a whole row of chrome to say something the interface already
     says: the × is labelled with what it removes, and a pinned idea still carries
     the accent rule down its left edge in the pool, where it never left. */
  @container doc (max-width: 380px) {
    .band-head .count { display: none; }
  }

  /* ── columns ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* ── the columns, on a phone ──────────────────────────────────────────────
     The sheet above already decided the hard part: narrowing these boards
     cannot mean fewer tracks, because a card chooses its column by its own
     attribute and a track that is not in the template is a track at zero width
     with cards still standing in it. So the columns stay, and the board scrolls
     sideways.
  
     What it did not decide is what sideways *feels* like. Today it is an
     accident — 682px of free scroll inside a page that is already scrolling the
     other way, with no snap, no peek and nothing on screen admitting a column
     exists. Everything below turns that accident into a gesture: one column
     fills the thumb's width, the next one shows its shoulder, and a swipe lands
     on a column rather than somewhere between two.
  
     All of it lives in one container query. `doc` is the <html> element's own
     inline size, so a host that docks a panel beside the document narrows this
     the same way a narrow phone does — which a media query would refuse to
     believe. Nothing here fires above 720px, so the wide board is the board it
     has always been. */
  
  /* The two pieces of chrome the script builds are page-only in both senses:
     they carry data-marble-transient, and above this breakpoint they are not on
     screen at all. This is the rule that makes the second one true. */
  .rv-cols, .rv-hud { display: none; }
  
  @container doc (max-width: 720px) {
  
    /* A flag, not a second breakpoint. The script needs to know whether the
       carousel is on, and the only honest answer is the one CSS just gave — so
       it reads this property rather than measuring the window for itself. */
    .pool { --rv-phone: 1; }
  
    .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool,
    .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool,
    .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool {
      /* 84% of the screen, capped so a large phone does not run an eighty
         character line. What is left over is the peek: about 50px of the next
         column, which is the whole affordance — a card cut off at the edge is
         the only honest way a board says "there is more of me this way". */
      --rv-col: min(340px, calc(84cqw - 10px));
      --rv-gutter: 14px;
  
      /* Full bleed. The peek only reads if the next column runs off the
         *screen* rather than stopping twenty pixels short of it inside a text
         margin. These two lengths are the stage's own side padding; the script
         re-measures them, so this keeps working if that padding ever moves. */
      margin-left: calc(var(--rv-bleed-l, 30px) * -1);
      margin-right: calc(var(--rv-bleed-r, 20px) * -1);
  
      padding-left: var(--rv-gutter);
      /* Trailing room the width of the scrollport, so the *last* column can
         come to rest at the left edge like every other one. Without it the
         scroller runs out of travel before the last snap position and a
         mandatory snap sits the board down somewhere nobody asked for. */
      padding-right: calc(100cqw - var(--rv-col) - var(--rv-gutter));
      padding-bottom: 8px;
  
      scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
      scroll-padding-left: var(--rv-gutter);
    }
  
    .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, var(--rv-col)); }
    .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, var(--rv-col)); }
    .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool { grid-template-columns: repeat(6, var(--rv-col)); }
  
    /* The lane is the column's own head and it sits in row 1, which makes it
       exactly the box the scroller should come to rest against. Every card
       below shares its left edge, so snapping the lane snaps the column.
       `always` is the difference between a carousel and a slide: one flick
       moves one column, however hard it is thrown. */
    .stage[data-arrange="form"] .pool > .lane,
    .stage[data-arrange="when"] .pool > .lane,
    .stage[data-arrange="term"] .pool > .lane {
      scroll-snap-align: start;
      scroll-snap-stop: always;
      padding-bottom: 4px;
    }
  
    /* A column name set at 10px was a label on a board you could see all of.
       Here it is the thing telling you where you are, so it is read at the
       weight of a heading and its rule is drawn in ink rather than in hairline.
       The count belongs to the strip above it and is not repeated here. */
    .pool > .lane > h4 {
      font-size: 12px;
      letter-spacing: .1em;
      color: var(--ink-2);
      padding-bottom: 9px;
      border-bottom-color: var(--muted);
    }
    .pool > .lane > .lane-why {
      font-size: 12px;
      color: var(--muted);
    }
  
    /* ── venue pills ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Every one of these is data-marble-editable, which makes it a tap target
       for editing and puts it under the 44px floor. It gets there the way the
       rest of the document does: a little more air in the pill itself, and an
       invisible expander for the remainder, so the type is untouched and the
       pill still reads as a small uppercase mark rather than a button. */
    .venues { gap: 15px 6px; margin-top: 8px; }
    .venue {
      position: relative;
      font-size: 10px;
      padding: 9px 10px;
    }
    .venue::before {
      content: "";
      position: absolute;
      inset: -7px -2px;
    }
  
    /* ── the map ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       A map is a claim about where things sit relative to each other, and at
       390px the claim is unreadable: a 612px canvas of absolutely positioned
       cards, clipped, overlapping, with no way to pan it. So on a phone the map
       stops pretending. The cards keep their coordinates — nothing here is
       written to the file, and the same document on a laptop still opens on the
       canvas it was arranged into — but they are read in file order down the
       page, which is the one arrangement that always fits.
  
       The inline left/top has to be shouted down, exactly as the sheet above
       shouts it down for every other arrangement: it is an inline style, and no
       selector outranks one. */
    .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 10px;
      min-height: 0;
      overflow: visible;
      border: 0;
      background: none;
    }
    .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool > .item {
      position: relative;
      left: auto !important;
      top: auto !important;
      max-width: none;
      cursor: auto;
      box-shadow: none;
    }
    /* Said out loud, because a view that quietly becomes a different view is a
       view the person will think is broken. */
    .stage[data-arrange="map"] .pool::before {
      content: "The map is a layout, and this screen is too narrow to hold one. The cards are listed here in the order they sit in the file; open the document wider to see where they are.";
      display: block;
      font-size: 12px;
      line-height: 1.5;
      color: var(--faint);
      padding: 10px 12px;
      border: 1px dashed var(--hair);
      border-radius: var(--r-2);
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 60%, var(--card));
    }
  
    /* ── + Idea ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The spine packer hides the column buttons it has no column for with
       `btn.hidden = true`, and the rule that shows them — a class inside an
       attribute selector — outranks the browser's own [hidden] rule, so they
       were never hidden at all. With no left or top of their own they fall back
       to the pool's top-left corner and land on the first card; one column
       means two of them sitting there. Hiding is not a suggestion. */
    .stage[data-arrange="thesis"] .pool > .col-add[hidden] { display: none; }
  
    /* The ones that are left are 35px of dashed outline, and the packer places
       them by measuring — its idea of how tall they are is written into where
       it puts the next one. So the visible button is not touched; each one
       grows an invisible expander into the slack the packer already left above
       and below it, up to the 44px floor, and the three of them are cut so
       that no two claim the same pixel. */
    .col-add::before { content: ""; position: absolute; }
    .col-add:not(.col-add-thesis)::before { inset: -5px 0 -4px; }
    .col-add-thesis:not(.is-rail)::before { inset: -1px 0 -8px; }
    .col-add-thesis.is-rail::before { inset: 0 -2px; }
  
    /* ── the framings ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Ten information graphics drawn on a 960-unit canvas, whose labels are
       nine to thirteen units tall. Fitted to a 340px column that is a little
       under 4px of type — a picture of a diagram rather than a diagram. So the
       figure becomes its own sideways scroller and the drawing keeps a width
       its labels survive; the title and the caption are prose and stay put,
       which `position: sticky` gives them for free inside a scroller that only
       moves one way. */
    .viz {
      overflow-x: auto;
      overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
      margin-left: calc(var(--rv-bleed-l, 30px) * -1);
      margin-right: calc(var(--rv-bleed-r, 20px) * -1);
      padding-bottom: 6px;
    }
    /* The text keeps its margin and the scroller has none, so sticky has one
       unambiguous edge to measure from — the screen's. Browsers do not agree
       about how a scroll container's own padding enters that measurement, and
       the fix for a caption that drifts is to give them nothing to disagree
       about. */
    .viz > h4,
    .viz > figcaption {
      position: sticky;
      left: var(--rv-bleed-l, 30px);
      margin-left: var(--rv-bleed-l, 30px);
      margin-right: var(--rv-bleed-r, 20px);
    }
    .viz > h4 { font-size: 14px; }
    .viz > figcaption { font-size: 13.5px; }
    .viz-svg {
      min-width: 780px;
      margin-left: var(--rv-bleed-l, 30px);
    }
  
    /* ── the column strip ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
       One segment per column, lit for the one being read. It is the standing
       answer to "how far along am I", and it is also how to cross a six column
       board without six swipes. Built in script, owned by the page, never
       written to the file. */
    .rv-cols {
      display: none;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 10px;
      /* Bled and inset exactly as the board is, so segment one begins where
         column one begins. The script hands it the same two measurements. */
      margin-left: calc(var(--rv-bleed-l, 30px) * -1);
      margin-right: calc(var(--rv-bleed-r, 20px) * -1);
      padding: 0 14px;
      margin-bottom: 6px;
    }
    .stage[data-arrange="form"] .rv-cols,
    .stage[data-arrange="when"] .rv-cols,
    .stage[data-arrange="term"] .rv-cols { display: flex; }
  
    .rv-cols-n {
      font: 600 10px/1 var(--sans);
      letter-spacing: .1em;
      color: var(--faint);
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      flex: none;
    }
    .rv-cols-track {
      display: flex;
      flex: 1;
      gap: 5px;
      min-width: 0;
    }
    /* 44px of height on every one of them; the mark itself is a 2px rule,
       because this is a place-marker and not a row of buttons. */
    .rv-dot {
      flex: 1 1 0;
      min-width: 0;
      height: 44px;
      position: relative;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0;
      border: 0;
      background: none;
      cursor: pointer;
      -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
    }
    .rv-dot::after {
      content: "";
      position: absolute;
      left: 0; right: 0; top: 50%;
      height: 2px;
      margin-top: -1px;
      border-radius: 2px;
      background: var(--hair);
      transition: background .18s ease, height .18s ease;
    }
    .rv-dot[aria-current="true"]::after {
      height: 3px;
      margin-top: -1.5px;
      background: var(--accent);
    }
    .rv-dot:focus-visible {
      outline: 1px solid var(--accent);
      outline-offset: -8px;
      border-radius: var(--r-2);
    }
  
    /* ── the column you are in ──────────────────────────────────────────────
       A lane header cannot stay in view here: the pool is its own scrollport
       and it does not scroll vertically, so there is no scrollport for a sticky
       header to stick to. The answer is the gesture's own receipt — a name that
       appears while the board is moving and leaves when it stops. It never
       takes a tap and it never occupies the page. */
    .rv-hud {
      position: fixed;
      left: 50%;
      bottom: calc(env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px) + 84px);
      z-index: 60;
      transform: translateX(-50%);
      display: none;
      max-width: 78cqw;
      overflow: hidden;
      text-overflow: ellipsis;
      white-space: nowrap;
      padding: 8px 14px;
      border-radius: 999px;
      border: 1px solid var(--hair);
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 88%, var(--card));
      backdrop-filter: blur(8px);
      box-shadow: 0 2px 10px color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 12%, transparent);
      font: 500 12px/1.2 var(--sans);
      color: var(--ink-2);
      pointer-events: none;
      opacity: 0;
      transition: opacity .18s ease;
    }
    .rv-hud[data-rv-on] { display: block; }
    .rv-hud.rv-lit { opacity: 1; }
    .rv-hud b {
      font-weight: 600;
      color: var(--faint);
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      margin-right: .5em;
    }
  }
  
  /* Snapping is placement, not animation, so it stays. Smooth travel is
     animation, and the strip's buttons ask for it explicitly — so this is the
     one place it has to be taken back. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .pool { scroll-behavior: auto; }
    .rv-hud { transition: none; }
  }

</style>
</head>
<body>

<aside class="rail">
  <span class="kicker">Research</span>
  <div class="rail-title" data-marble-id="rail-title" data-marble-editable>Research Vision</div>
  <div class="rail-sub" data-marble-id="rail-sub" data-marble-editable>Malleable software &amp; generative UI. Every idea below exists once in this file; the views are layouts over the same cards, so a change in one is a change in all of them.</div>

  <nav class="rail-versions" data-marble-id="rail-versions" aria-label="Document versions" hidden></nav>

  <p class="rail-group">Views</p>
  <ul class="nav" data-marble-transient>
    <li><button data-preset="spine"><span class="n">01</span>Spine</button></li>
    <li><button data-preset="board"><span class="n">02</span>Board</button></li>
    <li><button data-preset="timeline"><span class="n">03</span>Timeline</button></li>
    <li><button data-preset="now"><span class="n">04</span>Now</button></li>
    <li><button data-preset="map"><span class="n">05</span>Map</button></li>
    <li><button data-preset="claims"><span class="n">06</span>Claims</button></li>
    <li><button data-preset="questions"><span class="n">07</span>Questions</button></li>
    <li><button data-preset="framings"><span class="n">08</span>Framings</button></li>
  </ul>

  <p class="rail-group">Arrange by</p>
  <div class="dial" data-marble-transient>
    <button data-set="arrange:thesis">thesis</button>
    <button data-set="arrange:form">form</button>
    <button data-set="arrange:term">term</button>
    <button data-set="arrange:when">when</button>
    <button data-set="arrange:map">map</button>
    <button data-set="arrange:list">list</button>
    <button data-set="arrange:focus">focus</button>
  </div>

  <p class="rail-group">Focus on</p>
  <div class="dial" data-marble-transient>
    <button data-set="focus:t1">T1</button>
    <button data-set="focus:t2">T2</button>
    <button data-set="focus:t3">T3</button>
  </div>

  <p class="rail-group">Show</p>
  <div class="dial" data-marble-transient>
    <button data-set="show:all">everything</button>
    <button data-set="show:claims">claims</button>
    <button data-set="show:questions">questions</button>
    <button data-set="show:targets">targets</button>
  </div>

  <div class="rail-note" data-marble-id="rail-note" data-marble-editable>Take hold of a card by the rail down its left edge. In Spine, Board, Timeline and Now, drop it anywhere in a column to move it there — the wash shows the column, the line shows the place. Shift while dropping in Spine adds a second thesis instead of moving it. Arrow keys do the same from the keyboard. Drag a card up into the band to pin it as current work — the card stays in its column; the band is a reference. The form chip cycles rough → abstract → concrete → complete; a second chip appears once an idea is concrete. Mod+Z takes any of it back, a whole drag at a time.</div>
</aside>

<main data-focus="t1" class="stage" data-marble-id="stage" data-arrange="thesis" data-show="all">

<div class="band-wrap" data-marble-id="band-wrap">
  <div class="band-head">
    <h2 data-marble-id="band-h" data-marble-editable>Working on now</h2>
    <span class="count" data-marble-transient>by reference · stays in the pool</span>
  </div>
  <div class="band" data-marble-id="band">
</div>
</div>

<div class="stage-in">
  <div class="vhead">
    <span class="kicker" data-marble-transient>One pool, many readings</span>
    <h2 data-marble-id="v-h" data-marble-editable>My Malleable Software Research Program</h2>
    <p class="sub" data-marble-id="v-s" data-marble-editable>Every idea here is one card. The rail changes how the cards are arranged and how much of each one you read — it never changes which cards exist. Ideas run from rough to complete, and the line between a note and a project is a chip you cycle, not a category you file it under.</p>
  </div>

  <marble-alt class="northstar" data-marble-id="ns" data-marble-active="v2">
    <p data-marble-id="ns-v1" data-marble-alt="v1" data-marble-editable>Generative UIs are not the interfaces we know how to design. They are design spaces, not designs — and they need their own principles, their own craft, and their own idea of what a finished interface even is.</p>
    <p data-marble-id="ns-v2" data-marble-alt="v2" data-marble-editable>Malleable software only matters if people can envision the interfaces they could have. My work widens what a person can imagine building, and then collapses the distance to building it.</p>
    <p data-marble-id="ns-v3" data-marble-alt="v3" data-marble-editable>A generated interface is personal by construction. I study how to design for one person's experience — eliciting it continuously, modelling it, and translating between people's models of it.</p>
    <p data-marble-id="ns-v4" data-marble-alt="v4" data-marble-editable>My research program broadly takes the perspective that building software with AI requires new design principles for malleable user interfaces, new educational approaches to teach people how to use these new interfaces, collaboration tools to help people work together just as effectively under a more personalized software ecosystem.</p>
  </marble-alt>

  <div class="pool-actions" data-marble-transient>
    <button class="btn add-fallback" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-item" data-marble-into="#pool">+ Idea</button>
    <button class="btn add-fallback" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-thesis" data-marble-into="#pool" data-set-thesis="auto">+ Thesis</button>
    <button class="btn" data-toggle="density">Compact</button>
    <button class="btn" data-toggle="arrange-map">Arrange the map</button>
    <span class="count" data-counts></span>
  </div>

  <div class="pool" id="pool" data-marble-id="pool" data-show="all" data-marble-sortable="items" data-marble-canvas="off">
  <div class="lane" data-lane="form:rough" data-marble-id="l-f1"><h4 data-marble-id="l-f1-h" data-marble-editable>Rough</h4><p class="lane-why" data-marble-id="l-f1-w" data-marble-editable>A sentence and a feeling. Nothing has been decided.</p></div>
  <div class="lane" data-lane="form:abstract" data-marble-id="l-f2"><h4 data-marble-id="l-f2-h" data-marble-editable>Abstract</h4><p class="lane-why" data-marble-id="l-f2-w" data-marble-editable>The shape of a contribution is visible; the study is not.</p></div>
  <div class="lane" data-lane="form:concrete" data-marble-id="l-f3"><h4 data-marble-id="l-f3-h" data-marble-editable>Concrete</h4><p class="lane-why" data-marble-id="l-f3-w" data-marble-editable>There is a thing to build or run, and a venue to aim it at.</p></div>
  <div class="lane" data-lane="form:complete" data-marble-id="l-f4"><h4 data-marble-id="l-f4-h" data-marble-editable>Complete</h4><p class="lane-why" data-marble-id="l-f4-w" data-marble-editable>Written up and out of your hands.</p></div>

  <div class="lane" data-lane="when:now" data-marble-id="l-w1"><h4 data-marble-id="l-w1-h" data-marble-editable>Now</h4></div>
  <div class="lane" data-lane="when:next" data-marble-id="l-w2"><h4 data-marble-id="l-w2-h" data-marble-editable>Next</h4></div>
  <div class="lane" data-lane="when:later" data-marble-id="l-w3"><h4 data-marble-id="l-w3-h" data-marble-editable>Later</h4></div>
  <div class="lane" data-lane="when:none" data-marble-id="l-w4"><h4 data-marble-id="l-w4-h" data-marble-editable>Unsorted</h4></div>

  <div class="lane" data-lane="term:f26" data-marble-id="l-t1"><h4 data-marble-id="l-t1-h" data-marble-editable>Fall 2026</h4><div class="venues"><span class="venue" data-tier="core" data-marble-id="vn-chi27" data-marble-editable>CHI 2027 · ~Sept (check)</span><span class="venue" data-tier="background" data-marble-id="vn-iui" data-marble-editable>IUI · ~Oct (check)</span><span class="venue" data-tier="background" data-marble-id="vn-iclr" data-marble-editable>ICLR · ~Sept (check)</span></div></div>
  <div class="lane" data-lane="term:w27" data-marble-id="l-t2"><h4 data-marble-id="l-t2-h" data-marble-editable>Winter 2027</h4><div class="venues"><span class="venue" data-tier="background" data-marble-id="vn-dis" data-marble-editable>DIS · ~Jan (check)</span><span class="venue" data-tier="background" data-marble-id="vn-icml" data-marble-editable>ICML · ~Jan (check)</span></div></div>
  <div class="lane" data-lane="term:sp27" data-marble-id="l-t3"><h4 data-marble-id="l-t3-h" data-marble-editable>Spring 2027</h4><div class="venues"><span class="venue" data-tier="core" data-marble-id="vn-uist27" data-marble-editable>UIST 2027 · ~Apr (check)</span><span class="venue" data-tier="background" data-marble-id="vn-cscw" data-marble-editable>CSCW · ~Apr (check)</span><span class="venue" data-tier="background" data-marble-id="vn-vis" data-marble-editable>IEEE VIS · ~Mar (check)</span><span class="venue" data-tier="background" data-marble-id="vn-neurips" data-marble-editable>NeurIPS · ~May (check)</span></div></div>
  <div class="lane" data-lane="term:su27" data-marble-id="l-t4"><h4 data-marble-id="l-t4-h" data-marble-editable>Summer 2027</h4><div class="venues"><span class="venue" data-tier="background" data-marble-id="vn-aaai" data-marble-editable>AAAI · ~Aug (check)</span></div></div>
  <div class="lane" data-lane="term:f27" data-marble-id="l-t5"><h4 data-marble-id="l-t5-h" data-marble-editable>Fall 2027</h4><div class="venues"><span class="venue" data-tier="core" data-marble-id="vn-chi28" data-marble-editable>CHI 2028 · ~Sept (check)</span></div></div>
  <div class="lane" data-lane="term:beyond" data-marble-id="l-t6"><h4 data-marble-id="l-t6-h" data-marble-editable>Unscheduled</h4><p class="lane-why" data-marble-id="l-t6-w" data-marble-editable>Not aimed at a term yet. Drag one left when it is.</p></div>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="t1" data-kind="thesis" data-thesis="t1" style="left:24px;top:20px" data-marble-removable>
    <h3 data-marble-id="t1-h" data-marble-editable>Thesis 1 · GenUI Design: GenUI is a different design problem from conventional UI. How do we design GenUI?</h3>
    <p class="gloss" data-marble-id="t1-g" data-marble-editable>A different design problem from traditional UI</p>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="t1-s" data-marble-editable>You are no longer designing one interface but a space of possible ones. The user's journey is authored by them, not you — so a fixed set of affordances for a fixed path stops describing the job.</p>
    <div class="claim" data-marble-id="t1-c" data-mark="firm"><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="maturity"></button><h4 data-marble-id="t1-ch" data-marble-editable>Design the space, not the screen</h4><p data-marble-id="t1-cs" data-marble-editable>In GenUI the deliverable is a space of possible interfaces. A single mockup is a sample from that space, not the design.</p></div>
    <ul class="beats" data-marble-id="t1-b" data-marble-sortable="beats">
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="t1-b1" data-mark="warm" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="t1-b1-t" data-marble-editable>Does GenUI require a different design process, not just different artifacts?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="t1-b2" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="t1-b2-t" data-marble-editable>Settle the north-star statement — four framings down to one</p></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="addlink" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-beat" data-marble-into="prev">+ question</button>
  </article>

<article class="item" data-marble-id="i-odi" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t1" data-form="complete" data-mark="submitted" data-term="f26" style="left:24px;top:137px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-odi-h" data-marble-editable>Malleable ODIs &amp; Meridian</h3>
    <p class="gloss" data-marble-id="i-odi-g" data-marble-editable>A design space, not a design</p>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-odi-s" data-marble-editable>Designing for a design space of possible interfaces rather than one. What is the unit of design, and the unit of evaluation, when the artifact is a space and not a screen?</p>
    <p class="meta" data-marble-id="wamjsimw"><span class="k" data-marble-id="hfxeoxs3">venue</span><span data-marble-id="i-odi-v" data-marble-editable>CHI 2025 / UIST 2025</span></p>
    <ul class="beats" data-marble-id="i-odi-b" data-marble-sortable="beats">
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-odi-b1" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-odi-b1-t" data-marble-editable>What is the unit of design when the artifact is a space?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-odi-b2" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-odi-b2-t" data-marble-editable>How do you evaluate a design space rather than a design?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-odi-b3" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-odi-b3-t" data-marble-editable>Design for a design space of interfaces, not a single interface (Interacting with a Design Space of User Interfaces)</p></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="addlink" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-beat" data-marble-into="prev">+ question</button>
  </article>
<article class="item" data-marble-id="i-elicit" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t1" data-form="complete" data-mark="submitted" data-when="next" data-term="f26" style="left:810px;top:118px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-elicit-h" data-marble-editable>Elicitive UIs</h3>
    <p class="gloss" data-marble-id="i-elicit-g" data-marble-editable>Ask along the way, not in a wizard</p>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-elicit-s" data-marble-editable>Adaptive elicitation of preferences that rides along with use. Not an adaptive interface that guesses, and not a setup wizard — a continuous, low-friction read on what the person wants next.</p>
    <div class="claim" data-marble-id="i-elicit-c" data-mark="forming"><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="maturity"></button><h4 data-marble-id="i-elicit-ch" data-marble-editable>Elicitation rides along with use</h4><p data-marble-id="i-elicit-cs" data-marble-editable>Preferences are surfaced continuously through interaction, not collected once at the start. A generative interface that stops asking has stopped fitting.</p></div>
    <p class="meta" data-marble-id="at23gpn3"><span class="k" data-marble-id="t6x1qsge">venue</span><span data-marble-id="i-elicit-v" data-marble-editable>CHI 2027</span></p>
    <ul class="beats" data-marble-id="i-elicit-b" data-marble-sortable="beats">
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-elicit-b1" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-elicit-b1-t" data-marble-editable>When to ask versus infer?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-elicit-b2" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-elicit-b2-t" data-marble-editable>How to elicit without ever staging a configuration step?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-elicit-b3" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-elicit-b3-t" data-marble-editable>See the existing "Elicitive UIs" document in this drive</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-elicit-b4" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-elicit-b4-t" data-marble-editable>Adaptively drawing out user needs to tailor generative user interfaces</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-elicit-b5" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-elicit-b5-t" data-marble-editable>Pull the existing document into a paper outline</p></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="addlink" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-beat" data-marble-into="prev">+ question</button>
  </article>
<article data-now="1" class="item" data-marble-id="i-marble" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t1" data-form="abstract" data-mark="active" data-when="next" data-term="f26" style="left:286px;top:118px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-marble-h" data-marble-editable>Marble</h3>
    <p class="gloss" data-marble-id="i-marble-g" data-marble-editable>The interface is its own editable source</p>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-marble-s" data-marble-editable>Documents that are their own source: what is on screen is the file, so changing the interface is changing the code. A working substrate for malleable software with no build step and no state layer.</p>
    <div class="claim" data-marble-id="i-marble-c" data-mark="firm"><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="maturity"></button><h4 data-marble-id="i-marble-ch" data-marble-editable>The interface is the code</h4><p data-marble-id="i-marble-cs" data-marble-editable>When changing the interface is editing the source, there is no gap between using and authoring — and no separate state that can drift from what the file says. (Marble.)</p></div>
    <p class="meta" data-marble-id="mt8u3aan"><span class="k" data-marble-id="a3nfx2r9">venue</span><span data-marble-id="i-marble-v" data-marble-editable>UIST</span></p>
    <ul class="beats" data-marble-id="i-marble-b" data-marble-sortable="beats">
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-marble-b1" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-marble-b1-t" data-marble-editable>What cannot be expressed as an op?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-marble-b2" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-marble-b2-t" data-marble-editable>How far does "the file is the app" scale before it needs a framework again?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-marble-b3" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-marble-b3-t" data-marble-editable>Might we have to use this tool as a platform that students use to learn to build their own software apps?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-marble-b4" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-marble-b4-t" data-marble-editable>I'm thinking about this in the perspective of "Designing Generative Interf not UI"</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-marble-b5" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-marble-b5-t" data-marble-editable>Malleable Software with Coding Agents... Editing files in-place through the UI enables users to build apps with agents inside the interface. Contribution: New app building framework for collaborative human-agent app creation. The Cool Thing: Use agents not as just building/fixing the app, but also as features in the app itself.</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-marble-b6" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-marble-b6-t" data-marble-editable>Write up "the interface is the code" as a claim, not a demo</p></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="addlink" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-beat" data-marble-into="prev">+ question</button>
  </article>
<article data-parts="form state gloss say claim meta beats" data-now="2" class="item" data-marble-id="i-patterns" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t1" data-form="abstract" style="left:24px;top:862px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-patterns-h" data-marble-editable>Generating Design Patterns</h3>
    <p class="gloss" data-marble-editable data-ph="One-line summary" data-marble-id="aylxhyfi"></p>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-patterns-s" data-marble-editable data-ph="What it is, in a sentence or two">This would be an extension of Malleable ODIs research, where the goal is to generate UIs that support various design patterns.

Interns: Jeremy Chen</p>
    <div class="claim" data-mark="hunch" data-marble-id="m2kbas7i"><h4 data-marble-editable data-ph="The claim, stated flat" data-marble-id="pwkas5ut">Design Tools for GenUI</h4><p data-marble-editable data-ph="What it rules in or out" data-marble-id="x3g2296b">Is this about the GenUI to give or is it about the design tool for GenUI? I think it's about building a design tool for design pattern generation, and observing how UI/UX designers will design GenUI using this tool. GenUI Design is just a completely new practice, we want to observe this workflow.</p></div>
    <p class="meta" data-marble-id="xow7k7fd"><span class="k" data-marble-id="s4mtcgju">venue</span><span data-marble-editable data-ph="—" data-marble-id="32pysl0c">CHI 2027</span></p>
    <ul class="beats" data-marble-sortable="beats" data-marble-id="nmnw635v"><li class="beat" data-marble-removable data-marble-id="k937483o"><p data-marble-editable data-ph="An open question" data-marble-id="99fnyzoe">fdsaf</p></li></ul>
  </article>
<article data-now="3" data-parts="form state beats claim gloss say" class="item" data-kind="idea" data-form="abstract" style="left:24px;top:1259px" data-marble-removable data-thesis="t1" data-marble-id="d8k9bv3r"><h3 data-marble-editable data-ph="The idea, named" data-marble-id="pvwslmg4">Affordances for Realtime GenUI</h3><p class="gloss" data-marble-editable data-ph="One-line summary" data-marble-id="619qruqy"></p><p class="say" data-marble-editable data-ph="What it is, in a sentence or two" data-marble-id="g52y9zo7">This is thinking about what needs to be the design of our interfaces today, if we are going to interact with realtime generative UI? This encapsulates using models such as Jev (Typesafe AI) and Solaris (Runway AI). This design framework would be applicable to Jev and World Interface Models -- as long as these models can generate and re-generate UIs in realtime.</p>
    <div class="claim" data-mark="hunch" data-marble-id="xbisr18j"><h4 data-marble-editable data-ph="The claim, stated flat" data-marble-id="kd2lyro3">Designing for Realtime Requires N Factors</h4><p data-marble-editable data-ph="What it rules in or out" data-marble-id="4t694wgi">- Setting "Anti-affordances" (what the user cannot do)
- Designing for "Infinite Affordances" (not entirely sure what this means though)</p></div>
    <p class="meta" data-marble-id="qk2kw9tm"><span class="k" data-marble-id="mbsgyjxv">venue</span><span data-marble-editable data-ph="—" data-marble-id="zr4ey1pn"></span></p><ul class="beats" data-marble-sortable="beats" data-marble-id="c1kb1hsj"><li class="beat" data-marble-removable data-marble-id="ctuvjqrt"><p data-marble-editable data-ph="An open question" data-marble-id="l6r0dir1"></p></li></ul>
  </article>


  <article class="item" data-marble-id="t2" data-kind="thesis" data-thesis="t2" style="left:300px;top:20px" data-marble-removable>
    <h3 data-marble-id="t2-h" data-marble-editable>Thesis 2 · UI Envisioning: Leveraging GenUI is a new skill. How do we upskill users to build software tools with AI?</h3>
    <p class="gloss" data-marble-id="t2-g" data-marble-editable>The power to envision more interfaces</p>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="t2-s" data-marble-editable>The bottleneck is not generation, it is imagination. People can only ask for interfaces they can already picture; the work is widening the picture and shortening the path from picture to artifact.</p>
    <div class="claim" data-marble-id="t2-c" data-mark="forming"><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="maturity"></button><h4 data-marble-id="t2-ch" data-marble-editable>Imagination is the bottleneck, not generation</h4><p data-marble-id="t2-cs" data-marble-editable>People can only request interfaces they can picture. Widening and provoking that picture is the design work now that producing the artifact is cheap.</p></div>
  </article>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="t3" data-kind="thesis" data-thesis="t3" style="left:576px;top:20px" data-marble-removable>
    <h3 data-marble-id="t3-h" data-marble-editable>Thesis 3 · Personal User Experiences: GenUI is surfacing a phenomenon of Personal User Experiences. How do we design for it?</h3>
    <p class="gloss" data-marble-id="t3-g" data-marble-editable>Personal UX design</p>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="t3-s" data-marble-editable>Generated interfaces are personal by construction, so the design target is one person's experience, not a persona. That needs models of an individual's UX and ways to collaborate across them. What are the implications of Personal User Experience?</p>
    <div class="claim" data-marble-id="t3-c" data-mark="forming"><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="maturity"></button><h4 data-marble-id="t3-ch" data-marble-editable>Personal by construction</h4><p data-marble-id="t3-cs" data-marble-editable>A generated interface is made for one person in one moment. Model the person, not a persona — and treat that model as something they own.</p></div>
  </article>





  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-pux" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t3" data-form="abstract" data-when="later" data-term="sp27" style="left:576px;top:121px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-pux-h" data-marble-editable>Personal UX Models</h3>
    <p class="gloss" data-marble-id="i-pux-g" data-marble-editable>Condition a generator on one person</p>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-pux-s" data-marble-editable>A model of one individual's user experience that a generator can condition on. Not demographics, not a persona — the specific texture of how this person reads, moves through, and wants an interface.</p>
    <p class="meta" data-marble-id="jq4howxe"><span class="k" data-marble-id="tnpeprnf">venue</span><span data-marble-id="i-pux-v" data-marble-editable>CHI</span></p>
    <ul class="beats" data-marble-id="i-pux-b" data-marble-sortable="beats">
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-pux-b1" data-mark="warm" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-pux-b1-t" data-marble-editable>What goes in it — is it a knowledge graph, or is it about creativity and envisioning, which a graph does not capture?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-pux-b2" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-pux-b2-t" data-marble-editable>How is it built and kept current?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-pux-b3" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-pux-b3-t" data-marble-editable>Who owns it, and who can read it?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-pux-b4" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-pux-b4-t" data-marble-editable>A small probe study of one person</p></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="addlink" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-beat" data-marble-into="prev">+ question</button>
  </article>




  <article data-mark="seed" data-thesis="t2" data-parts="form beats say claim meta" data-term="sp27" class="item" data-kind="idea" data-form="concrete" style="left:852px;top:400px" data-marble-removable data-marble-id="tdymvi6h"><h3 data-marble-editable data-ph="The idea, named" data-marble-id="825fxrnv">Stroke Curling</h3>
    <p class="gloss" data-marble-editable data-ph="One-line summary" data-marble-id="7304fhpo"></p><p class="say" data-marble-editable data-ph="What it is, in a sentence or two" data-marble-id="9mef3inw">MSR Project for UIST 2027</p>
    <div class="claim" data-mark="forming" data-marble-id="u06fc330"><h4 data-marble-editable data-ph="The claim, stated flat" data-marble-id="yyywykpn">How do we navigate and refine a continuously diverse design space?</h4><p data-marble-editable data-ph="What it rules in or out" data-marble-id="84j0zlow">Curl of the stroke can map to tuning diversity</p></div>
    <p class="meta" data-marble-id="i8qfdhm4"><span class="k" data-marble-id="3kap9lud">venue</span><span data-marble-editable data-ph="—" data-marble-id="98b3ysd1">UIST 2027</span></p><ul class="beats" data-marble-sortable="beats" data-marble-id="ztv2kl2a"><li class="beat" data-marble-removable data-marble-id="03mj5ab8"><p data-marble-editable data-ph="An open question" data-marble-id="av9h1lf0"></p></li></ul>
  </article>
  <article data-parts="form say beats claim meta" data-thesis="t2" data-term="sp27" class="item" data-kind="idea" data-form="abstract" style="left:852px;top:490px" data-marble-removable data-marble-id="gyx185po"><h3 data-marble-editable data-ph="The idea, named" data-marble-id="m7fsn0jr">AppGalleries</h3><p class="say" data-marble-editable data-ph="What it is, in a sentence or two" data-marble-id="8l1w9e7h">MSR Project for UIST 2027</p>
    <div class="claim" data-mark="forming" data-marble-id="6ea6mfzd"><h4 data-marble-editable data-ph="The claim, stated flat" data-marble-id="p6bh7ab4">How do we represent a design space of generated interfaces?</h4><p data-marble-editable data-ph="What it rules in or out" data-marble-id="k7bhgteo">What is the key problem here, and what is the key solution we propose?</p></div>
    <p class="meta" data-marble-id="t8yc73lb"><span class="k" data-marble-id="vohcakgu">venue</span><span data-marble-editable data-ph="—" data-marble-id="hfivrbk1">UIST 2027</span></p><ul class="beats" data-marble-sortable="beats" data-marble-id="q8vlcnk8"><li class="beat" data-marble-removable data-marble-id="wis25ska"><p data-marble-editable data-ph="An open question" data-marble-id="6bceqgxs"></p></li></ul>
  </article>
  <article data-parts="form state say beats gloss" class="item" data-marble-id="i-corpus" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t2" data-form="abstract" data-when="later" data-term="sp27" style="left:300px;top:137px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-corpus-h" data-marble-editable>Representation Corpus</h3>
    <p class="gloss" data-marble-id="i-corpus-g" data-marble-editable>Widen what a person can draw from</p>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-corpus-s" data-marble-editable>A library of ways an interface could be, assembled to widen what a person can envision. The raw material for imagination when a blank prompt is the bottleneck.</p>
    <div class="claim" data-mark="hunch" data-marble-id="p1szfbrn"><h4 data-marble-editable data-ph="The claim, stated flat" data-marble-id="5kma1b33">A tool in the background</h4><p data-marble-editable data-ph="What it rules in or out" data-marble-id="m2yojp8m"></p></div>
    <p class="meta" data-marble-id="io0svu9d"><span class="k" data-marble-id="ic2qw639">venue</span><span data-marble-id="i-corpus-v" data-marble-editable></span></p>
    <ul class="beats" data-marble-id="i-corpus-b" data-marble-sortable="beats">
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-corpus-b1" data-mark="cold" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-corpus-b1-t" data-marble-editable>What is a representation, concretely, as a corpus entry?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-corpus-b2" data-mark="cold" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-corpus-b2-t" data-marble-editable>Corpus versus grammar — enumerate examples, or capture the rules that generate them?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-corpus-b3" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-corpus-b3-t" data-marble-editable>Scope what a corpus entry is</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-corpus-b4" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-corpus-b4-t" data-marble-editable>https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.11503v1</p></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="addlink" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-beat" data-marble-into="prev">+ question</button>
  </article>
  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-envis" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t2" data-form="rough" style="left:300px;top:481px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-envis-h" data-marble-editable>Envisioning capability</h3>
    <p class="gloss" data-marble-id="i-envis-g" data-marble-editable>Model what a user can and cannot picture creating</p>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-envis-s" data-marble-editable>Model not just what a user needs or prefers but what they can and cannot picture creating.</p>
    <ul class="beats" data-marble-id="i-envis-b" data-marble-sortable="beats">
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-envis-b1" data-mark="hot" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-envis-b1-t" data-marble-editable>Is GenUI fundamentally about envisioning capability — modelling what a user can and cannot imagine creating?</p></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="addlink" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-beat" data-marble-into="prev">+ question</button>
  </article>


  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-ifori" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t1" data-form="abstract" style="left:24px;top:595px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-ifori-h" data-marble-editable>I for an I</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-ifori-s" data-marble-editable>Generating User Interfaces to Adaptively Visualize Innerworkings of a Generated User Interface - we've seen ppl make codebase visualizations, what else is out there though? What kinds of things do we need to visualize for users?</p>
  </article>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-widgets" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t1" data-form="rough" style="left:24px;top:1032px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-widgets-h" data-marble-editable>Permeating Widgets</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-widgets-s" data-marble-editable>Designing UI widgets that are made to attach and detach across different apps.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-multi" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t1" data-form="rough" style="left:24px;top:785px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-multi-h" data-marble-editable>Multi-modality in generative UI?</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-multi-s" data-marble-editable>Multi-modality as an elicitation channel.</p>
    <ul class="beats" data-marble-id="i-multi-b" data-marble-sortable="beats">
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-multi-b1" data-mark="cold" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-multi-b1-t" data-marble-editable>Is GenUI about multi-modality? Where does modality sit — is it an elicitation channel, an output space, or a red herring?</p></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="addlink" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-beat" data-marble-into="prev">+ question</button>
  </article>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-traces" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t1" data-form="rough" style="left:24px;top:939px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-traces-h" data-marble-editable>Visualizing the Traces of GenUI Changes</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-traces-s" data-marble-editable>How do we trace GenUI changes? — We can visualize, but would visualizations get in the way of the changes...? Not very informative? Comparisons of before and after? But that's only if a specific component changed... Maybe the approach is to not only make a change, but implement customization features that help the user go from one change to another and then demonstrate how to make that custmoization to go back..??? That's just dynavis though...

... or maybe it's some special kind of visalization... let's just keep this in the backburner!</p>
    <ul class="beats" data-marble-id="i-traces-b" data-marble-sortable="beats">
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-traces-b1" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-traces-b1-t" data-marble-editable>What about GenUI is different that you can't use previous methods?</p></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="addlink" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-beat" data-marble-into="prev">+ question</button>
  </article>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-framework" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t1" data-form="rough" style="left:24px;top:502px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-framework-h" data-marble-editable>A general design framework for GenUI</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-framework-s" data-marble-editable>How do you encode/instruct GenUI when you have a broad design approach for it? What is this broader, general design framework?</p>
  </article>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-agents" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t1 t2" data-form="rough" style="left:24px;top:425px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-agents-h" data-marble-editable>Malleability Agents</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-agents-s" data-marble-editable>people don't customize because it's hard and it gets in the way of their task at hand... But computer use agents don't have the cognitive overhead and can complete the task as well as customize the UI....... but why would the computer use agent even want to customize the UI while it's doing stuff? hmmmmm... maybe it's for the user...

Malleability Agents....??? Terraforming Agents..??? How do we properly blend computer use? Maybe we have this agent that bases its decisions entirely under two choices: (1) Navigate to Complete the Task or (2) Create the Code that Completes the Task....???

Like Video Editing task. I guess you have to just complete the task. But what if the agent could change the editing interface to be a personalized UI for editing that specific video? That's pretty hard to imagine right?</p>
  </article>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-course" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t2" data-form="rough" style="left:300px;top:327px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-course-h" data-marble-editable>Course for Vibe Coding Software Tools</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-course-s" data-marble-editable>How do we teach students to build their own software tools with AI?</p>
  </article>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-bloom" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t2" data-form="rough" style="left:300px;top:250px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-bloom-h" data-marble-editable>Bloom Taxonomy</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-bloom-s" data-marble-editable>Using bloom taxonomy to categorize types of GenUI users and their capabilities</p>
  </article>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-creativity" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t2" data-form="rough" style="left:300px;top:404px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-creativity-h" data-marble-editable>Creativity Model</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-creativity-s" data-marble-editable>A creativity graph of the user — what they have made, what is adjacent, what is a reach — or what about like a "Creativity Model" -- dataset for making creative connections in generating new representations or new ways to see some kind of information... information &lt;-&gt; [this mapping is contribution] &lt;-&gt; representation</p>
  </article>

  <article data-parts="form state gloss say meta beats claim" class="item" data-marble-id="i-trans" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t3" data-form="abstract" data-when="later" data-term="su27" style="left:576px;top:234px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-trans-h" data-marble-editable>Collaboration in Personal UX</h3>
    <p class="gloss" data-marble-id="i-trans-g" data-marble-editable>Make one person's UX legible to another</p>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-trans-s" data-marble-editable>Collaboration across personal UX models: making one person's experience of an interface legible to another, so a shared interface is a real negotiation rather than a lowest common denominator.</p>
    <div class="claim" data-mark="hunch" data-marble-id="wsm0pmso"><h4 data-marble-editable data-ph="The claim, stated flat" data-marble-id="hs8l0y4d">PUX Translator..??</h4><p data-marble-editable data-ph="What it rules in or out" data-marble-id="q48rohvh">Not sure how we're going to build it or whether this even is a problem that a single prompt can't fix honestly.</p></div>
    <p class="meta" data-marble-id="z7rlf9ei"><span class="k" data-marble-id="uc65302i">venue</span><span data-marble-id="i-trans-v" data-marble-editable>CHI / CSCW</span></p>
    <ul class="beats" data-marble-id="i-trans-b" data-marble-sortable="beats">
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-trans-b1" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-trans-b1-t" data-marble-editable>What is lost in translation between two people's UX?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-trans-b2" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-trans-b2-t" data-marble-editable>Is a shared interface a compromise, or a third thing neither person would have made alone?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-trans-b3" data-mark="warm" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-trans-b3-t" data-marble-editable>Can you collaborate through personal user experiences? What exactly is a UX Translator — a diff, a mediator, a shared third model?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-trans-b4" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-trans-b4-t" data-marble-editable>Personal UX will introduce misaligned UX across different people. How do we foster collaboration while retaining Personal UX? Make one person's interface legible to another? Translation? Synchronization?</p></li>
      <li class="beat" data-marble-id="i-trans-b5" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-id="i-trans-b5-t" data-marble-editable>The CSCW framing</p></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="addlink" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-beat" data-marble-into="prev">+ question</button>
  </article>
  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-pux-methods" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t3" data-form="rough" style="left:576px;top:424px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-pux-methods-h" data-marble-editable>Designing for a personal user experience</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-pux-methods-s" data-marble-editable>Methods, not just artifacts.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-pds" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t3" data-form="rough" style="left:576px;top:347px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-pds-h" data-marble-editable>Personal Design Systems?</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-pds-s" data-marble-editable>Do people have their own preferred systems for user journeys? Like beyond UI Style, but workflow.. information density, personal user journey... how do we observe this and build this?</p>
  </article>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-workflows" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t3" data-form="rough" style="left:576px;top:610px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-workflows-h" data-marble-editable>Learning new workflows from a database of PUX Models</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-workflows-s" data-marble-editable>It's always hard to teach users new ways of working more productively or creatively. So far our only approaches are recommending predefined ideal workflows and fostering an active social community for that workflow. But neither of them can support the diverse workflows that we've seen with GenUI. Can we leverage a diverse database of PUX Models to assist users in learning new workflows that are personalized to their task, but introduce people to new and better ways of working?</p>
  </article>

  <article class="item" data-marble-id="i-pux-viz" data-kind="idea" data-thesis="t3" data-form="rough" style="left:576px;top:517px" data-marble-removable>
    <button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button>
    <button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button>
    <h3 data-marble-id="i-pux-viz-h" data-marble-editable>Visualizing differences in Personal User Experiences</h3>
    <p class="say" data-marble-id="i-pux-viz-s" data-marble-editable>Visualizing the similarities and differences in people's Personal User Experiences.</p>
  </article>





  <article class="item" data-marble-id="ln-1" data-kind="link" style="left:168px;top:1119px" data-marble-removable>
    <h3 data-marble-id="ln-1-h" data-marble-editable>→ both lean on a model of the person</h3>
  </article>
  <article class="item" data-marble-id="ln-2" data-kind="link" style="left:444px;top:1119px" data-marble-removable>
    <h3 data-marble-id="ln-2-h" data-marble-editable>→ corpus feeds the design space</h3>
  </article>
  <article class="item" data-marble-id="ln-3" data-kind="link" style="left:720px;top:1119px" data-marble-removable>
    <h3 data-marble-id="ln-3-h" data-marble-editable>→ elicitation writes the personal model</h3>
  </article>
  <article data-parts="form state beats say gloss" data-now="4" class="item" data-kind="idea" data-form="abstract" style="left:24px;top:1399px" data-marble-removable data-thesis="t1" data-marble-id="1u95e5k2"><h3 data-marble-editable data-ph="The idea, named" data-marble-id="pjk3nwtj">GenUI Design - Position Paper to arXiv</h3><p class="gloss" data-marble-editable data-ph="One-line summary" data-marble-id="brfdtjoa">I want to say that one avenue for HCI is to actively push towards GenUI Design. List out the key stakeholders for GenUI use for what kind of level of GenUI.</p><p class="say" data-marble-editable data-ph="What it is, in a sentence or two" data-marble-id="58731r3d">I want to say that one avenue for HCI is to actively push towards GenUI Design. List out the key stakeholders for GenUI use for what kind of level of GenUI.</p><ul class="beats" data-marble-sortable="beats" data-marble-id="9xuqq5iz"><li class="beat" data-marble-removable data-marble-id="etce2nft"><p data-marble-editable data-ph="An open question" data-marble-id="sok5hv5n"></p></li></ul></article>
</div>

  <div class="framings" data-marble-id="framings">  <div class="vhead">
    <span class="kicker">01½ — The vision, visualized</span>
    <h2 data-marble-id="vg-h" data-marble-editable>Ten ways to draw the nesting</h2>
    <p class="sub" data-marble-id="vg-s" data-marble-editable>One structure — an affordance inside one interface inside one person's experience inside a group inside the economy — drawn ten ways, borrowing from ten unrelated fields. Every framing marks the interface as ① (the PhD), the person and the group as ③, and shows ② — learning to wield it — as the move between two scopes rather than a scope of its own. Flip with the version switch; the framings you set aside stay in the file.</p>
  </div>

  <svg class="viz-defs" data-marble-id="viz-defs" aria-hidden="true">
    <defs>
      <marker id="viz-ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8.5" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path class="mk" d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z"/></marker>
      <marker id="viz-ah-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8.5" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path class="mk-acc" d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z"/></marker>
    </defs>
  </svg>

  <marble-alt class="vizgallery" data-marble-id="vg-alt" data-marble-active="v2">

    <figure class="viz" data-marble-alt="v1" data-marble-id="viz-1">
      <h4><span class="n">01</span><span data-marble-id="viz-1-h" data-marble-editable>Matryoshka — each shell opens onto the next</span></h4>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 960 470" class="viz-svg" role="img" aria-label="Five nested dolls on one baseline: a solid widget doll inside an interface doll inside a person doll inside a group doll inside the economy doll, with a dotted opening seam across the four outer shells.">
        <text x="60" y="30" font-size="10" class="d-kick">ZOOM OUT — EACH SHELL OPENS ONTO THE NEXT</text>
        <path class="s-box" d="M270 430 L270 210 C270 44 690 44 690 210 L690 430 Z"/>
        <path class="s-box" d="M315 430 L315 244 C315 100 645 100 645 244 L645 430 Z"/>
        <path class="s-box" d="M358 430 L358 274 C358 150 602 150 602 274 L602 430 Z"/>
        <path class="s-acc" d="M398 430 L398 302 C398 206 562 206 562 302 L562 430 Z"/>
        <path class="s-box" d="M438 430 L438 332 C438 278 522 278 522 332 L522 430 Z"/>
        <line class="s-dash" x1="320" y1="168" x2="640" y2="168"/>
        <line class="s-dash" x1="366" y1="220" x2="594" y2="220"/>
        <line class="s-dash" x1="408" y1="268" x2="552" y2="268"/>
        <line class="s-dash" x1="446" y1="320" x2="514" y2="320"/>
        <text x="480" y="92" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" class="d-serif">society &amp; economy of software</text>
        <text x="480" y="132" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" class="d-serif">a group working together — ③</text>
        <text x="480" y="180" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" class="d-serif">one person's experience — ③</text>
        <text x="480" y="242" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" class="d-serif">one interface, designed as a space — ①</text>
        <text x="480" y="258" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" class="d-acc">the PhD</text>
        <text x="480" y="362" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" class="d-serif">a widget</text>
        <text x="480" y="392" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" class="d-faint">solid — the base case, it does not open</text>
        <path class="s-accline" d="M704 250 C 664 264 632 282 602 300" marker-end="url(#viz-ah-acc)"/>
        <text x="712" y="244" font-size="10" class="d-acc">② learning —</text>
        <text x="712" y="258" font-size="10" class="d-acc">opening a shell</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption data-marble-id="viz-1-c" data-marble-editable>Five dolls on one baseline, drawn along their opening seams. Each shell you twist apart hands you a smaller whole world: the group inside the economy, the person inside the group, the interface inside the person. The widget is the one doll that is solid — the base case, nothing nested inside it. ② is the act of opening: learning to wield the interface is prying one scope apart to reach the next.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="viz" data-marble-alt="v2" data-marble-id="viz-2">
      <h4><span class="n">02</span><span data-marble-id="viz-2-h" data-marble-editable>Tree rings — read the age from the centre out</span></h4>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 960 470" class="viz-svg" role="img" aria-label="A cross-cut log: a pith dot at the centre for the widget, then concentric growth rings for interface, person, group and economy, with one radial crack running from pith to bark.">
        <text x="60" y="30" font-size="10" class="d-kick">A CUT LOG — ONE RING PER SCOPE</text>
        <circle class="d-fill" cx="470" cy="248" r="212"/>
        <circle class="s-line" cx="470" cy="248" r="208" style="stroke-width:3"/>
        <circle class="s-line" cx="470" cy="248" r="168"/>
        <circle class="s-line" cx="470" cy="248" r="126"/>
        <circle class="s-accline" cx="470" cy="248" r="82" style="stroke-width:5"/>
        <circle class="s-line" cx="470" cy="248" r="44"/>
        <circle class="s-box" cx="470" cy="248" r="13"/>
        <path class="s-accline" d="M478 238 L516 214 L556 232 L602 206 L648 226 L676 208" marker-end="url(#viz-ah-acc)"/>
        <text x="470" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" class="d-serif">bark — society &amp; economy</text>
        <text x="470" y="106" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" class="d-serif">a group — ③</text>
        <text x="470" y="148" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" class="d-serif">one person's experience — ③</text>
        <text x="470" y="190" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" class="d-serif">one interface — ①</text>
        <text x="470" y="205" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" class="d-acc">the widest, darkest ring — the PhD</text>
        <text x="470" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" class="d-serif">pith</text>
        <text x="470" y="286" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" class="d-faint">the pith is a widget — the oldest concern</text>
        <text x="694" y="196" font-size="10" class="d-acc">② the check —</text>
        <text x="694" y="210" font-size="10" class="d-acc">pith to bark</text>
        <text x="60" y="452" font-size="10" class="d-faint">ring width = attention spent · the ① ring is the fat one</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption data-marble-id="viz-2-c" data-marble-editable>Saw the program across and count the rings. The pith at the dead centre is a single widget — the oldest thing in the trunk, laid down first. Every ring out is a wider scope, and ring width is attention: the ① ring, one interface designed as a space, is the fat dark band the PhD spends its years on. ② is the radial check that opens as the wood dries — the one line that runs from pith straight out to bark, the path a learner takes from the centre.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="viz" data-marble-alt="v3" data-marble-id="viz-3">
      <h4><span class="n">03</span><span data-marble-id="viz-3-h" data-marble-editable>S-expression — the nesting written as one line of code</span></h4>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 960 300" class="viz-svg" role="img" aria-label="The five scopes written as nested Lisp parentheses: open-economy, open-group, open-person, open-interface, open-widget, then five closing parens. A caret under the interface paren points outward, labelled learning.">
        <text x="60" y="34" font-size="10" class="d-kick">THE DOCUMENT IS ITS OWN SOURCE — SO IS THIS PICTURE</text>
        <g font-size="10" class="d-faint" text-anchor="middle">
          <text x="150" y="96">society &amp; economy</text>
          <text x="285" y="96">a group · ③</text>
          <text x="390" y="96">one person · ③</text>
          <text x="512" y="96" class="d-acc">designed as a space · ①  ·  the PhD</text>
          <text x="676" y="96">an affordance</text>
        </g>
        <g class="s-line">
          <path d="M150 104 V132"/><path d="M285 104 V132"/><path d="M390 104 V132"/><path d="M512 104 V132"/><path d="M676 104 V132"/>
        </g>
        <text x="128" y="178" font-size="25" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace" xml:space="preserve"><tspan>(economy (group (person </tspan><tspan class="d-acc" font-weight="700">(interface </tspan><tspan>(widget</tspan><tspan class="d-acc" font-weight="700">)</tspan><tspan>))))</tspan></text>
        <text x="500" y="214" font-size="22" class="d-acc" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace">^</text>
        <path class="s-accline" d="M496 224 C 452 236 424 236 396 236" marker-end="url(#viz-ah-acc)"/>
        <text x="150" y="240" font-size="11" class="d-acc">② learning to wield it — the caret climbs one scope outward</text>
        <text x="128" y="276" font-size="10" class="d-faint">the widget is the only leaf — every paren above it is a container</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption data-marble-id="viz-3-c" data-marble-editable>The whole structure is one balanced expression: (economy (group (person (interface (widget))))). Depth is containment; each paren you step inside is a narrower scope, and the widget is the only atom — everything above it is a list holding another list. The (interface …) pair is bold: that is the ① scope, the PhD. ② is the caret, and it points left — outward — because getting better at the interface means moving up a level, not down.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="viz" data-marble-alt="v4" data-marble-id="viz-4">
      <h4><span class="n">04</span><span data-marble-id="viz-4-h" data-marble-editable>Droste — a screen, on a screen, on a screen</span></h4>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 960 470" class="viz-svg" role="img" aria-label="Five devices drawn one inside the next like a picture within a picture: the outer economy device, then group, then person, then a sharp interface device, then a small widget device, with a gaze arrow diving inward.">
        <text x="60" y="30" font-size="10" class="d-kick">A SCREEN, ON A SCREEN, ON A SCREEN</text>
        <rect class="s-dash" x="150" y="40" width="660" height="412" rx="20"/>
        <rect class="s-line" x="150" y="40" width="16" height="412" fill="none"/>
        <rect class="s-dash" x="212" y="80" width="536" height="332" rx="18"/>
        <rect class="s-dash" x="274" y="122" width="412" height="250" rx="16"/>
        <rect class="s-acc" x="338" y="166" width="284" height="162" rx="14"/>
        <rect class="s-box" x="402" y="206" width="156" height="82" rx="10"/>
        <rect class="s-line" x="470" y="212" width="20" height="5" rx="2"/>
        <text x="170" y="64" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif">a hand holds a phone — society &amp; economy</text>
        <text x="230" y="104" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif">on it, a group — ③ collaboration</text>
        <text x="292" y="146" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif">on it, one person — ③ personal UX</text>
        <text x="356" y="190" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif">one interface, in focus — ①</text>
        <text x="356" y="205" font-size="9" class="d-acc">the PhD</text>
        <text x="480" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif">a widget</text>
        <path class="s-accline" d="M760 96 C 686 150 660 190 590 240" marker-end="url(#viz-ah-acc)"/>
        <text x="700" y="86" font-size="10" class="d-acc">② the gaze,</text>
        <text x="700" y="100" font-size="10" class="d-acc">one screen deeper</text>
        <text x="480" y="438" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" class="d-faint">each screen holds a whole person again</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption data-marble-id="viz-4-c" data-marble-editable>A hand holds a device; on its screen, a person holds a device; on that screen, again — five frames down. The picture-within-a-picture does the nesting literally: every scope is a screen, and every screen contains the next. The ① frame is the one in sharp focus, the interface designed as a space. ② is the line of sight diving one screen inward — and note it goes <em>in</em> for a "zoom out", the point being that every scope, looked into, contains a whole person again.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="viz" data-marble-alt="v5" data-marble-id="viz-5">
      <h4><span class="n">05</span><span data-marble-id="viz-5-h" data-marble-editable>Eukaryotic cell — nucleus to organism</span></h4>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 960 470" class="viz-svg" role="img" aria-label="A cell diagram: a ribosome dot for the widget, a nucleus for the interface, cytoplasm for the person, a ring of adjacent cells for the group, and a surrounding wash for the economy, with a vesicle crossing the nuclear membrane.">
        <text x="60" y="30" font-size="10" class="d-kick">ONE CELL — NUCLEUS TO ORGANISM</text>
        <rect class="d-fill" x="0" y="44" width="960" height="410"/>
        <text x="80" y="66" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif">bloodstream — society &amp; economy of software</text>
        <g class="s-line" fill="none">
          <circle cx="146" cy="150" r="120"/><circle cx="816" cy="150" r="120"/>
          <circle cx="150" cy="352" r="118"/><circle cx="812" cy="348" r="118"/>
        </g>
        <text x="120" y="250" font-size="10" class="d-mut">adjacent cells — ③ collaboration</text>
        <ellipse class="s-line" cx="470" cy="256" rx="300" ry="188"/>
        <ellipse class="s-line" cx="470" cy="256" rx="293" ry="181"/>
        <text x="470" y="110" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" class="d-serif">cell membrane — one person · ③ personal UX</text>
        <ellipse class="s-acc" cx="470" cy="262" rx="146" ry="112"/>
        <text x="470" y="240" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" class="d-serif">nucleus — one interface</text>
        <text x="470" y="256" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" class="d-serif">designed as a space — ①</text>
        <text x="470" y="272" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" class="d-acc">the PhD</text>
        <g class="s-accline" fill="none">
          <path d="M410 300 q 30 18 60 0 t 60 0"/>
          <path d="M410 318 q 30 16 60 0 t 60 0"/>
        </g>
        <circle class="s-box" cx="352" cy="196" r="8"/>
        <text x="352" y="180" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" class="d-mut">a ribosome — one widget</text>
        <circle class="s-acc" cx="470" cy="150" r="11"/>
        <path class="s-dash" d="M470 96 V206" marker-end="url(#viz-ah-acc)"/>
        <text x="486" y="126" font-size="10" class="d-acc">② a vesicle across the membrane — learning</text>
        <ellipse class="s-line" cx="300" cy="360" rx="34" ry="20"/><text x="300" y="364" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" class="d-mut">Elicitive UIs</text>
        <ellipse class="s-line" cx="648" cy="352" rx="44" ry="20"/><text x="648" y="356" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" class="d-mut">Representation Corpus</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption data-marble-id="viz-5-c" data-marble-editable>The scopes are the anatomy of a single cell. The nucleus is the interface designed as a space — ①, the PhD — its chromatin the design projects. The cytoplasm around it is one person's experience; the membrane is the person; the tissue of identical cells pressing in is the group; the wash beyond is the economy. The widget is a lone ribosome, a machine that does one job. ② is a vesicle crossing the nuclear membrane — the traffic in and out is how a cell learns what to make next.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="viz" data-marble-alt="v6" data-marble-id="viz-6">
      <h4><span class="n">06</span><span data-marble-id="viz-6-h" data-marble-editable>Gravity well — deepest at the centre, a marble at the bottom</span></h4>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 960 430" class="viz-svg" role="img" aria-label="A rubber-sheet funnel drawn as nested ellipse contours descending to a centre, with a marble resting at the bottom and a second marble climbing the right wall on a dashed path.">
        <text x="60" y="30" font-size="10" class="d-kick">A RUBBER SHEET — DEEPEST AT THE CENTRE</text>
        <path class="s-line" d="M92 96 C 150 250 360 340 470 356 C 580 340 790 250 848 96"/>
        <ellipse class="s-line" cx="470" cy="96" rx="378" ry="52"/>
        <ellipse class="s-line" cx="470" cy="158" rx="278" ry="44"/>
        <ellipse class="s-line" cx="470" cy="216" rx="192" ry="36"/>
        <ellipse class="s-accline" cx="470" cy="272" rx="116" ry="26" style="stroke-width:4"/>
        <ellipse class="s-line" cx="470" cy="320" rx="58" ry="16"/>
        <circle class="s-acc" cx="470" cy="350" r="15"/>
        <text x="470" y="386" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" class="d-serif">a marble — one widget</text>
        <text x="858" y="92" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif" text-anchor="end">society &amp; economy — widest, weakest pull</text>
        <text x="756" y="156" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif" text-anchor="end">a group — ③</text>
        <text x="664" y="214" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif" text-anchor="end">one person — ③</text>
        <text x="590" y="270" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif" text-anchor="end">one interface — ①</text>
        <text x="590" y="284" font-size="9" class="d-acc" text-anchor="end">the steep wall — the PhD</text>
        <path class="s-dash" d="M470 350 Q 612 322 668 236" marker-end="url(#viz-ah-acc)"/>
        <circle class="s-acc" cx="668" cy="236" r="10"/>
        <text x="684" y="232" font-size="10" class="d-acc">② climbing out of the well — learning</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption data-marble-id="viz-6-c" data-marble-editable>Picture the scopes as a single well pressed into a rubber sheet: nested contour rings falling to a point. A marble rests at the bottom — one widget, and the pun is deliberate. The ① ring is the steep, shaded wall the PhD works on. The economy is the shallowest, widest curve: the weakest pull where you stand, the largest reach overall. ② is a marble halfway up the wall on a dashed arc — learning to wield the interface is climbing one scope out against the pull.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="viz" data-marble-alt="v7" data-marble-id="viz-7">
      <h4><span class="n">07</span><span data-marble-id="viz-7-h" data-marble-editable>Cartographic zoom — six magnifications of one place</span></h4>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 960 340" class="viz-svg" role="img" aria-label="Five map tiles in a row from widget scale to economy scale, each with the same crosshair marking the same place, a magnifier over the interface tile, and a zoom arrow running the length of the row.">
        <text x="60" y="28" font-size="10" class="d-kick">SIX MAGNIFICATIONS OF ONE PLACE</text>
        <g class="s-line" fill="none">
          <rect class="s-box" x="45"  y="70" width="150" height="150" rx="6"/>
          <rect class="s-box" x="225" y="70" width="150" height="150" rx="6"/>
          <rect class="s-box" x="405" y="70" width="150" height="150" rx="6"/>
          <rect class="s-box" x="585" y="70" width="150" height="150" rx="6"/>
          <rect class="s-box" x="765" y="70" width="150" height="150" rx="6"/>
        </g>
        <rect class="s-acc" x="225" y="70" width="150" height="150" rx="6"/>
        <g class="s-dash" fill="none">
          <path d="M95 70 V220 M145 70 V220 M45 120 H195 M45 170 H195"/>
          <path d="M275 70 V220 M325 70 V220 M225 120 H375 M225 170 H375"/>
          <path d="M455 70 V220 M505 70 V220 M405 120 H555 M405 170 H555"/>
          <path d="M635 70 V220 M685 70 V220 M585 120 H735 M585 170 H735"/>
          <path d="M815 70 V220 M865 70 V220 M765 120 H915 M765 170 H915"/>
        </g>
        <g class="s-accline" fill="none">
          <path d="M116 141 h8 M120 137 v8"/><path d="M296 141 h8 M300 137 v8"/><path d="M476 141 h8 M480 137 v8"/><path d="M656 141 h8 M660 137 v8"/><path d="M836 141 h8 M840 137 v8"/>
        </g>
        <circle class="s-accline" cx="356" cy="196" r="40" fill="none"/>
        <path class="s-accline" d="M384 224 L410 250"/>
        <text x="300" y="252" font-size="9" class="d-acc" text-anchor="middle">the PhD</text>
        <g font-size="9.5" class="d-mut" text-anchor="middle">
          <text x="120" y="240">1 : 1</text><text x="120" y="256" class="d-serif">a widget</text>
          <text x="300" y="240">≈ 1 : 10²</text><text x="300" y="256" class="d-serif">one interface — ①</text>
          <text x="480" y="240">≈ 1 : 10³</text><text x="480" y="256" class="d-serif">one person — ③</text>
          <text x="660" y="240">≈ 1 : 10⁴</text><text x="660" y="256" class="d-serif">a group — ③</text>
          <text x="840" y="240">≈ 1 : 10⁶</text><text x="840" y="256" class="d-serif">the economy</text>
        </g>
        <path class="s-accline" d="M60 296 H900" marker-end="url(#viz-ah-acc)"/>
        <text x="60" y="288" font-size="10" class="d-faint">zoom in · 1:1</text>
        <text x="900" y="288" font-size="10" class="d-faint" text-anchor="end">② zoom out · the economy — the control that moves between scales</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption data-marble-id="viz-7-c" data-marble-editable>Five map tiles, each the next zoom level of the last, with a scale bar under every one — 1:1 at the widget, roughly a millionth at the economy. The crosshair is in the same spot on every tile: it is one person, one place, seen at six magnifications, not five different things. A loupe sits on the ① tile, the interface, where the PhD reads the fine print. ② is the zoom control itself — the act of stepping between scales.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="viz" data-marble-alt="v8" data-marble-id="viz-8">
      <h4><span class="n">08</span><span data-marble-id="viz-8-h" data-marble-editable>Blueprint section — each floor has to be built</span></h4>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 960 470" class="viz-svg" role="img" aria-label="An architect's section drawing: five stacked floor slabs from an economy foundation up to a widget attic, a depth ruler down the left margin, a detail bubble on the interface floor, and a figure climbing a ladder between two floors.">
        <text x="60" y="30" font-size="10" class="d-kick">AN ARCHITECT'S SECTION — EACH FLOOR IS BUILT</text>
        <line class="s-line" x1="104" y1="96" x2="104" y2="430"/>
        <g class="s-line">
          <path d="M98 112 H110"/><path d="M98 188 H110"/><path d="M98 268 H110"/><path d="M98 336 H110"/><path d="M98 404 H110"/>
        </g>
        <g font-size="9" class="d-faint" text-anchor="end">
          <text x="94" y="116">1</text><text x="94" y="192">2</text><text x="94" y="272">3</text><text x="94" y="340">4</text><text x="94" y="408">5</text>
        </g>
        <rect class="s-box" x="150" y="98"  width="620" height="34" rx="4"/>
        <text x="176" y="120" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif">the widget — one affordance</text>
        <rect class="s-acc" x="150" y="152" width="700" height="72" rx="4"/>
        <g class="s-line"><path d="M170 224 L214 152 M214 224 L258 152 M258 224 L302 152 M302 224 L346 152"/></g>
        <text x="176" y="184" font-size="11" class="d-serif">one interface, designed as a space — ①</text>
        <circle class="s-accline" cx="756" cy="188" r="26" fill="none"/>
        <text x="756" y="185" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5" class="d-acc">see:</text>
        <text x="756" y="196" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5" class="d-acc">the PhD</text>
        <rect class="s-box" x="150" y="244" width="620" height="48" rx="4"/>
        <text x="176" y="273" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif">one person's experience — ③ personal UX</text>
        <rect class="s-box" x="150" y="312" width="620" height="48" rx="4"/>
        <text x="176" y="341" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif">a group working together — ③ collaboration</text>
        <rect class="s-box" x="150" y="380" width="620" height="52" rx="4"/>
        <g class="s-line"><path d="M160 432 L200 380 M210 432 L250 380 M260 432 L300 380 M310 432 L350 380 M360 432 L400 380"/></g>
        <text x="176" y="410" font-size="10.5" class="d-serif">society &amp; economy — the foundation</text>
        <g class="s-accline" fill="none"><path d="M636 292 V224 M660 292 V224 M636 276 H660 M636 258 H660 M636 240 H660"/></g>
        <circle class="s-accline" cx="648" cy="300" r="7" fill="none"/>
        <path class="s-accline" d="M648 307 V322 M640 314 H656"/>
        <text x="684" y="266" font-size="10" class="d-acc">② climbing</text>
        <text x="684" y="280" font-size="10" class="d-acc">between floors —</text>
        <text x="684" y="294" font-size="10" class="d-acc">learning</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption data-marble-id="viz-8-c" data-marble-editable>A section cut through the program as a building. Society and economy is the foundation everything rests on; the interface floor — ① — is drawn tall, with a detail bubble calling out the PhD. A depth ruler runs down the left margin. The point the drawing makes that a diagram of rings cannot: every one of these floors is a structure someone has to build and stand on. ② is the figure on the ladder, bridging two floors — that is what learning to wield the interface looks like from inside the building.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="viz" data-marble-alt="v9" data-marble-id="viz-9">
      <h4><span class="n">09</span><span data-marble-id="viz-9-h" data-marble-editable>Filesystem path — containers down to one file</span></h4>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 960 400" class="viz-svg" role="img" aria-label="A breadcrumb path economy slash group slash person slash interface slash widget dot aff, then the same drawn as an indented directory tree with the interface directory expanded and highlighted, and a cd dot dot prompt walking toward the root.">
        <text x="60" y="30" font-size="10" class="d-kick">A PATH — CONTAINERS ALL THE WAY DOWN TO ONE FILE</text>
        <text x="80" y="66" font-size="15" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace"><tspan class="d-mut">economy</tspan><tspan class="d-faint"> ▸ </tspan><tspan class="d-mut">group</tspan><tspan class="d-faint"> ▸ </tspan><tspan class="d-mut">person</tspan><tspan class="d-faint"> ▸ </tspan><tspan class="d-acc" font-weight="700">interface</tspan><tspan class="d-faint"> ▸ </tspan><tspan>widget.aff</tspan></text>
        <line class="s-line" x1="80" y1="86" x2="900" y2="86"/>
        <path class="s-line" d="M132 128 V276 M132 164 H150 M164 164 H182 M196 200 H214 M228 236 H246 M260 272 H278" fill="none"/>
        <path class="s-line" d="M164 128 V200 M196 128 V236 M228 128 V272" fill="none" opacity="0.55"/>
        <g font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace" font-size="13">
          <text x="138" y="132" class="d-mut">▾ economy/</text>
          <text x="700" y="132" text-anchor="end" class="d-faint" font-size="10">society &amp; the economy of software</text>
          <text x="186" y="168" class="d-mut">▾ group/</text>
          <text x="700" y="168" text-anchor="end" class="d-faint" font-size="10">③ collaboration</text>
          <text x="218" y="204" class="d-mut">▾ person/</text>
          <text x="700" y="204" text-anchor="end" class="d-faint" font-size="10">③ personal UX</text>
          <rect class="s-acc" x="244" y="222" width="360" height="26" rx="4"/>
          <text x="250" y="240" class="d-acc" font-weight="700">▾ interface/</text>
          <text x="700" y="240" text-anchor="end" class="d-acc" font-size="10"># the PhD — ①</text>
          <text x="282" y="276">▪ widget.aff</text>
          <text x="700" y="276" text-anchor="end" class="d-faint" font-size="10">the one file</text>
        </g>
        <path class="s-accline" d="M116 292 V150" marker-end="url(#viz-ah-acc)"/>
        <text x="120" y="326" font-size="13" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace"><tspan class="d-acc">$ cd ..</tspan><tspan class="d-faint"> ▏</tspan></text>
        <text x="220" y="326" font-size="10" class="d-acc">② climbing toward the root — learning</text>
        <text x="120" y="366" font-size="10" class="d-faint">every level is a directory — a container with no content of its own — until the leaf</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption data-marble-id="viz-9-c" data-marble-editable>The nesting is a file path: economy/group/person/interface/widget.aff, and below it the same thing as a directory tree. Every segment is a folder — a container that holds nothing but the next container — until the very bottom, where widget.aff is the only actual file. The interface/ directory is expanded and highlighted, tagged as the PhD. ② is a cd .. at the prompt: learning to wield the interface is walking one level back toward the root.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="viz" data-marble-alt="v10" data-marble-id="viz-10">
      <h4><span class="n">10</span><span data-marble-id="viz-10-h" data-marble-editable>Onion — a halved bulb, alive and storing energy</span></h4>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 960 470" class="viz-svg" role="img" aria-label="A botanical cross-section of a halved bulb: nested pointed scales from a papery outer skin down to a basal plate, roots below, and a green shoot rising up the central axis and out the top.">
        <text x="60" y="30" font-size="10" class="d-kick">A HALVED BULB — ALIVE, STORING ENERGY TO GROW</text>
        <path class="s-dash" d="M230 424 C 230 250 300 96 480 82 C 660 96 730 250 730 424 Z"/>
        <path class="s-line" d="M286 424 C 286 264 342 132 480 120 C 618 132 674 264 674 424 Z"/>
        <path class="s-line" d="M338 424 C 338 280 384 168 480 158 C 576 168 622 280 622 424 Z"/>
        <path class="s-acc" d="M388 424 C 388 300 420 210 480 202 C 540 210 572 300 572 424 Z"/>
        <path class="s-box" d="M432 424 C 432 336 452 278 480 274 C 508 278 528 336 528 424 Z"/>
        <g class="s-line"><path d="M404 396 L444 320 M420 400 L458 328 M436 404 L470 340"/></g>
        <ellipse class="s-box" cx="480" cy="424" rx="58" ry="10"/>
        <g class="s-line"><path d="M456 434 C 452 448 448 456 440 464 M480 434 V466 M504 434 C 508 448 512 456 520 464"/></g>
        <path class="s-accline" d="M480 424 C 470 300 490 170 480 52" style="stroke-width:3"/>
        <path class="s-accline" d="M480 70 C 500 56 520 58 528 40 C 508 42 490 50 480 66" style="stroke-width:1.5"/>
        <g class="d-serif" font-size="10.5" font-style="italic" text-anchor="end">
          <text x="196" y="120">a — society &amp; economy</text>
          <text x="196" y="176">b — a group · ③</text>
          <text x="196" y="240">c — one person · ③</text>
          <text x="196" y="304">d — one interface · ①  (the PhD)</text>
          <text x="196" y="360">e — a widget</text>
        </g>
        <g class="s-line"><path d="M200 116 H262 M200 172 H302 M200 236 H352 M200 300 H400 M200 356 H452"/></g>
        <text x="700" y="150" font-size="10" class="d-acc">② a shoot rising</text>
        <text x="700" y="164" font-size="10" class="d-acc">through the layers —</text>
        <text x="700" y="178" font-size="10" class="d-acc">learning, toward light</text>
        <text x="480" y="456" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" class="d-faint">basal plate &amp; roots — access · incentives</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption data-marble-id="viz-10-c" data-marble-editable>A botanical plate of a bulb sliced in half: tight concentric scales from the papery outer skin — society and the economy — down to the basal plate, a single widget, with roots of access and incentive below. Scale d, the interface designed as a space, is cross-hatched and labelled as the PhD. The difference from a stack of rings: a bulb is alive and holding energy to grow. ② is the pale shoot running straight up the central axis and out the top — a learner breaking through the layers toward light.</figcaption>
    </figure>

  </marble-alt></div>
</div>

</main>

<template data-marble-transient id="tpl-thesis">
  <article class="item" data-kind="thesis" data-form="abstract" style="left:44px;top:44px" data-marble-removable><h3 data-marble-editable data-ph="Thesis · the claim in one line"></h3><p class="gloss" data-marble-editable data-ph="Short label"></p><p class="say" data-marble-editable data-ph="Why it is a different problem, in a sentence or two"></p></article>
</template>
<template data-marble-transient id="tpl-item">
  <article class="item" data-kind="idea" data-form="rough" style="left:44px;top:44px" data-marble-removable><button class="chip form" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-form" data-ring="form"></button><button class="chip pub" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="state"></button><h3 data-marble-editable data-ph="The idea, named"></h3><p class="gloss" data-marble-editable data-ph="One-line summary"></p><p class="say" data-marble-editable data-ph="What it is, in a sentence or two"></p><ul class="beats" data-marble-sortable="beats"><li class="beat" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-editable data-ph="An open question"></p></li></ul><button class="addlink" data-marble-transient data-marble-add="#tpl-beat" data-marble-into="prev">+ question</button></article>
</template>
<template data-marble-transient id="tpl-beat">
  <li class="beat" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-editable data-ph="An open question"></p></li>
</template>
<template data-marble-transient id="tpl-claim">
  <div class="claim" data-mark="hunch"><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="maturity"></button><h4 data-marble-editable data-ph="The claim, stated flat"></h4><p data-marble-editable data-ph="What it rules in or out"></p></div>
</template>
<template data-marble-transient id="tpl-gloss">
  <p class="gloss" data-marble-editable data-ph="One-line summary"></p>
</template>
<template data-marble-transient id="tpl-say">
  <p class="say" data-marble-editable data-ph="What it is, in a sentence or two"></p>
</template>
<template data-marble-transient id="tpl-meta">
  <p class="meta"><span class="k">venue</span><span data-marble-editable data-ph="—"></span></p>
</template>
<template data-marble-transient id="tpl-beats">
  <ul class="beats" data-marble-sortable="beats"><li class="beat" data-marble-removable><button class="chip" data-marble-transient data-cycle="data-mark" data-ring="heat"></button><p data-marble-editable data-ph="An open question"></p></li></ul>
</template>

<script>
/* The document's own behaviour. It talks only to window.marble, never a
   server, so it runs unchanged under any host and does nothing at all under
   none: with no carrier the page is still readable and the views still
   switch, nothing is remembered.

   Every fact stays in the markup. How the pool is laid out is data-arrange on
   .stage; how much of each card you read is data-show. A card's form is
   data-form on the card. Which preset is lit is derived from those two
   attributes into a marble- class and re-derived after every reconcile, which
   is exactly when a derived class has just been lost. */
(() => {
  const stage = document.querySelector('[data-marble-id="stage"]');
  const pool = document.querySelector('[data-marble-id="pool"]');
  const band = document.querySelector('[data-marble-id="band"]');
  if (!stage || !pool) return;

  const presets = [...document.querySelectorAll('[data-preset]')];
  const dials = [...document.querySelectorAll('[data-set]')];
  const wired = new WeakSet();

  // A preset is a name for a pair of values, not a seventh thing to store.
  const PRESETS = {
    spine:     { arrange: 'thesis', show: 'all' },
    board:     { arrange: 'form',   show: 'all' },
    timeline:  { arrange: 'term',   show: 'all' },
    now:       { arrange: 'when',   show: 'all' },
    map:       { arrange: 'map',    show: 'all' },
    claims:    { arrange: 'thesis', show: 'claims' },
    questions: { arrange: 'thesis', show: 'questions' },
    framings:  { arrange: 'framings', show: 'all' },
  };

  const RINGS = {
    form: ['rough', 'abstract', 'concrete', 'complete'],
    state: ['seed', 'active', 'drafting', 'submitted'],
    maturity: ['hunch', 'forming', 'firm'],
    heat: ['cold', 'warm', 'hot'],
    plan: ['someday', 'planned', 'active', 'done'],
  };

  // The arrangements whose columns mean something, so a drop in one is a
  // change of value rather than only a change of order.
  const LANED = {
    thesis: 'data-thesis',
    form: 'data-form',
    when: 'data-when',
    term: 'data-term',
  };

  // One writer: the page moves and the file hears it in the same breath, or
  // neither happens. With no carrier only the first half runs.
  function write(el, name, value) {
    const was = el.getAttribute(name);
    if (was === value) return;
    if (value === null) el.removeAttribute(name);
    else el.setAttribute(name, value);
    const id = window.marble && window.marble.id(el);
    if (!id) return;
    window.marble.op({ type: 'setAttr', id, name, value }, { immediate: true });
  }

  // Same, plus a recorded inverse — for the gestures a person would expect
  // Mod+Z to undo, which is every gesture that moves a card.
  function writeUndoable(el, name, value) {
    const was = el.getAttribute(name);
    if (was === value) return;
    const id = window.marble && window.marble.id(el);
    // Clearing has to be spelled the same way here as in write above, or a
    // card dropped in an unsorted column ends up holding the word "null".
    if (value === null) el.removeAttribute(name);
    else el.setAttribute(name, value);
    if (!id) return;
    const op = { type: 'setAttr', id, name, value };
    window.marble.record({ redo: [op], undo: [{ type: 'setAttr', id, name, value: was }] });
    window.marble.op(op, { immediate: true });
  }

  /* Switching a view is the one moment where it is worth saying out loud that
     these are the same cards: they travel to their new places instead of the
     screen being replaced. The names are assigned for the length of the
     animation and taken off again, so nothing page-only ever reaches an op. */
  function transition(change) {
    const reduced = matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
    if (reduced || !document.startViewTransition) return change();
    const moving = [...pool.children, ...document.querySelectorAll('[data-marble-id="band"] > *')];
    for (const el of moving) {
      const id = window.marble && window.marble.id(el);
      if (id) el.style.viewTransitionName = 'i' + id.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9]/g, '');
    }
    const run = document.startViewTransition(change);
    run.finished.finally(() => {
      for (const el of moving) el.style.viewTransitionName = '';
    });
  }

  function setView(arrange, show, focus) {
    transition(() => {
      if (arrange) write(stage, 'data-arrange', arrange);
      /* The map is the one arrangement whose entire content is where each
         card is, so it opens ready to be rearranged instead of asking to be
         switched on first — a view whose only gesture is off by default is a
         view that looks broken. Leaving it hands the cards back to typing. */
      if (arrange === 'map') write(pool, 'data-marble-canvas', '');
      else if (arrange) write(pool, 'data-marble-canvas', 'off');
      if (show) {
        write(stage, 'data-show', show);
        write(pool, 'data-show', show);
      }
      if (focus) write(stage, 'data-focus', focus);
      // The arrange button alone (not the thesis picker) has no thesis to
      // name, so it opens on the first one rather than an empty list.
      else if (arrange === 'focus' && !stage.hasAttribute('data-focus')) write(stage, 'data-focus', 't1');
      paint();
    });
  }

  // Which card closes a thesis column is derived from DOM order and the same
  // column rule the grid uses — never written down.
  function thesisColumn(el) {
    const tags = (el.getAttribute('data-thesis') || '').split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean);
    if (tags.includes('t3')) return 3;
    if (tags.includes('t2')) return 2;
    if (tags.includes('t1')) return 1;
    return 1;
  }

  /* The spine's own measurements. A floor, because three columns stop being
     readable below it; a ceiling, because the stage now takes the whole site
     and a card left to grow would run a ninety-character line. */
  const SPINE_GUTTER = 12;
  const SPINE_RAIL = 40;
  const SPINE_COL_MIN = 240;
  const SPINE_COL_MAX = 520;

  /* How many columns fit is asked of the pool, never of the window: a docked
     panel narrows this element and leaves innerWidth untouched. */
  function spineColCount(width) {
    if (!width) return 3;
    for (const cols of [3, 2]) {
      const rail = SPINE_RAIL + SPINE_GUTTER;
      if ((width - rail - SPINE_GUTTER * (cols - 1)) / cols >= SPINE_COL_MIN) return cols;
    }
    return 1;
  }

  function spineVisible(el) {
    if (!el.classList.contains('item')) return false;
    if (el.dataset.kind === 'link') return false;
    return getComputedStyle(el).display !== 'none';
  }

  function clearSpinePack() {
    pool.style.removeProperty('--spine-h');
    pool.style.removeProperty('--spine-th');
    pool.classList.remove('marble-measuring');
    for (const card of pool.querySelectorAll(':scope > .item')) {
      card.style.removeProperty('--spine-x');
      card.style.removeProperty('--spine-y');
      card.style.removeProperty('--spine-w');
      card.classList.remove('marble-col-start', 'marble-col-end');
    }
    for (const btn of pool.querySelectorAll(':scope > .col-add')) btn.hidden = true;
  }

  function ensureColAdds() {
    if (pool.querySelector(':scope > .col-add')) return;
    for (const t of ['t1', 't2', 't3']) {
      const btn = document.createElement('button');
      btn.className = 'col-add';
      btn.type = 'button';
      btn.hidden = true;
      btn.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
      btn.setAttribute('data-marble-add', '#tpl-item');
      btn.setAttribute('data-marble-into', '#pool');
      btn.setAttribute('data-set-thesis', t);
      btn.textContent = '+ Idea';
      btn.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Add an idea to thesis ' + t.slice(1));
      pool.append(btn);
      if (window.marble) window.marble.adopt(btn);
    }
    const thesis = document.createElement('button');
    thesis.className = 'col-add col-add-thesis';
    thesis.type = 'button';
    thesis.hidden = true;
    thesis.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
    thesis.setAttribute('data-marble-add', '#tpl-thesis');
    thesis.setAttribute('data-marble-into', '#pool');
    thesis.setAttribute('data-set-thesis', 'auto');
    thesis.textContent = '+ Thesis';
    pool.append(thesis);
    if (window.marble) window.marble.adopt(thesis);
  }

  let packing = false;
  function packSpine() {
    if (stage.getAttribute('data-arrange') !== 'thesis') {
      clearSpinePack();
      return;
    }
    if (packing) return;
    packing = true;
    ensureColAdds();
    const width = pool.clientWidth;
    const cols = spineColCount(width);
    const gutter = SPINE_GUTTER;
    const rail = cols > 1 ? SPINE_RAIL : 0;
    const cards = [...pool.querySelectorAll(':scope > .item')].filter(spineVisible);
    if (!width || !cards.length) {
      packing = false;
      return;
    }
    const colW = Math.min(
      SPINE_COL_MAX,
      (width - gutter * (cols - 1) - (rail ? rail + gutter : 0)) / cols,
    );
    for (const card of cards) card.style.setProperty('--spine-w', colW + 'px');
    pool.classList.add('marble-measuring');
    let head = 0;
    for (const card of cards) {
      if (card.getAttribute('data-kind') === 'thesis') head = Math.max(head, card.offsetHeight);
    }
    pool.classList.remove('marble-measuring');
    const headPx = head + 'px';
    if (pool.style.getPropertyValue('--spine-th') !== headPx) pool.style.setProperty('--spine-th', headPx);
    const heights = Array(cols).fill(0);
    const starts = Array(cols).fill(null);
    const ends = Array(cols).fill(null);
    for (const card of cards) {
      card.classList.remove('marble-col-start', 'marble-col-end');
      let col = thesisColumn(card) - 1;
      if (col >= cols) col = cols - 1;
      const y = heights[col] === 0 ? 0 : heights[col] - 1;
      const x = col * (colW + gutter);
      card.style.setProperty('--spine-x', x + 'px');
      card.style.setProperty('--spine-y', y + 'px');
      heights[col] = y + card.offsetHeight;
      if (!starts[col]) starts[col] = card;
      ends[col] = card;
    }
    for (const card of starts) if (card) card.classList.add('marble-col-start');
    for (const card of ends) if (card) card.classList.add('marble-col-end');

    const addGap = 8;
    const addH = 34;
    for (let i = 0; i < 3; i += 1) {
      const btn = pool.querySelector(':scope > .col-add[data-set-thesis="t' + (i + 1) + '"]');
      if (!btn) continue;
      if (i >= cols) { btn.hidden = true; continue; }
      btn.hidden = false;
      btn.style.left = (i * (colW + gutter)) + 'px';
      btn.style.top = (heights[i] + addGap) + 'px';
      btn.style.width = colW + 'px';
      btn.style.height = '';
      btn.classList.remove('is-rail');
    }
    const thesisBtn = pool.querySelector(':scope > .col-add-thesis');
    if (thesisBtn) {
      thesisBtn.hidden = false;
      if (rail) {
        thesisBtn.classList.add('is-rail');
        thesisBtn.style.left = (cols * (colW + gutter)) + 'px';
        thesisBtn.style.top = '0px';
        thesisBtn.style.width = rail + 'px';
        thesisBtn.style.height = Math.max(head, 88) + 'px';
      } else {
        thesisBtn.classList.remove('is-rail');
        thesisBtn.style.left = '0px';
        thesisBtn.style.top = (heights[0] + addGap + addH + 6) + 'px';
        thesisBtn.style.width = colW + 'px';
        thesisBtn.style.height = '';
      }
    }

    const foot = addGap + addH + (rail ? 0 : addH + 6);
    const maxH = Math.max(0, ...heights) + foot;
    const next = maxH + 'px';
    if (pool.style.getPropertyValue('--spine-h') !== next) pool.style.setProperty('--spine-h', next);
    packing = false;
  }

  const spineItemObs = new ResizeObserver(() => {
    if (packing || stage.getAttribute('data-arrange') !== 'thesis') return;
    packSpine();
  });
  /* The item observer cannot see this one: a card's width is pinned in pixels
     by the pack, so it never changes when the space around it does. Watching
     the pool itself is what notices a panel docking beside the document — and
     the width guard is what keeps the pack's own height write from looping. */
  let spineBoxW = 0;
  const spineBoxObs = new ResizeObserver((entries) => {
    const w = Math.round(entries[entries.length - 1].contentRect.width);
    if (w === spineBoxW) return;
    spineBoxW = w;
    packSpine();
  });
  spineBoxObs.observe(pool);

  const spineWatched = new Set();
  function watchSpineItems() {
    for (const card of pool.querySelectorAll(':scope > .item')) {
      if (spineWatched.has(card)) continue;
      spineWatched.add(card);
      spineItemObs.observe(card);
    }
  }

  function stampThesisStacks() {
    watchSpineItems();
    packSpine();
  }

  const PARTS = [
    { id: 'form',  label: 'Form',      hint: 'rough → complete', sel: ':scope > .chip.form:not(.part-ghost)', off: 'form' },
    { id: 'state', label: 'Status',    hint: 'seed → submitted', sel: ':scope > .chip.pub:not(.part-ghost)', off: 'status' },
    { id: 'gloss', label: 'Short line', hint: 'one-line gloss',  sel: ':scope > .gloss:not(.part-ghost)', tpl: '#tpl-gloss' },
    { id: 'say',   label: 'Pitch',     hint: 'the longer note',  sel: ':scope > .say:not(.part-ghost)',   tpl: '#tpl-say' },
    { id: 'claim', label: 'Claim',     hint: 'quote bar',        sel: ':scope > .claim:not(.part-ghost)', tpl: '#tpl-claim' },
    { id: 'meta',  label: 'Venue',     hint: 'where it is aimed', sel: ':scope > .meta:not(.part-ghost)', tpl: '#tpl-meta' },
    { id: 'beats', label: 'Questions', hint: 'open questions',   sel: ':scope > .beats:not(.part-ghost)', tpl: '#tpl-beats' },
  ];

  /* Which of those a card is offered is a question about what kind of card it
     is, and the answer belongs in one place — the ghost you can click, the
     chip the card wears and the rule that hides it all read it from here. A
     thesis is a claim, not a paper: no form to run through, nothing to submit.
     A target is a date: it has a plan and no form. A link is a remark about
     two cards sitting next to each other and has no fields of its own. */
  const KIND_PARTS = {
    idea:   ['form', 'state', 'gloss', 'say', 'claim', 'meta', 'beats'],
    thesis: ['gloss', 'say', 'claim', 'meta', 'beats'],
    target: ['state', 'gloss', 'say', 'claim', 'meta', 'beats'],
    link:   [],
  };

  function offeredParts(card) {
    const listed = KIND_PARTS[card.getAttribute('data-kind')];
    return listed || KIND_PARTS.idea;
  }

  function offers(card, id) {
    return offeredParts(card).includes(id);
  }

  function mint() {
    return window.marble ? window.marble.newId() : Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 10);
  }

  function stampIds(root) {
    const nodes = [root, ...root.querySelectorAll('*')];
    for (const el of nodes) {
      if (el.hasAttribute('data-marble-transient')) continue;
      if (el.closest('svg')) continue;
      if (!el.getAttribute('data-marble-id')) el.setAttribute('data-marble-id', mint());
    }
  }

  /* A card that has never been through the picker says what it has by having
     it. The two dials are read from the attribute they dial and not from the
     chip, which is transient chrome this script puts there — reading the chip
     would be asking the answer to tell us the question. */
  function inferParts(card) {
    const parts = [];
    if (card.hasAttribute('data-form')) parts.push('form');
    if (card.hasAttribute('data-mark')) parts.push('state');
    if (card.querySelector(':scope > .gloss:not(.part-ghost)')) parts.push('gloss');
    if (card.querySelector(':scope > .say:not(.part-ghost)')) parts.push('say');
    if (card.querySelector(':scope > .claim:not(.part-ghost)')) parts.push('claim');
    if (card.querySelector(':scope > .meta:not(.part-ghost)')) parts.push('meta');
    if (card.querySelector(':scope > .beats:not(.part-ghost)')) parts.push('beats');
    return parts;
  }

  function partsOf(card) {
    const listed = card.getAttribute('data-parts');
    const named = listed != null
      ? listed.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean)
      : inferParts(card);
    return named.filter((id, i) => named.indexOf(id) === i && offers(card, id));
  }

  function hasPart(card, id) {
    return partsOf(card).includes(id);
  }

  /* Named is not the same as present. A card can list a part whose block was
     never inserted — and then the ghost standing in for it read as already on,
     so the first click on it turned it further off and did nothing you could
     see. What is on is what is both asked for and there. */
  function partLive(card, id) {
    if (!hasPart(card, id)) return false;
    const spec = PARTS.find((p) => p.id === id);
    if (!spec || !spec.tpl) return true;
    return !!card.querySelector(spec.sel);
  }

  function injectChip(card, kind) {
    const cls = kind === 'form' ? 'form' : 'pub';
    if (card.querySelector(':scope > .chip.' + cls + ':not(.part-ghost)')) return;
    const ring = kind === 'form' ? 'form'
      : card.getAttribute('data-kind') === 'target' ? 'plan' : 'state';
    const attr = kind === 'form' ? 'data-form' : 'data-mark';
    const btn = document.createElement('button');
    btn.className = 'chip ' + cls;
    btn.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
    btn.setAttribute('data-cycle', attr);
    btn.setAttribute('data-ring', ring);
    btn.type = 'button';
    const h3 = card.querySelector(':scope > h3');
    card.insertBefore(btn, h3 || card.firstChild);
    if (window.marble) window.marble.adopt(btn);
    wireChips(card);
  }

  function injectAddlink(card) {
    if (card.querySelector(':scope > .addlink')) return;
    const btn = document.createElement('button');
    btn.className = 'addlink';
    btn.type = 'button';
    btn.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
    btn.setAttribute('data-marble-add', '#tpl-beat');
    btn.setAttribute('data-marble-into', 'prev');
    btn.textContent = '+ question';
    const fieldBtn = card.querySelector(':scope > .field-btn');
    if (fieldBtn) card.insertBefore(btn, fieldBtn);
    else card.append(btn);
    if (window.marble) window.marble.adopt(btn);
  }

  const FIELD_ICON = '<svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" d="M3 4.5h10M3 8h10M3 11.5h10"/><circle cx="6.5" cy="4.5" r="1.15" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="10.2" cy="8" r="1.15" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="5.2" cy="11.5" r="1.15" fill="currentColor"/></svg>';

  function ensureFieldBtn(card) {
    // A link carries a single line and nothing else, so it is offered no
    // picker rather than one that opens onto seven fields it cannot wear.
    if (!offeredParts(card).length) return;
    if (card.querySelector(':scope > .field-btn')) return;
    const btn = document.createElement('button');
    btn.className = 'field-btn';
    btn.type = 'button';
    btn.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
    btn.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Choose what this card shows');
    btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', 'false');
    btn.innerHTML = FIELD_ICON;
    btn.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      event.preventDefault();
      event.stopPropagation();
      toggleFieldsMode(card, btn);
    });
    card.append(btn);
  }

  function injectInnerChip(owner, ring) {
    if (!owner || owner.querySelector(':scope > .chip')) return;
    const btn = document.createElement('button');
    btn.className = 'chip';
    btn.type = 'button';
    btn.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
    btn.setAttribute('data-cycle', 'data-mark');
    btn.setAttribute('data-ring', ring);
    owner.insertBefore(btn, owner.firstChild);
    if (window.marble) window.marble.adopt(btn);
  }

  function ensureCardChrome() {
    const cards = [...pool.querySelectorAll(':scope > .item')];
    for (const card of cards) {
      stripGhosts(card);
      if (hasPart(card, 'form')) {
        if (!card.hasAttribute('data-form')) card.setAttribute('data-form', 'rough');
        injectChip(card, 'form');
      }
      if (hasPart(card, 'state')) {
        if (!card.hasAttribute('data-mark')) {
          card.setAttribute('data-mark', card.getAttribute('data-kind') === 'target' ? 'someday' : 'seed');
        }
        injectChip(card, 'state');
      }
      if (hasPart(card, 'beats')) {
        injectAddlink(card);
        for (const beat of card.querySelectorAll(':scope > .beats > .beat')) injectInnerChip(beat, 'heat');
      }
      if (hasPart(card, 'claim')) {
        const claim = card.querySelector(':scope > .claim:not(.part-ghost)');
        if (claim) injectInnerChip(claim, 'maturity');
      }
      ensureFieldBtn(card);
      const grip = card.querySelector(':scope > .marble-grip');
      if (grip && grip.getAttribute('draggable') !== 'true') grip.setAttribute('draggable', 'true');
      wireChips(card);
      if (card.hasAttribute('data-fields-open')) ensureGhosts(card);
      else stripGhosts(card);
    }
  }

  /* A part goes where the card's order says it goes, not after whatever was
     added last: it lands before the first part that comes after it, so a short
     line added to a card that already has a pitch is still above the pitch. */
  function partAnchor(card, id) {
    const start = PARTS.findIndex((p) => p.id === id);
    for (let i = start + 1; i < PARTS.length; i++) {
      const el = card.querySelector(PARTS[i].sel);
      // An anchor the file cannot name is no anchor at all: the block would
      // land here on the page and at the end of the card in the file. A few
      // venue lines were authored without an id and are exactly that.
      if (el && !(window.marble && !window.marble.id(el))) return el;
    }
    return null;
  }

  function insertPersistent(card, node, id) {
    stampIds(node);
    const before = partAnchor(card, id);
    const html = window.marble && window.marble.source
      ? window.marble.source.outer(node)
      : node.outerHTML;
    if (before) card.insertBefore(node, before);
    else {
      const chrome = card.querySelector(':scope > .addlink')
        || card.querySelector(':scope > .field-btn');
      if (chrome) card.insertBefore(node, chrome);
      else card.append(node);
    }
    if (!window.marble) return null;
    window.marble.adopt(node);
    const op = {
      type: 'insert',
      html,
      parentId: window.marble.id(card),
      beforeId: before && window.marble.id(before) ? window.marble.id(before) : null,
    };
    return op;
  }

  function cloneTpl(sel) {
    const seed = document.querySelector(sel)?.content?.firstElementChild;
    return seed ? seed.cloneNode(true) : null;
  }

  function togglePart(card, id) {
    if (!offers(card, id)) return;
    const on = partLive(card, id);
    const kept = partsOf(card).filter((p) => p !== id);
    const next = on ? kept : PARTS.map((p) => p.id).filter((p) => kept.includes(p) || p === id);
    const spec = PARTS.find((p) => p.id === id);
    const ops = [];

    if (!on) {
      if (id === 'form' && !card.hasAttribute('data-form')) {
        card.setAttribute('data-form', 'rough');
        const attrId = window.marble && window.marble.id(card);
        if (attrId) ops.push({ type: 'setAttr', id: attrId, name: 'data-form', value: 'rough' });
      }
      if (id === 'state' && !card.hasAttribute('data-mark')) {
        const value = card.getAttribute('data-kind') === 'target' ? 'someday' : 'seed';
        card.setAttribute('data-mark', value);
        const attrId = window.marble && window.marble.id(card);
        if (attrId) ops.push({ type: 'setAttr', id: attrId, name: 'data-mark', value });
      }
      if (spec && spec.tpl && !card.querySelector(spec.sel)) {
        const node = cloneTpl(spec.tpl);
        if (node) {
          const op = insertPersistent(card, node, id);
          if (op) ops.push(op);
        }
      }
    }

    const was = card.getAttribute('data-parts');
    const value = next.join(' ');
    card.setAttribute('data-parts', value);
    const idAttr = window.marble && window.marble.id(card);
    if (idAttr && window.marble) {
      ops.push({ type: 'setAttr', id: idAttr, name: 'data-parts', value });
      const undo = [];
      for (const op of ops) {
        const inv = window.marble.invert ? window.marble.invert(op) : null;
        if (inv) undo.unshift(Array.isArray(inv) ? inv[0] : inv);
      }
      if (was !== value && !undo.some((u) => u && u.name === 'data-parts')) {
        undo.unshift({ type: 'setAttr', id: idAttr, name: 'data-parts', value: was });
      }
      window.marble.record({ redo: ops, undo });
      for (const op of ops) {
        if (op.type !== 'insert') window.marble.op(op, { immediate: true });
        else window.marble.op(op, { immediate: true });
      }
    }

    ensureCardChrome();
    paint();
    packSpine();
  }

  let fieldsFor = null;

  function stripGhosts(card) {
    for (const ghost of card.querySelectorAll(':scope > .part-ghost')) ghost.remove();
  }

  function ghostAnchor(card, id) {
    const start = PARTS.findIndex((p) => p.id === id);
    for (let i = start + 1; i < PARTS.length; i++) {
      const spec = PARTS[i];
      const found = card.querySelector(spec.sel)
        || card.querySelector(':scope > .part-ghost[data-part="' + spec.id + '"]');
      if (found) return found;
    }
    return card.querySelector(':scope > .addlink')
      || card.querySelector(':scope > .field-btn')
      || null;
  }

  function makeGhost(spec) {
    const btn = document.createElement('button');
    btn.type = 'button';
    btn.className = 'part-ghost';
    btn.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
    btn.setAttribute('data-part', spec.id);
    btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', 'false');
    btn.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Show ' + spec.label);
    if (spec.id === 'form') {
      btn.className += ' chip form';
      btn.textContent = spec.off;
    } else if (spec.id === 'state') {
      btn.className += ' chip pub';
      btn.textContent = spec.off;
    } else if (spec.id === 'gloss') {
      btn.className += ' gloss';
      btn.textContent = spec.label;
    } else if (spec.id === 'say') {
      btn.className += ' say';
      btn.textContent = 'The longer pitch';
    } else if (spec.id === 'claim') {
      btn.className += ' claim';
      btn.innerHTML = '<span class="part-ghost-h">The claim</span><span class="part-ghost-p">What it rules in or out</span>';
    } else if (spec.id === 'meta') {
      btn.className += ' meta';
      btn.innerHTML = '<span class="k">venue</span><span>—</span>';
    } else if (spec.id === 'beats') {
      btn.className += ' beats';
      btn.innerHTML = '<span class="part-ghost-beat">an open question</span>';
    }
    return btn;
  }

  function markPartState(card) {
    const composing = card.hasAttribute('data-fields-open');
    for (const spec of PARTS) {
      const el = card.querySelector(spec.sel);
      if (!el) continue;
      el.classList.toggle('marble-part-off', composing && !hasPart(card, spec.id));
      if (el.matches('.chip[data-cycle]')) paintChip(el);
    }
  }

  function ensureGhosts(card) {
    stripGhosts(card);
    markPartState(card);
    if (!card.hasAttribute('data-fields-open')) return;
    const h3 = card.querySelector(':scope > h3');
    for (const spec of PARTS) {
      if (!offers(card, spec.id)) continue;
      if (card.querySelector(spec.sel)) continue;
      const ghost = makeGhost(spec);
      if (spec.id === 'form' || spec.id === 'state') card.insertBefore(ghost, h3 || card.firstChild);
      else {
        const before = ghostAnchor(card, spec.id);
        if (before) card.insertBefore(ghost, before);
        else card.append(ghost);
      }
    }
  }

  function closeFieldsMode() {
    if (!fieldsFor) return;
    const card = fieldsFor;
    card.removeAttribute('data-fields-open');
    const btn = card.querySelector(':scope > .field-btn');
    if (btn) btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', 'false');
    stripGhosts(card);
    markPartState(card);
    fieldsFor = null;
    packSpine();
  }

  function toggleFieldsMode(card, btn) {
    if (fieldsFor === card) {
      closeFieldsMode();
      return;
    }
    if (fieldsFor) closeFieldsMode();
    fieldsFor = card;
    card.setAttribute('data-fields-open', '');
    btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', 'true');
    ensureGhosts(card);
    packSpine();
  }

  function partFromEvent(event, card) {
    const t = event.target;
    if (!(t instanceof Element) || !card.contains(t)) return null;
    if (t.closest('.field-btn, .marble-grip, .addlink')) return null;
    const ghost = t.closest('.part-ghost');
    if (ghost && ghost.parentElement === card) return ghost.dataset.part;
    for (const spec of PARTS) {
      const el = card.querySelector(spec.sel);
      if (el && (el === t || el.contains(t))) return spec.id;
    }
    return null;
  }

  function onFieldsClick(event) {
    const card = event.target.closest && event.target.closest('.item');
    if (!card || !card.hasAttribute('data-fields-open')) return;
    if (pool !== card.parentElement && band !== card.parentElement) return;
    const id = partFromEvent(event, card);
    if (!id) return;
    event.preventDefault();
    event.stopPropagation();
    togglePart(card, id);
  }

  document.addEventListener('click', onFieldsClick, true);
  document.addEventListener('pointerdown', (event) => {
    if (!fieldsFor) return;
    if (fieldsFor.contains(event.target)) return;
    closeFieldsMode();
  });
  document.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
    if (event.key === 'Escape' && fieldsFor) {
      const btn = fieldsFor.querySelector(':scope > .field-btn');
      closeFieldsMode();
      btn?.focus();
    }
  });

  function cardId(card) {
    return (window.marble && window.marble.id(card)) || card.getAttribute('data-marble-id');
  }

  function nowCards() {
    return [...pool.querySelectorAll(':scope > .item[data-now]')]
      .sort((a, b) => (parseInt(a.getAttribute('data-now'), 10) || 0) - (parseInt(b.getAttribute('data-now'), 10) || 0));
  }

  function nowSource(ref) {
    const id = ref && ref.dataset.ref;
    if (!id) return null;
    return pool.querySelector(':scope > .item[data-marble-id="' + CSS.escape(id) + '"]');
  }

  function applyNowOrder(list, previous) {
    const redo = [];
    const undo = [];
    const kept = new Set(list);
    list.forEach((card, i) => {
      const value = String(i + 1);
      const was = previous.has(card) ? previous.get(card) : card.getAttribute('data-now');
      if (was === value) return;
      card.setAttribute('data-now', value);
      const id = cardId(card);
      if (id && window.marble) {
        redo.push({ type: 'setAttr', id, name: 'data-now', value });
        undo.push({ type: 'setAttr', id, name: 'data-now', value: was == null ? null : was });
      }
    });
    for (const [card, was] of previous) {
      if (kept.has(card)) continue;
      if (card.hasAttribute('data-now')) card.removeAttribute('data-now');
      const id = cardId(card);
      if (id && window.marble) {
        redo.push({ type: 'setAttr', id, name: 'data-now', value: null });
        undo.push({ type: 'setAttr', id, name: 'data-now', value: was });
      }
    }
    if (redo.length && window.marble) {
      window.marble.record({ redo, undo: undo.reverse() });
      for (const op of redo) window.marble.op(op, { immediate: true });
    }
  }

  function pinNow(card, before) {
    if (!card || card.parentElement !== pool) return;
    const prevList = nowCards();
    const previous = new Map(prevList.map((c) => [c, c.getAttribute('data-now')]));
    if (!previous.has(card)) previous.set(card, card.getAttribute('data-now'));
    const list = prevList.filter((c) => c !== card);
    let at = before ? list.indexOf(before) : -1;
    if (at < 0) at = list.length;
    list.splice(at, 0, card);
    applyNowOrder(list, previous);
    paint();
    packSpine();
  }

  function unpinNow(card) {
    if (!card || !card.hasAttribute('data-now')) return;
    const prevList = nowCards();
    const previous = new Map(prevList.map((c) => [c, c.getAttribute('data-now')]));
    applyNowOrder(prevList.filter((c) => c !== card), previous);
    paint();
    packSpine();
  }

  function syncNowRef(ref, card) {
    const title = ref.querySelector('.now-ref-title');
    const gloss = ref.querySelector('.now-ref-gloss');
    const form = ref.querySelector('.now-ref-form');
    if (title) title.textContent = (card.querySelector(':scope > h3')?.textContent || '').trim() || 'Untitled';
    const line = (card.querySelector(':scope > .gloss')?.textContent || card.querySelector(':scope > .say')?.textContent || '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
    if (gloss) gloss.textContent = line.length > 88 ? line.slice(0, 87) + '…' : line;
    if (form) form.textContent = card.getAttribute('data-form') || '';
    const formVal = card.getAttribute('data-form');
    if (formVal) ref.setAttribute('data-form', formVal);
    else ref.removeAttribute('data-form');
  }

  function makeNowRef(id) {
    const ref = document.createElement('div');
    ref.className = 'now-ref';
    ref.draggable = true;
    ref.tabIndex = 0;
    ref.setAttribute('role', 'button');
    ref.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
    ref.dataset.ref = id;
    ref.innerHTML = '<span class="now-ref-form"></span><span class="now-ref-title"></span><span class="now-ref-gloss"></span><button type="button" class="now-ref-x" aria-label="Unpin from now">×</button>';
    ref.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      if (event.target.closest('.now-ref-x')) {
        event.preventDefault();
        event.stopPropagation();
        const src = nowSource(ref);
        if (src) unpinNow(src);
        return;
      }
      const src = nowSource(ref);
      if (!src) return;
      src.scrollIntoView({ block: 'center', behavior: 'smooth' });
      src.classList.add('marble-now-flash');
      setTimeout(() => src.classList.remove('marble-now-flash'), 900);
    });
    ref.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
      if (event.key !== 'Enter' && event.key !== ' ') return;
      if (event.target.closest('.now-ref-x')) return;
      event.preventDefault();
      ref.click();
    });
    return ref;
  }

  function paintNow() {
    if (!band) return;
    const cards = nowCards();
    const have = new Map([...band.querySelectorAll(':scope > .now-ref')].map((el) => [el.dataset.ref, el]));
    const used = new Set();
    for (const card of cards) {
      const id = cardId(card);
      if (!id) continue;
      let ref = have.get(id);
      if (!ref) {
        ref = makeNowRef(id);
        band.append(ref);
      }
      used.add(id);
      syncNowRef(ref, card);
      band.append(ref);
    }
    for (const [id, ref] of have) if (!used.has(id)) ref.remove();
    band.classList.toggle('is-empty', !cards.length);
  }

  // Runs now, over every insert, and over the whole body after a reconcile.
  function paint() {
    const arrange = stage.getAttribute('data-arrange');
    const show = stage.getAttribute('data-show');
    const focus = stage.getAttribute('data-focus');
    for (const button of presets) {
      const want = PRESETS[button.dataset.preset];
      const on = !!want && want.arrange === arrange && want.show === show;
      button.classList.toggle('marble-on', on);
      button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(on));
    }
    for (const button of dials) {
      const [dial, value] = button.dataset.set.split(':');
      const current = dial === 'arrange' ? arrange : dial === 'show' ? show : focus;
      const on = current === value;
      button.classList.toggle('marble-on', on);
      button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(on));
    }
    const canvasOn = pool.getAttribute('data-marble-canvas') !== 'off';
    for (const button of document.querySelectorAll('[data-toggle="arrange-map"]')) {
      button.hidden = arrange !== 'map';
      button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(canvasOn));
      button.textContent = canvasOn ? 'Done — type into cards' : 'Arrange the map';
    }
    const compactOn = stage.getAttribute('data-density') === 'compact';
    const compactable = arrange === 'focus' || arrange === 'thesis';
    for (const button of document.querySelectorAll('[data-toggle="density"]')) {
      button.hidden = !compactable;
      button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(compactOn));
      button.textContent = compactOn ? 'More detail' : 'Compact';
    }
    stampThesisStacks();
    ensureCardChrome();
    paintNow();
    for (const slot of document.querySelectorAll('[data-counts]')) {
      const ideas = pool.querySelectorAll(':scope > .item[data-kind="idea"]').length;
      const live = pool.querySelectorAll(':scope > .item[data-now]').length;
      slot.textContent = ideas + ' ideas · ' + live + ' in hand';
    }
    for (const chip of document.querySelectorAll('.chip[data-cycle]')) paintChip(chip);
  }

  /* A dial reads its own attribute — except while the picker is open and the
     slot is off, where it says which slot it is. Older cards carry a chip in
     the file where newer ones grow a ghost, and the two read "—" and "status"
     for the same state until both came through here. */
  function paintChip(chip) {
    const owner = chip.closest('[' + chip.dataset.cycle + ']');
    const value = owner ? owner.getAttribute(chip.dataset.cycle) : null;
    const off = chip.classList.contains('marble-part-off')
      ? PARTS.find((p) => p.off && chip.classList.contains(p.id === 'form' ? 'form' : 'pub'))
      : null;
    chip.textContent = off ? off.off : (value || '—');
    const ring = RINGS[chip.dataset.ring] || [];
    chip.title = (value || 'unset') + ' — click to cycle: ' + ring.join(' → ');
    chip.setAttribute('aria-label', chip.dataset.ring + ': ' + (value || 'unset') + '. Click to cycle.');
  }

  function wireChips(root) {
    const scope = root && root.querySelectorAll ? root : document;
    const chips = [
      ...(root && root.matches?.('.chip[data-cycle]') ? [root] : []),
      ...scope.querySelectorAll('.chip[data-cycle]'),
    ];
    for (const chip of chips) {
      if (wired.has(chip)) continue;
      wired.add(chip);
      chip.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const name = chip.dataset.cycle;
        const ring = RINGS[chip.dataset.ring];
        if (!ring) return;
        // A chip with no value yet still has an owner — the card it sits in —
        // so it falls to the front of the ring rather than doing nothing.
        const owner = chip.closest('[' + name + ']') || chip.closest('.item, .claim, .beat');
        if (!owner) return;
        const next = ring[(ring.indexOf(owner.getAttribute(name)) + 1) % ring.length];
        writeUndoable(owner, name, next);
        chip.textContent = next;
        paint();
      });
    }
  }

  /* ── moving a card ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

     There is one pool of cards and every arrangement but the list and the
     map decides where a card sits from its attributes, not from its place in
     the file. The carrier's sortable only ever reorders the file, so left to
     itself a drag in the spine or on the board changed something real and
     showed nothing: the card sprang back to the column its attributes still
     named, and the gesture read as broken. So a drag in a columned
     arrangement is taken over here and made to mean the thing it looks like
     it means — this card now belongs to that column, in that position.

     A column is CSS placement rather than a box, so there is nothing under
     the pointer to drop onto. What there is, is a heading pinned to the top
     of each column, whose horizontal span is the column's; measure those and
     the whole column is a target. In the spine there are no headings because
     the thesis card is the heading, so the thesis cards are the lanes. */
  function lanesFor(arrange) {
    const of = (el, value) => ({ el, value });
    if (arrange === 'thesis') {
      return [...pool.querySelectorAll('.item[data-kind="thesis"]')]
        .map((el) => of(el, el.getAttribute('data-marble-id')))
        .filter((lane) => lane.value);
    }
    return [...pool.querySelectorAll('.lane[data-lane^="' + arrange + ':"]')]
      .map((el) => of(el, el.dataset.lane.split(':')[1]));
  }

  /* Strict containment would leave the gutters dead: a drop in the ten
     pixels between two columns would do nothing and read as a failed
     gesture, which is exactly the feeling this is meant to remove. The
     nearest column wins instead, so every pixel of the pool is live. */
  function laneAt(x, arrange) {
    let best = null;
    let nearest = Infinity;
    for (const lane of lanesFor(arrange)) {
      if (!lane.el.getClientRects().length) continue;
      const box = lane.el.getBoundingClientRect();
      const gap = x < box.left ? box.left - x : x > box.right ? x - box.right : 0;
      if (gap < nearest) { nearest = gap; best = lane; }
    }
    return best;
  }

  /* Which cards are already in that column, top to bottom. Asked of the
     layout rather than of the attributes: a card serving two theses is drawn
     in one column only, and where it is drawn is the truth the person
     dragging is looking at. */
  function cardsIn(box, except) {
    return [...pool.children]
      .filter((el) => {
        if (el === except || !el.classList.contains('item')) return false;
        if (!el.getClientRects().length) return false;
        const at = el.getBoundingClientRect();
        const middle = at.left + at.width / 2;
        return middle >= box.left && middle <= box.right;
      })
      .sort((a, b) => a.getBoundingClientRect().top - b.getBoundingClientRect().top);
  }

  // The card the drop would land above, or null for the end of the column.
  const spotIn = (cards, y) =>
    cards.find((card) => {
      const at = card.getBoundingClientRect();
      return y < at.top + at.height / 2;
    }) || null;

  const nextCard = (el) => {
    let after = el.nextElementSibling;
    while (after && after.hasAttribute('data-marble-transient')) after = after.nextElementSibling;
    return after;
  };

  const idOf = (el) => (el && window.marble ? window.marble.id(el) : null);

  /* The wash over the target column and the line at the place the card would
     take. Both are page-only: they are marked transient, so the carrier
     never reads them as content, and they are gone before anything is
     written. */
  let zone = null;
  let line = null;

  function mark(lane, y, card) {
    if (!zone) {
      zone = document.createElement('div');
      zone.className = 'dropzone';
      zone.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
      line = document.createElement('div');
      line.className = 'dropline';
      line.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
    }
    if (zone.parentElement !== pool) pool.append(zone, line);

    const frame = pool.getBoundingClientRect();
    const box = lane.el.getBoundingClientRect();
    const left = box.left - frame.left;
    const top = box.bottom - frame.top + 6;
    zone.style.left = left + 'px';
    zone.style.width = box.width + 'px';
    zone.style.top = top + 'px';
    zone.style.height = Math.max(46, pool.clientHeight - top - 2) + 'px';

    const cards = cardsIn(box, card);
    const above = spotIn(cards, y);
    let at = top + 7;
    if (above) at = above.getBoundingClientRect().top - frame.top - 6;
    else if (cards.length) at = cards[cards.length - 1].getBoundingClientRect().bottom - frame.top + 4;
    line.style.left = left + 'px';
    line.style.width = box.width + 'px';
    line.style.top = at + 'px';
  }

  function clearMarks() {
    if (zone) { zone.remove(); line.remove(); }
    for (const el of document.querySelectorAll('.marble-target')) el.classList.remove('marble-target');
  }

  /* A card is born from a template, so it is born holding the template's map
     coordinates — which means every new idea would be stacked on the same
     corner of the map, on top of the thesis already sitting there and on top
     of the last idea added. A card still wearing those coordinates has never
     been placed, so it is given the first clear row under everything that
     has. */
  const UNPLACED = 'left:44px;top:44px';

  function place(root) {
    const scope = root && root.querySelectorAll ? root : document;
    const fresh = [...scope.querySelectorAll('.item')].filter(
      (el) => el.parentElement === pool
        && (el.getAttribute('style') || '').replace(/\s/g, '') === UNPLACED
    );
    if (!fresh.length) return;
    let lowest = 0;
    for (const el of pool.children) {
      if (!el.classList.contains('item') || fresh.includes(el)) continue;
      const top = parseInt(el.style.top, 10);
      if (!Number.isNaN(top)) lowest = Math.max(lowest, top);
    }
    // A card is never taller than a screen of its own, so a clear row below
    // the lowest one placed is clear whatever the cards happen to say.
    let left = 24;
    let top = lowest + 140;
    for (const el of fresh) {
      write(el, 'style', 'left:' + left + 'px;top:' + top + 'px');
      left += 276;
      if (left > 852) { left = 24; top += 100; }
    }
  }

  /* The two halves of the gesture — which column, and where in it — filed as
     one change, so one Mod+Z takes the whole drag back instead of half of
     it. */
  function land(card, name, value, above) {
    const was = card.getAttribute(name);
    const fromParent = card.parentElement;
    const fromBefore = nextCard(card);
    const moved = fromParent !== pool || fromBefore !== above;
    if (was === value && !moved) return;

    if (value === null) card.removeAttribute(name);
    else card.setAttribute(name, value);
    if (moved) pool.insertBefore(card, above);

    const id = idOf(card);
    if (!id || !window.marble) return;
    const redo = [];
    const undo = [];
    if (was !== value) {
      redo.push({ type: 'setAttr', id, name, value });
      undo.push({ type: 'setAttr', id, name, value: was });
    }
    if (moved) {
      redo.push({ type: 'move', id, parentId: idOf(pool), beforeId: idOf(above) });
      undo.unshift({ type: 'move', id, parentId: idOf(fromParent), beforeId: idOf(fromBefore) });
    }
    window.marble.record({ redo, undo });
    for (const op of redo) window.marble.op(op, { immediate: true });
  }

  // Which value a drop writes. Holding shift in the spine adds the thesis
  // rather than moving to it, which is the only way to say out loud that one
  // idea is serving two of them.
  function valueFor(arrange, card, lane, add) {
    // "Not sorted yet" is a column like any other, and its honest spelling
    // is no attribute at all.
    if (lane.value === 'none') return null;
    if (!add || arrange !== 'thesis') return lane.value;
    const held = (card.getAttribute('data-thesis') || '').split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean);
    if (!held.includes(lane.value)) held.push(lane.value);
    return held.join(' ');
  }

  /* The drag is watched from the top of the document so that the card being
     carried is known before any of the listeners below need it, and so that
     the carrier's own commit can be headed off on the way down when this
     file has already done the filing itself. */
  let carried = null;
  let filed = false;

  /* A column here can run four screens deep, so a drag has to be able to
     reach past the edge of the window. The scrolling that makes that
     possible is driven by the drag's own dragover events and nothing else:
     scrolling from an animation frame instead wedges the nested loop the
     browser runs a drag inside, and the whole page stops answering. */
  /* Only while the pointer is actually travelling. A hand coming to rest
     against the edge to let go still twitches a pixel or two, and a browser
     will not accept a drop into a document that is scrolling as the button
     comes up — so scrolling through those last small movements is how a
     finished drag gets thrown away. Settling stops the page; moving on
     again starts it. */
  let was = null;

  function reach(x, y) {
    const travelled = !was || Math.abs(y - was.y) >= 4 || Math.abs(x - was.x) >= 4;
    was = { x, y };
    if (!travelled) return;
    const edge = 104;
    const below = y - (window.innerHeight - edge);
    const above = edge - y;
    const by = below > 0 ? Math.min(30, below / 2.5) : above > 0 ? -Math.min(30, above / 2.5) : 0;
    if (by) window.scrollBy(0, by);
  }

  const itemOf = (el) => {
    if (!el || !el.closest) return null;
    const grip = el.closest('.marble-grip');
    const fromGrip = grip && grip.parentElement;
    if (fromGrip && fromGrip.classList.contains('item')) return fromGrip;
    const item = el.closest('.item');
    return item && (item.parentElement === pool || item.parentElement === band) ? item : null;
  };

  // A thesis card is the heading of its own column in the spine, so it is
  // the one card there that a column cannot be told to hold.
  const columned = (card, arrange) =>
    !!card && !(arrange === 'thesis' && card.getAttribute('data-kind') === 'thesis');

  document.addEventListener('dragstart', (event) => {
    const ref = event.target.closest && event.target.closest('.now-ref');
    carried = ref ? nowSource(ref) : itemOf(event.target);
    filed = false;
    was = null;
    if (!carried) return;
    document.body.classList.add('carrying');
    try {
      event.dataTransfer.effectAllowed = 'move';
      if (event.dataTransfer.types.length === 0) {
        event.dataTransfer.setData('text/plain', cardId(carried) || 'item');
      }
    } catch (_) { /* some browsers freeze dataTransfer after dragstart */ }
  }, true);

  document.addEventListener('dragend', (event) => {
    document.body.classList.remove('carrying');
    clearMarks();
    if (filed && carried) {
      // This file has already moved the card and recorded both halves as one
      // change; letting the carrier commit as well would file the move twice
      // and put an extra step in the undo stack. Stopping the event on the
      // way down means its handler never runs, so the tidying it would have
      // done is done here instead.
      event.stopPropagation();
      carried.classList.remove('marble-dragging');
    }
    carried = null;
    filed = false;
    paint();
  }, true);

  /* The band lives inside the working area but is not a column: dropping
     there pins data-now instead of landing in a thesis. Columns keep their
     hands off anything over the band. */
  const wrap = document.querySelector('[data-marble-id="band-wrap"]');
  const onBand = (event) => !!wrap && wrap.contains(event.target);

  function over(event) {
    const arrange = stage.getAttribute('data-arrange');
    if (!LANED[arrange] || !carried || onBand(event)) return;
    reach(event.clientX, event.clientY);
    event.preventDefault();
    event.dataTransfer.dropEffect = 'move';
    const lane = laneAt(event.clientX, arrange);
    clearMarks();
    if (!lane || !columned(carried, arrange)) return;
    lane.el.classList.add('marble-target');
    mark(lane, event.clientY, carried);
  }

  function down(event) {
    const arrange = stage.getAttribute('data-arrange');
    if (onBand(event)) { clearMarks(); return; }
    if (!LANED[arrange] || !carried) { clearMarks(); return; }
    event.preventDefault();
    const lane = laneAt(event.clientX, arrange);
    const card = carried;
    clearMarks();
    if (!lane || !columned(card, arrange)) return;
    const above = spotIn(cardsIn(lane.el.getBoundingClientRect(), card), event.clientY);
    land(card, LANED[arrange], valueFor(arrange, card, lane, event.shiftKey), above);
    filed = true;
    paint();
  }

  pool.addEventListener('dragover', (event) => {
    /* The carrier would reorder the grid live under the pointer. In a single
       column that is the clearest feedback there is; in a dense grid it is
       cards leaping between columns while you are still deciding, which
       reads as a fault. This listener is registered before the carrier wires
       its own, so stopping here stops that one, and the wash and the line
       say the same thing without moving anything. */
    if (LANED[stage.getAttribute('data-arrange')] && carried) event.stopImmediatePropagation();
    over(event);
  });

  pool.addEventListener('drop', (event) => {
    if (LANED[stage.getAttribute('data-arrange')] && carried) event.stopImmediatePropagation();
    down(event);
  });

  /* The pool is only as tall as the cards in it, and the page keeps going
     below it. A card let go in that margin — which is exactly where the
     page's own scrolling tends to leave the pointer — would otherwise fall
     through and the whole drag would be lost without a word. The working
     area answers for the columns as well, so there is nowhere inside it that
     silently swallows a card. Drops over the pool never reach here: that
     listener stops them, having already done the same work. */
  stage.addEventListener('dragover', over);
  stage.addEventListener('drop', down);

  stage.addEventListener('dragleave', (event) => {
    if (!stage.contains(event.relatedTarget)) clearMarks();
  });

  function eventOverBand(event) {
    return !!(wrap && event.target && wrap.contains(event.target));
  }

  function hitIsBand(x, y) {
    if (!wrap) return false;
    const hit = document.elementFromPoint(x, y);
    return !!(hit && wrap.contains(hit));
  }

  function draggingCard() {
    return document.querySelector('.pool > .item.marble-dragging')
      || (carried && pool.contains(carried) ? carried : null);
  }

  function pinCardOnBand(card, at) {
    if (!card || !wrap) return false;
    const before = at ? nowSource(at) : null;
    if (!(before === card && card.hasAttribute('data-now'))) {
      pinNow(card, before && before !== card ? before : null);
    }
    return true;
  }

  /* The band is a pin, not a destination. Dropping a card there writes
     data-now on the pool card and leaves the card where it is. The handle's
     drag is effectAllowed=move, so the band has to answer move — copy is
     refused and the drop never fires. Pointer-driven sortable never emits
     HTML5 drop, so pointerup over the band pins the same way. */
  document.addEventListener('dragover', (event) => {
    const card = carried || draggingCard();
    if (!card || !eventOverBand(event)) {
      if (band && !eventOverBand(event)) band.classList.remove('marble-target');
      return;
    }
    event.preventDefault();
    event.stopPropagation();
    try { event.dataTransfer.dropEffect = 'move'; } catch (_) {}
    band.classList.add('marble-target');
  }, true);

  document.addEventListener('drop', (event) => {
    const card = carried || draggingCard();
    if (!card || !eventOverBand(event)) return;
    event.preventDefault();
    event.stopPropagation();
    const ref = event.target.closest && event.target.closest('.now-ref');
    if (pinCardOnBand(card, ref)) filed = true;
    band.classList.remove('marble-target');
  }, true);

  document.addEventListener('pointermove', (event) => {
    if (!document.body.classList.contains('marble-dragging-now')) return;
    band?.classList.toggle('marble-target', hitIsBand(event.clientX, event.clientY));
  }, true);

  document.addEventListener('pointerup', (event) => {
    if (!document.body.classList.contains('marble-dragging-now') && !carried) return;
    const card = draggingCard();
    if (!card) return;
    if (!hitIsBand(event.clientX, event.clientY)) return;
    const ref = document.elementFromPoint(event.clientX, event.clientY)?.closest?.('.now-ref');
    if (pinCardOnBand(card, ref)) filed = true;
    band?.classList.remove('marble-target');
  }, true);

  if (wrap && band) {
    wrap.addEventListener('dragleave', (event) => {
      if (!wrap.contains(event.relatedTarget)) band.classList.remove('marble-target');
    });
  }

  /* The same move without a mouse. A handle is a button, so it can be tabbed
     to; from there the arrow keys walk the card across the columns of
     whatever arrangement is on. */
  document.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
    if (event.key !== 'ArrowLeft' && event.key !== 'ArrowRight') return;
    if (event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey || event.altKey) return;
    const card = itemOf(event.target);
    const arrange = stage.getAttribute('data-arrange');
    const name = card && LANED[arrange];
    if (!name || !columned(card, arrange)) return;
    const lanes = lanesFor(arrange).filter((lane) => lane.el.getClientRects().length);
    if (!lanes.length) return;
    event.preventDefault();
    const at = card.getBoundingClientRect();
    const middle = at.left + at.width / 2;
    let index = lanes.findIndex((lane) => {
      const box = lane.el.getBoundingClientRect();
      return middle >= box.left && middle <= box.right;
    });
    if (index < 0) index = 0;
    const step = event.key === 'ArrowRight' ? 1 : -1;
    const next = lanes[index + step];
    if (!next) return;
    writeUndoable(card, name, valueFor(arrange, card, next, false));
    paint();
    // The card is re-laid-out under the same handle, which keeps the focus.
    event.target.focus();
  });

  for (const button of presets) {
    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const want = PRESETS[button.dataset.preset];
      if (want) setView(want.arrange, want.show);
    });
  }
  for (const button of dials) {
    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const [dial, value] = button.dataset.set.split(':');
      // Focus means nothing without the focus arrangement showing it, so
      // picking a thesis to focus on also switches to it — one click, not two.
      if (dial === 'focus') { setView('focus', null, value); return; }
      setView(dial === 'arrange' ? value : null, dial === 'show' ? value : null);
    });
  }
  for (const button of document.querySelectorAll('[data-toggle="arrange-map"]')) {
    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
      write(pool, 'data-marble-canvas', pool.getAttribute('data-marble-canvas') === 'off' ? '' : 'off');
      paint();
    });
  }
  for (const button of document.querySelectorAll('[data-toggle="density"]')) {
    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
      write(stage, 'data-density', stage.getAttribute('data-density') === 'compact' ? null : 'compact');
      paint();
      const until = performance.now() + 360;
      const tick = (now) => {
        packSpine();
        if (now < until) requestAnimationFrame(tick);
      };
      requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    });
  }

  addEventListener('resize', packSpine);

  wireChips(document);
  paint();
  packSpine();
  if (window.marble) {
    if (window.marble.pageOnly) window.marble.pageOnly('data-fields-open');
    window.marble.register(wireChips);
    window.marble.register(place);
    window.marble.register(paint);
    window.marble.register(packSpine);
    window.marble.register(ensureCardChrome);
  } else {
    addEventListener('marble:ready', (event) => {
      if (event.detail.pageOnly) event.detail.pageOnly('data-fields-open');
      event.detail.register(wireChips);
      event.detail.register(place);
      event.detail.register(paint);
      event.detail.register(packSpine);
      event.detail.register(ensureCardChrome);
    }, { once: true });
  }
})();
</script>
<script>
(() => {
  const begin = (marble) => {
    const TRANSIENT = 'data-marble-transient';
    const ID = 'data-marble-id';

    const isTransient = (el) => el.hasAttribute(TRANSIENT);
    const persistentChildren = (el) => [...el.children].filter((c) => !isTransient(c));

    function nextPersistentSibling(el) {
      let sibling = el.nextElementSibling;
      while (sibling && isTransient(sibling)) sibling = sibling.nextElementSibling;
      return sibling;
    }

    // An element can hold several roles at once — a beat is both editable and a
    // sortable item — so wiring is tracked per role, not per element.
    const roles = new WeakMap();
    function claim(el, role) {
      const held = roles.get(el) ?? new Set();
      if (held.has(role)) return false;
      held.add(role);
      roles.set(el, held);
      return true;
    }

    const within = (root, selector) => [
      ...(root.matches?.(selector) ? [root] : []),
      ...root.querySelectorAll(selector),
    ];

    // Each part contributes its own styling, its own wiring pass, and the
    // selector for what it makes touchable — so removing a part removes all
    // three and nothing dangles.
    const css = [];
    const wirers = [];
    const addressable = [];

    // A subtree that isn't in the document yet can't be addressed by path, so
    // it carries its ids with it — they are serialized into the insert op.
    function assignIdsIn(root) {
      for (const el of [root, ...root.querySelectorAll('*')]) {
        if (isTransient(el) || marble.id(el)) continue;
        if (el === root || el.matches(ADDRESSABLE)) el.setAttribute(ID, marble.newId());
      }
    }

    css.push(`
      .marble-item { position: relative; }
      .marble-item.marble-dragging { opacity: .4; }
      .marble-grip {
        position: absolute; left: -1.5rem; top: .1rem;
        display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px;
        opacity: 0; transition: opacity .12s ease;
      }
      .marble-item:hover > .marble-grip, .marble-grip:focus-within { opacity: 1; }
      .marble-btn {
        width: 1.1rem; height: 1.1rem; padding: 0; border: 0; border-radius: 3px;
        background: none; color: currentColor; opacity: .35;
        font: 500 11px/1.1rem ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
        text-align: center; cursor: pointer;
      }
      .marble-btn:hover { opacity: 1; background: rgba(127, 127, 127, .14); }
    `);

    function grip(item) {
      let grip = item.querySelector(':scope > .marble-grip');
      if (!grip) {
        grip = document.createElement('div');
        grip.className = 'marble-grip';
        grip.setAttribute(TRANSIENT, '');
        // The item may itself be editable, so the chrome has to be an atomic
        // island the caret can't wander into.
        grip.setAttribute('contenteditable', 'false');
        item.prepend(grip);
        item.classList.add('marble-item');
      }
      return grip;
    }

    // The text an element actually owns — the grip and any other chrome is the
    // page's, so it is not part of what the row says.
    const ownText = (el) =>
      [...el.childNodes]
        .filter((n) => !(n.nodeType === 1 && n.hasAttribute(TRANSIENT)))
        .map((n) => n.textContent)
        .join('');

    // Emptiness is derived, never stored: it is read back off the text already
    // in the file. CSS can say :empty, but not "empty apart from the chrome
    // this script just injected", which is every editable row in this document.
    function markEmpty(el) {
      el.classList.toggle('marble-empty', !ownText(el).trim());
    }

    // One remove, so the × and the Backspace below record the same inverse.
    function removeItem(el) {
      const id = marble.id(el);
      // Invert against the live node — after remove, the prior state is gone.
      const inverse = marble.invert({ type: 'remove', id });
      el.remove();
      const op = { type: 'remove', id };
      if (inverse) marble.record({ redo: [op], undo: [inverse] });
      marble.op(op, { immediate: true });
    }

    const previousRow = (el) => {
      let sibling = el.previousElementSibling;
      while (sibling && isTransient(sibling)) sibling = sibling.previousElementSibling;
      return sibling?.matches('[data-marble-editable]') ? sibling : null;
    };

    const caretToEnd = (el) => {
      el.focus();
      const range = document.createRange();
      range.selectNodeContents(el);
      range.collapse(false);
      const selection = getSelection();
      selection?.removeAllRanges();
      selection?.addRange(range);
    };

    // Re-derived over every insert and over the whole body after a reconcile,
    // which is exactly when a derived class has just been lost. Unclaimed, so
    // unlike the wirers below it runs again for rows it has already seen.
    wirers.push((root) => {
      for (const el of within(root, '[data-marble-editable]')) markEmpty(el);
    });

    // These live in the page and never in the file, which the carrier has no
    // way to know until it is told.
    marble.pageOnly('contenteditable', 'spellcheck');
    addressable.push('[data-marble-editable]');
    wirers.push(wireEditable);

    function wireEditable(root) {
      for (const el of within(root, '[data-marble-editable]')) {
        if (!claim(el, 'editable')) continue;

        // plaintext-only keeps the browser from injecting <div> and <br> soup
        // on Enter, so textContent stays the whole truth and setText is safe.
        el.setAttribute('contenteditable', 'plaintext-only');
        if (el.contentEditable !== 'plaintext-only') el.setAttribute('contenteditable', 'true');
        el.spellcheck = false;
        markEmpty(el);

        // One undo step per edit session: prior text is captured on focus, and
        // keystrokes coalesce against that same entry until blur.
        let prior = null;
        el.addEventListener('focus', () => {
          prior = marble.text(el);
        });
        el.addEventListener('input', () => {
          markEmpty(el);
          const id = marble.id(el);
          const text = marble.text(el);
          const op = { type: 'setText', id, text };
          if (prior !== null && text !== prior) {
            marble.record(
              { redo: [op], undo: [{ type: 'setText', id, text: prior }] },
              { coalesce: true },
            );
          }
          marble.op(op);
        });
        el.addEventListener('blur', () => {
          prior = null;
          marble.flush();
        });

        // Most editable nodes here are a heading or a single line, where Enter
        // means "done" and a newline buried in the text is a mistake you can't
        // see. Shift-Enter still breaks the line where a break is wanted.
        el.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
          // A row you have just emptied is debris, and while it is empty its
          // chrome is stood down, so there is no × to aim at. Backspace takes
          // it out the way it does in every outliner, and Mod+Z brings it back.
          if (
            event.key === 'Backspace' &&
            el.hasAttribute('data-marble-removable') &&
            !ownText(el).trim()
          ) {
            event.preventDefault();
            const back = previousRow(el);
            removeItem(el);
            if (back) caretToEnd(back);
            return;
          }
          if (event.key === 'Escape' || (event.key === 'Enter' && !event.shiftKey)) {
            event.preventDefault();
            el.blur();
          }
        });
      }
    }

    css.push(`.marble-remove:hover { color: #b4544f; }`);
    addressable.push('[data-marble-removable]');
    wirers.push(wireRemovable);

    function wireRemovable(root) {
      for (const el of within(root, '[data-marble-removable]')) {
        if (!claim(el, 'removable')) continue;

        const button = document.createElement('button');
        button.className = 'marble-btn marble-remove';
        button.setAttribute(TRANSIENT, '');
        button.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Delete');
        button.title = 'Delete';
        button.textContent = '×';
        button.addEventListener('click', () => removeItem(el));
        grip(el).append(button);
      }
    }

    css.push(`
      .marble-handle { cursor: grab; }
      .marble-handle:active { cursor: grabbing; }
    `);
    marble.pageOnly('draggable');
    addressable.push('[data-marble-sortable]', '[data-marble-sortable] > *');
    wirers.push(wireSortable);

    let dragging = null;
    let origin = null;

    // Items move freely between containers declaring the same group.
    const group = (el) => el?.getAttribute('data-marble-sortable') ?? null;

    function wireSortableItem(item) {
      if (!claim(item, 'sortable-item')) return;

      const handle = document.createElement('button');
      handle.className = 'marble-btn marble-handle';
      handle.setAttribute(TRANSIENT, '');
      handle.setAttribute('draggable', 'true');
      handle.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Reorder');
      handle.title = 'Drag to reorder';
      handle.textContent = '⠿';

      handle.addEventListener('dragstart', (event) => {
        dragging = item;
        origin = { parent: item.parentElement, before: nextPersistentSibling(item) };
        item.classList.add('marble-dragging');
        event.dataTransfer.effectAllowed = 'move';
        event.dataTransfer.setData('text/plain', marble.id(item));
        event.dataTransfer.setDragImage(item, 12, 12);
      });

      handle.addEventListener('dragend', () => {
        if (!dragging) return;
        dragging.classList.remove('marble-dragging');
        commitMove();
        dragging = null;
        origin = null;
      });

      grip(item).prepend(handle);
    }

    function wireSortable(root) {
      for (const container of within(root, '[data-marble-sortable]')) {
        for (const item of persistentChildren(container)) wireSortableItem(item);
        if (!claim(container, 'sortable')) continue;

        // Reorder live during the drag, then report the final resting place.
        // Moving the element *is* the code change, not a trigger for one.
        container.addEventListener('dragover', (event) => {
          // Sortables nest — beats inside sections — so a container only
          // accepts items from a sortable of the same group, innermost first.
          if (!dragging || group(container) !== group(origin.parent)) return;
          event.stopPropagation();
          event.preventDefault();
          event.dataTransfer.dropEffect = 'move';

          const target = persistentChildren(container)
            .filter((child) => child !== dragging)
            .find((child) => {
              const rect = child.getBoundingClientRect();
              return event.clientY < rect.top + rect.height / 2;
            });

          const settled =
            dragging.parentElement === container &&
            (target ?? null) === nextPersistentSibling(dragging);
          if (!settled) container.insertBefore(dragging, target ?? null);
        });

        container.addEventListener('drop', (event) => event.preventDefault());
      }
    }

    function commitMove() {
      const parent = dragging.parentElement;
      const before = nextPersistentSibling(dragging);
      if (parent === origin.parent && before === origin.before) return;

      const op = {
        type: 'move',
        id: marble.id(dragging),
        parentId: marble.id(parent),
        beforeId: marble.id(before),
      };
      const inverse = {
        type: 'move',
        id: marble.id(dragging),
        parentId: marble.id(origin.parent),
        beforeId: marble.id(origin.before),
      };
      marble.record({ redo: [op], undo: [inverse] });
      marble.op(op, { immediate: true });
    }

    // A sortable answers "in what order", which is all a list needs. A canvas
    // answers "where", which a list has no way to say. The position is an
    // inline style on the item itself, so it is one attribute in the file
    // rather than a coordinate table kept beside the document.
    //
    //   <div data-marble-canvas>
    //     <svg data-marble-id="a" style="left:24px;top:12px">…</svg>
    //
    // The attribute's value says whether the canvas is currently accepting
    // drags. A canvas showing a settled arrangement should not be nudged by a
    // stray click, and withdrawing the affordance is itself an ordinary
    // setAttr.

    css.push(`
      [data-marble-canvas]:not([data-marble-canvas="off"]) > * { cursor: grab; }
      [data-marble-canvas] > .marble-moving { cursor: grabbing; user-select: none; }
    `);
    addressable.push('[data-marble-canvas]', '[data-marble-canvas] > *');
    wirers.push(wireCanvas);

    const SNAP = 4;
    const snap = (n) => Math.round(n / SNAP) * SNAP;

    // Reading the attribute back off the element would hand the file the
    // browser's own serialization — respaced, reordered, semicolon appended —
    // which is the same renormalization the patcher exists to avoid. The value
    // is composed here instead, so the bytes in the page and the bytes in the
    // file are the same bytes. Anything else on the element is carried through:
    // a canvas owns where a piece is and nothing else about it.
    function withPosition(style, x, y) {
      const rest = (style ?? '')
        .split(';')
        .map((declaration) => declaration.trim())
        .filter((declaration) => declaration && !/^(left|top)\s*:/i.test(declaration));
      return [`left:${x}px`, `top:${y}px`, ...rest].join(';');
    }

    function wireCanvas(root) {
      for (const canvas of within(root, '[data-marble-canvas]')) {
        if (!claim(canvas, 'canvas')) continue;

        // Delegated, so a piece arriving later needs no wiring of its own.
        canvas.addEventListener('pointerdown', (event) => {
          if (canvas.getAttribute('data-marble-canvas') === 'off') return;
          if (event.button !== 0) return;

          const item = [...persistentChildren(canvas)].find((child) =>
            child.contains(event.target),
          );
          if (!item || !marble.id(item)) return;

          event.preventDefault();
          item.classList.add('marble-moving');
          canvas.setPointerCapture(event.pointerId);

          // Measured rather than read off the style, because a piece may be an
          // <svg>, which has no offsetLeft, and may not carry a position yet.
          const held = item.getBoundingClientRect();
          const grabX = event.clientX - held.left;
          const grabY = event.clientY - held.top;
          const priorStyle = item.getAttribute('style');

          // The page moves during the drag and the file hears about it once,
          // on release: an op per pointermove would be a hundred splices for
          // one gesture, and every one of them a lie about where the piece
          // ended up. Stays null until the pointer actually travels, so a click
          // that only meant to select something doesn't file an op saying it
          // moved.
          let at = null;
          const move = (event) => {
            const box = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
            const x = event.clientX - box.left - grabX;
            const y = event.clientY - box.top - grabY;
            // Shift is the escape hatch from the grid, for the piece that has
            // to sit half a step over.
            at = event.shiftKey
              ? { x: Math.round(x), y: Math.round(y) }
              : { x: snap(x), y: snap(y) };
            item.style.left = `${at.x}px`;
            item.style.top = `${at.y}px`;
          };

          const drop = () => {
            canvas.removeEventListener('pointermove', move);
            item.classList.remove('marble-moving');
            if (!at) return;
            const value = withPosition(item.getAttribute('style'), at.x, at.y);
            item.setAttribute('style', value);
            const op = { type: 'setAttr', id: marble.id(item), name: 'style', value };
            marble.record({
              redo: [op],
              undo: [{ type: 'setAttr', id: marble.id(item), name: 'style', value: priorStyle }],
            });
            marble.op(op, { immediate: true });
          };

          canvas.addEventListener('pointermove', move);
          canvas.addEventListener('pointerup', drop, { once: true });
          canvas.addEventListener('pointercancel', drop, { once: true });
        });
      }
    }

    // <button data-marble-add="#tpl-section" data-marble-into="#outline">
    wirers.push(wireAdders);

    function wireAdders(root) {
      for (const button of within(root, '[data-marble-add]')) {
        if (!claim(button, 'adder')) continue;

        button.addEventListener('click', () => {
          const template = document.querySelector(button.dataset.marbleAdd);
          const into = button.dataset.marbleInto;
          // "prev" targets the list right before the button, so per-item adders
          // don't each need a unique selector.
          const container =
            into === 'prev' ? button.previousElementSibling : document.querySelector(into);
          const seed = template?.content?.firstElementChild;
          if (!seed || !container) {
            console.error('[marble] add: missing template or container', button.dataset);
            return;
          }

          const node = seed.cloneNode(true);
          let thesisSlot = button.dataset.setThesis;
          if (thesisSlot === 'auto') {
            const used = new Set(
              [...document.querySelectorAll('#pool .item[data-kind="thesis"]')]
                .map((el) => el.getAttribute('data-thesis'))
                .filter(Boolean),
            );
            thesisSlot = ['t1', 't2', 't3'].find((t) => !used.has(t)) || 't3';
          }
          if (thesisSlot) node.setAttribute('data-thesis', thesisSlot);
          // Every clone carries the template's one pair of coordinates, so on a
          // canvas the second piece lands exactly under the first and looks
          // like the button did nothing. Step down the diagonal to a free spot.
          if (container.hasAttribute('data-marble-canvas')) {
            const taken = new Set(
              [...container.children]
                .filter((child) => !isTransient(child))
                .map((child) => `${child.style.left},${child.style.top}`),
            );
            let x = parseInt(node.style.left, 10) || 24;
            let y = parseInt(node.style.top, 10) || 24;
            while (taken.has(`${x}px,${y}px`)) { x += 24; y += 24; }
            node.setAttribute('style', withPosition(node.getAttribute('style'), x, y));
          }
          assignIdsIn(node);
          // Serialized before wiring, so no transient chrome lands in the file.
          const html = marble.source.outer(node);

          container.append(node);
          marble.adopt(node);
          const op = { type: 'insert', html, parentId: marble.id(container), beforeId: null };
          const inverse = marble.invert(op);
          if (inverse) marble.record({ redo: [op], undo: [inverse] });
          marble.op(op, { immediate: true });

          // The new row is often itself the editable one — a bare list row, a
          // canvas node — and querySelector only looks at descendants, so it
          // found nothing and the caret was left wherever it happened to be.
          const first = node.matches('[data-marble-editable]')
            ? node
            : node.querySelector('[data-marble-editable]');
          first?.focus();
        });
      }
    }

    // HTML represents exactly one state: a heading is whatever it says, and the
    // three phrasings you were choosing between live in your head or in a
    // comment. <marble-alt> holds them as siblings and shows one. Which one is
    // showing is an attribute in the file, so switching is an ordinary setAttr.
    //
    //   <marble-alt data-marble-id="title" data-marble-active="v2">
    //     <h1 data-marble-alt="v1" data-marble-editable>…</h1>
    //     <h1 data-marble-alt="v2" data-marble-editable>…</h1>
    //   </marble-alt>
    //
    // Which one *displays* is the app's own stylesheet, not this file's
    // business — a document has to show the right version with no host at all.

    css.push(`
      .marble-alts {
        display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 3px; margin-top: .5rem;
        font: 500 10px ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
        user-select: none; opacity: .35; transition: opacity .14s ease;
      }
      marble-alt:hover .marble-alts, .marble-alts:focus-within { opacity: 1; }
      .marble-alts-track {
        position: relative; display: flex; padding: 2px; border-radius: 999px;
        background: rgba(127, 127, 127, .13);
      }
      .marble-alts-thumb {
        position: absolute; top: 2px; bottom: 2px; left: 0; width: 0;
        border-radius: 999px; background: rgba(255, 255, 255, .95);
        box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, .16);
        transition: transform .18s cubic-bezier(.32, .72, 0, 1), width .18s cubic-bezier(.32, .72, 0, 1);
      }
      .marble-alts-v {
        position: relative; padding: 2px 8px; border: 0; border-radius: 999px;
        background: none; color: currentColor; opacity: .5; cursor: pointer;
        font: inherit; letter-spacing: .04em;
      }
      .marble-alts-v[aria-pressed="true"] { opacity: 1; }
      .marble-alts-v:focus-visible { outline: 1px solid #5b8def; outline-offset: 1px; }
      .marble-alts button.marble-alts-edge {
        width: 16px; height: 16px; padding: 0; border: 0; border-radius: 999px;
        background: none; color: currentColor; opacity: .4; cursor: pointer;
        font: inherit; line-height: 16px;
      }
      .marble-alts button.marble-alts-edge:hover { opacity: 1; background: rgba(127, 127, 127, .16); }
      .marble-alts .marble-alts-drop:hover { color: #b4544f; }
    `);
    addressable.push('marble-alt', 'marble-alt > *');
    wirers.push(wireAlternatives);

    const ALT = 'data-marble-alt';
    const ACTIVE = 'data-marble-active';

    const nameOf = (el) => el?.getAttribute(ALT) ?? null;

    function activeVersion(alt) {
      const versions = persistentChildren(alt);
      // An unrecognized name means the file was hand-edited into disagreement.
      // Falling back to the first version keeps something on screen either way.
      return versions.find((el) => nameOf(el) === alt.getAttribute(ACTIVE)) ?? versions[0] ?? null;
    }

    function nextName(versions) {
      const numbered = versions
        .map((el) => Number(/^v(\d+)$/.exec(nameOf(el) ?? '')?.[1]))
        .filter(Number.isFinite);
      return `v${Math.max(versions.length, ...numbered) + 1}`;
    }

    function wireAlternatives(root) {
      for (const alt of within(root, 'marble-alt')) {
        if (!claim(alt, 'alt')) continue;

        const widget = document.createElement('div');
        widget.className = 'marble-alts';
        widget.setAttribute(TRANSIENT, '');
        // The versions may themselves be editable, so the chrome has to be an
        // island the caret can't wander into.
        widget.setAttribute('contenteditable', 'false');
        widget.innerHTML = `
          <span class="marble-alts-track" role="group" aria-label="Versions">
            <span class="marble-alts-thumb"></span>
          </span>
          <button class="marble-alts-edge marble-alts-add" title="Add a version">+</button>
          <button class="marble-alts-edge marble-alts-drop" title="Delete this version">×</button>
        `;
        const track = widget.querySelector('.marble-alts-track');
        const thumb = widget.querySelector('.marble-alts-thumb');
        const drop = widget.querySelector('.marble-alts-drop');

        // No control decides what is on screen: each one writes the attribute
        // and the stylesheet reads it. That is why a switch arriving from a
        // text editor or a model lands exactly the way a click does.
        function setActive(name) {
          if (!name || alt.getAttribute(ACTIVE) === name) return null;
          const id = marble.id(alt);
          const undo = { type: 'setAttr', id, name: ACTIVE, value: alt.getAttribute(ACTIVE) };
          const redo = { type: 'setAttr', id, name: ACTIVE, value: name };
          alt.setAttribute(ACTIVE, name);
          marble.op(redo, { immediate: true });
          return { redo, undo };
        }

        function show(name) {
          const step = setActive(name);
          if (step) marble.record({ redo: [step.redo], undo: [step.undo] });
          render();
        }

        function render() {
          const versions = persistentChildren(alt);

          // A hand-written alternative needn't be named; naming it is a splice,
          // exactly like the ids assigned on first sight.
          versions.forEach((version, index) => {
            if (nameOf(version)) return;
            const name = `v${index + 1}`;
            version.setAttribute(ALT, name);
            if (marble.id(version)) {
              marble.op({ type: 'setAttr', id: marble.id(version), name: ALT, value: name });
            }
          });

          const current = activeVersion(alt);

          // Rebuilt only when the set of versions changes, so an ordinary
          // switch moves the thumb rather than replacing the control under the
          // cursor.
          const names = versions.map(nameOf);
          if (track.dataset.names !== names.join('\u0000')) {
            track.dataset.names = names.join('\u0000');
            for (const stale of track.querySelectorAll('button')) stale.remove();
            for (const name of names) {
              const button = document.createElement('button');
              button.className = 'marble-alts-v';
              button.dataset.name = name;
              button.textContent = name;
              button.addEventListener('click', () => show(name));
              track.append(button);
            }
          }

          for (const button of track.querySelectorAll('button')) {
            const on = button.dataset.name === nameOf(current);
            button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(on));
            if (!on) continue;
            thumb.style.width = `${button.offsetWidth}px`;
            thumb.style.transform = `translateX(${button.offsetLeft}px)`;
          }
          drop.hidden = versions.length < 2;
        }

        widget.querySelector('.marble-alts-add').addEventListener('click', () => {
          const current = activeVersion(alt);
          if (!current) return;

          // The new version starts as a copy of the one on screen, because a
          // candidate phrasing is nearly always an edit of the current one.
          const version = marble.clone(current);
          for (const el of [version, ...version.querySelectorAll(`[${ID}]`)]) {
            el.removeAttribute(ID);
          }
          const name = nextName(persistentChildren(alt));
          version.setAttribute(ALT, name);
          assignIdsIn(version);

          const html = marble.source.outer(version);
          alt.insertBefore(version, widget);
          marble.adopt(version);
          const insert = { type: 'insert', html, parentId: marble.id(alt), beforeId: null };
          marble.op(insert, { immediate: true });
          const active = setActive(name);
          const undo = [marble.invert(insert), active?.undo].filter(Boolean);
          const redo = [insert, active?.redo].filter(Boolean);
          if (undo.length && redo.length) marble.record({ redo, undo });
          render();

          version.focus();
          getSelection()?.selectAllChildren(version);
        });

        drop.addEventListener('click', () => {
          const versions = persistentChildren(alt);
          if (versions.length < 2) return;
          const at = versions.indexOf(activeVersion(alt));
          const doomed = versions[at];
          const next = nameOf(versions[at + 1] ?? versions[at - 1]);
          const remove = { type: 'remove', id: marble.id(doomed) };
          const removeUndo = marble.invert(remove);
          const active = setActive(next);
          doomed.remove();
          marble.op(remove, { immediate: true });
          const undo = [removeUndo, active?.undo].filter(Boolean);
          const redo = [active?.redo, remove].filter(Boolean);
          if (undo.length && redo.length) marble.record({ redo, undo });
          render();
        });

        track.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
          const step = { ArrowLeft: -1, ArrowRight: 1 }[event.key];
          if (!step) return;
          event.preventDefault();
          const versions = persistentChildren(alt);
          const at = versions.indexOf(activeVersion(alt));
          const name = nameOf(versions[(at + step + versions.length) % versions.length]);
          show(name);
          track.querySelector(`[data-name="${CSS.escape(name)}"]`)?.focus();
        });

        alt.append(widget);
        render();
        // The thumb is measured, so it needs one frame in which the text has
        // been laid out — and the widget may have been wired before its font
        // arrived.
        requestAnimationFrame(render);

        new MutationObserver(render).observe(alt, {
          attributes: true,
          attributeFilter: [ACTIVE],
          childList: true,
        });
      }
    }

    // Undo/redo is a carrier capability; binding the keys is the app's call,
    // same as drawing a save indicator. Native inputs keep the browser's own
    // undo — marble editables preventDefault so the stacks do not fight.
    addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
      const mod = event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey;
      if (!mod || event.altKey) return;
      const key = event.key.toLowerCase();
      const isUndo = key === 'z' && !event.shiftKey;
      const isRedo = (key === 'z' && event.shiftKey) || key === 'y';
      if (!isUndo && !isRedo) return;

      const target = event.target;
      if (
        target instanceof HTMLInputElement ||
        target instanceof HTMLTextAreaElement ||
        (target instanceof HTMLElement &&
          target.isContentEditable &&
          !target.hasAttribute('data-marble-editable') &&
          !target.closest('[data-marble-editable]'))
      ) {
        return;
      }

      // A staged proposal is not history yet — Mod+Z discards it instead.
      if (isUndo && typeof marble.discardStaged === 'function' && marble.discardStaged()) {
        event.preventDefault();
        return;
      }

      if (isUndo && marble.canUndo) {
        event.preventDefault();
        marble.undo();
      } else if (isRedo && marble.canRedo) {
        event.preventDefault();
        marble.redo();
      }
    });

    // The carrier reports whether a change reached the file; drawing that is
    // the app's call, and an app that wants no save indicator deletes this.

    css.push(`
      .marble-status {
        position: fixed; right: 1rem; bottom: 1rem; z-index: 9999;
        padding: .3rem .6rem; border-radius: 999px;
        font: 500 11px ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
        letter-spacing: .04em; pointer-events: none;
        opacity: 0; transition: opacity .25s ease;
        background: rgba(20, 22, 26, .92); color: #b7c0cc; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.12);
      }
      .marble-status[data-state="saving"] { opacity: 1; }
      .marble-status[data-state="saved"]  { opacity: .55; }
      .marble-status[data-state="error"]  { opacity: 1; background: #46201f; color: #ff9b9b; }
    `);

    const status = document.createElement('div');
    status.className = 'marble-status';
    status.setAttribute(TRANSIENT, '');
    document.body.append(status);

    let statusTimer = null;
    document.addEventListener('marble:status', ({ detail }) => {
      status.dataset.state = detail.state;
      status.textContent =
        detail.state === 'saving'
          ? 'saving…'
          : detail.state === 'saved'
            ? 'saved'
            : `error — ${detail.message}`;
      clearTimeout(statusTimer);
      if (detail.state === 'saved') {
        statusTimer = setTimeout(() => (status.dataset.state = 'idle'), 1200);
      }
    });

    const style = document.createElement('style');
    style.setAttribute(TRANSIENT, '');
    style.textContent = css.join('\n');
    document.head.append(style);

    // Anything a hand can act on has to be addressable in the file first.
    const ADDRESSABLE = addressable.join(', ');
    marble.address(document.querySelectorAll(ADDRESSABLE));

    // Runs now over the document as it stands, and again over anything that
    // arrives later — an insert, an accepted proposal, an edit made to the file
    // in another window.
    marble.register((root) => {
      for (const wirer of wirers) wirer(root);
    });
  };

  // The carrier is injected after the document has been parsed, so this waits
  // rather than the other way round. No carrier, no affordances: the document
  // is still a page you can read, it just isn't one you can change.
  if (window.marble) begin(window.marble);
  else addEventListener('marble:ready', (event) => begin(event.detail), { once: true });
})();
</script>
<script>
/* The versions strip. Which documents are "the versions" is the folder's own
   answer — sibling .mrbl files beside this one — so nothing here is a fact
   this file keeps a copy of; it is read fresh from marble.docs() every time
   it paints and thrown away, the same way the idea counts in the rail are. */
(() => {
  const slot = document.querySelector('[data-marble-id="rail-versions"]');
  if (!slot) return;

  async function paintVersions() {
    if (!window.marble) return;
    const docs = await window.marble.docs();
    const here = window.marble.app;
    const parent = here.includes('/') ? here.slice(0, here.lastIndexOf('/')) : '';
    const siblings = docs
      .filter((doc) => doc.folder === parent)
      .sort((a, b) => b.modified - a.modified);
    slot.replaceChildren();
    slot.hidden = siblings.length < 2;
    for (const doc of siblings) {
      const isHere = doc.path === here;
      const pill = document.createElement(isHere ? 'span' : 'a');
      pill.className = 'version-pill' + (isHere ? ' is-current' : '');
      if (!isHere) pill.href = window.marble.href(doc.path);
      pill.textContent = doc.title;
      slot.appendChild(pill);
    }
  }

  function begin(marble) {
    marble.register(paintVersions);
  }
  if (window.marble) begin(window.marble);
  else addEventListener('marble:ready', (event) => begin(event.detail), { once: true });
})();
</script>
<script>
/* The phone chrome's behaviour. Everything it creates is
   data-marble-transient: it belongs to the page, not to the file, so none of
   it is ever written back. Nothing here files an op, because none of it is a
   change to the document — it is a way of reading one. Every window.marble
   use is guarded, so the file still opens with no host at all. */

/* ── shell ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
/* ── the phone shell ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

   Four pieces of chrome, all of them the page's and none of them the file's:

     .pm-opts   the one new control — the rest of the rail, on request
     .pm-grab   the sheet's own edge, which is also how you shut it
     .pm-scrim  what "tap outside to dismiss" is made of
     .pm-head   the document's name, and the name of the view the bar is lit on

   Everything else the phone layout does is CSS moving elements that were
   already in the file. This script exists for the three things CSS cannot say:
   which view is current *in words*, whether the sheet is open, and where the
   lit pill is in a strip that scrolls.

   Nothing here files an op, because nothing here is a change to the document.
   Which view you are reading is already written on .stage as data-arrange; the
   name in the header is read back out of it, never stored beside it. Open and
   shut is the page's business and lives in a marble- prefixed class, which the
   carrier hands back after a reconcile instead of replacing with the file's
   idea of it.                                                                */

(function () {
  'use strict';

  var rail  = document.querySelector('.rail');
  var nav   = rail && rail.querySelector('.nav');
  var stage = document.querySelector('.stage');
  if (!rail || !nav || !stage) return;          // a different document: do nothing

  var PHONE = 720;
  function isPhone() {
    /* the same question the stylesheet asks: the width of <html>, which is the
       `doc` container, and not the width of the window — a docked host panel
       narrows the one and leaves the other alone */
    return document.documentElement.clientWidth <= PHONE;
  }
  function calm() {
    return window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
  }

  // ── the chrome ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  function el(tag, cls, attrs) {
    var n = document.createElement(tag);
    n.className = cls;
    n.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
    for (var k in attrs) if (attrs.hasOwnProperty(k)) n.setAttribute(k, attrs[k]);
    return n;
  }

  // the toggle goes first in the bar, so the order a finger reads it in and the
  // order a keyboard reaches it in are the same order
  var optsLi = el('li', 'pm-opts');
  var optsBtn = el('button', '', {
    type: 'button',
    'aria-expanded': 'false',
    'aria-haspopup': 'dialog',
    'aria-label': 'Document options'
  });
  optsBtn.textContent = '⋯';               // ⋯
  optsLi.appendChild(optsBtn);
  nav.insertBefore(optsLi, nav.firstChild);

  var grab = el('button', 'pm-grab', { type: 'button', 'aria-label': 'Close options' });
  rail.insertBefore(grab, rail.firstChild);

  var scrim = el('div', 'pm-scrim');
  document.body.appendChild(scrim);

  var head = el('div', 'pm-head');
  var headDoc = el('span', 'pm-head-doc');
  var headView = el('span', 'pm-head-view');
  head.appendChild(headDoc);
  head.appendChild(headView);
  stage.insertBefore(head, stage.firstChild);

  // ── open and shut ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  var OPEN = 'marble-pm-open';
  var lastFocus = null;

  function open() { return rail.classList.contains(OPEN); }

  function setOpen(on) {
    if (on === open()) return;
    rail.classList.toggle(OPEN, on);
    scrim.classList.toggle('marble-pm-on', on);
    optsBtn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(on));
    if (on) {
      lastFocus = document.activeElement;
      grab.focus({ preventScroll: true });
      rail.scrollTop = 0;
    } else if (lastFocus && document.contains(lastFocus)) {
      lastFocus.focus({ preventScroll: true });
      lastFocus = null;
    }
  }

  optsBtn.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(!open()); });
  grab.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(false); });
  scrim.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(false); });

  document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) {
    if (!open()) return;
    if (e.key === 'Escape') { e.stopPropagation(); setOpen(false); return; }
    if (e.key !== 'Tab') return;
    /* Focus stays inside the sheet while the sheet is over the page, because a
       cursor you cannot see is worse than no cursor. The bar counts as part of
       it — it is literally inside it — so the toggle is the last stop and Tab
       from there comes back round to the top. */
    var stops = rail.querySelectorAll(
      'button, [href], input, select, textarea, [tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"]), [contenteditable=""], [contenteditable="true"]'
    );
    var live = [];
    for (var i = 0; i < stops.length; i++) {
      var s = stops[i];
      if (s.disabled) continue;
      if (!s.getClientRects().length) continue;
      if (getComputedStyle(s).visibility === 'hidden') continue;
      live.push(s);
    }
    if (!live.length) return;
    var first = live[0], last = live[live.length - 1];
    var at = document.activeElement;
    if (e.shiftKey && (at === first || !rail.contains(at))) { e.preventDefault(); last.focus(); }
    else if (!e.shiftKey && (at === last || !rail.contains(at))) { e.preventDefault(); first.focus(); }
  }, true);

  // ── what the header says ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

  /* The lit pill is derived by the document itself, from data-arrange and
     data-show, onto every [data-preset] it found at load. So the name of the
     current view is not a ninth thing to keep — it is read back off whichever
     button the document lit. When no preset matches (a pair of dial settings
     nobody gave a name to) the arrangement's own word stands in. */
  function litPreset() {
    return nav.querySelector('button[data-preset].marble-on');
  }

  function viewName() {
    var lit = litPreset();
    if (lit) {
      var n = lit.querySelector('.n');
      var label = lit.textContent || '';
      if (n) label = label.replace(n.textContent, '');
      return label.trim();
    }
    return (stage.getAttribute('data-arrange') || '').trim();
  }

  function derive() {
    var title = document.querySelector('.rail-title');
    headDoc.textContent = title ? (title.textContent || '').trim() : '';
    headView.textContent = viewName();
  }

  /* Bring the lit pill into view — but only when it is actually out of it, so
     this never argues with a strip the reader is scrolling themselves. */
  function reveal(smooth) {
    if (!isPhone()) return;
    var lit = litPreset();
    if (!lit) return;
    var li = lit.closest('li');
    if (!li) return;
    var bar = nav.getBoundingClientRect();
    var pill = li.getBoundingClientRect();
    var maskedLeft = bar.left + optsLi.getBoundingClientRect().width;
    if (pill.left >= maskedLeft - 1 && pill.right <= bar.right + 1) return;
    var to = nav.scrollLeft + (pill.left - maskedLeft) - 8;
    nav.scrollTo({ left: Math.max(0, to), behavior: smooth && !calm() ? 'smooth' : 'auto' });
  }

  var queued = false;
  function refresh(smooth) {
    if (queued) return;
    queued = true;
    requestAnimationFrame(function () {
      queued = false;
      derive();
      reveal(smooth !== false);
    });
  }

  /* data-arrange and data-show are the facts; the document repaints the pills
     synchronously when they change, so by the time this observer runs the lit
     button is already the right one. This catches every route into a view —
     a pill, a dial, an undo, or the file changing underneath us. */
  new MutationObserver(function () { refresh(true); }).observe(stage, {
    attributes: true,
    attributeFilter: ['data-arrange', 'data-show', 'data-focus']
  });

  // ── phone / not-phone ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  /* The rail is a plain complementary landmark on a wide screen and a sheet on
     a narrow one, and only one of those is worth telling a screen reader about.
     These attributes are the page's reading of the file, so they are re-applied
     from marble.register: a reconcile against the file is exactly the moment a
     page-applied attribute has just been lost. */
  var wasPhone = null;
  function fit() {
    var phone = isPhone();
    if (phone === wasPhone) return;
    wasPhone = phone;
    if (phone) {
      optsBtn.setAttribute('aria-haspopup', 'dialog');
    } else {
      setOpen(false);
    }
    refresh(false);
  }

  function adopt() {
    /* Re-assert everything the page owns. The carrier hands the whole body to
       what a document registered after it has reconciled against the file,
       which is exactly the moment page-owned chrome has just been read over.
       insertBefore moves a node that is already there, so each of these is
       idempotent and also repairs position, not only presence. */
    if (nav.firstChild !== optsLi) nav.insertBefore(optsLi, nav.firstChild);
    if (rail.firstChild !== grab) rail.insertBefore(grab, rail.firstChild);
    if (!document.body.contains(scrim)) document.body.appendChild(scrim);
    if (stage.firstChild !== head) stage.insertBefore(head, stage.firstChild);
    refresh(false);
  }

  if (window.ResizeObserver) {
    new ResizeObserver(fit).observe(document.documentElement);
  } else {
    addEventListener('resize', fit);
  }
  fit();

  addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () { refresh(false); });
  addEventListener('load', function () { refresh(false); });

  function begin(marble) { marble.register(adopt); }
  if (window.marble) begin(window.marble);
  else addEventListener('marble:ready', function () { if (window.marble) begin(window.marble); });
}());

/* ── band ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
/* The band's strip, and the one thing CSS cannot say about it.
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   On a phone the band is a horizontal scroller with its scrollbar hidden, and a
   scroller you cannot reach from a keyboard is a part of the document that only
   exists for people with a finger. Every card in the strip is already a button,
   so tabbing walks through them and the strip follows — but the strip itself
   should also be a place you can land and push with the arrow keys, the way a
   browser now makes any scroller that shows a scrollbar.

   `tabindex` is the page's here and never the file's. It is not a fact about
   this document, it is a fact about whether the strip happens to overflow the
   width it was given right now, so it is derived from that and re-derived every
   time the answer could have changed — including after the carrier reconciles
   the page against the file, which is exactly when a derived attribute has just
   been lost. With no host at all none of this is any different: nothing here
   asks the carrier for anything it cannot do without. */
(() => {
  const carrier = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window.marble : null;

  // So a host never writes this attribute into the file.
  try { carrier && carrier.pageOnly && carrier.pageOnly('tabindex'); } catch (e) { /* older carrier */ }

  let watched = null;

  function reach() {
    const band = document.querySelector('.band');
    if (!band) return;
    const scrolls = band.scrollWidth - band.clientWidth > 1;
    if (scrolls) {
      if (band.getAttribute('tabindex') !== '0') band.setAttribute('tabindex', '0');
    } else if (band.hasAttribute('tabindex')) {
      // Do not leave a tab stop on a strip that has nothing to scroll: on a wide
      // screen the band is a plain row and stopping there says nothing.
      band.removeAttribute('tabindex');
    }
    watch(band);
  }

  // The answer changes when the container is resized, and when a card is pinned
  // or unpinned — which is a child list change the strip's own size never sees.
  function watch(band) {
    if (watched === band) return;
    watched = band;
    if (typeof ResizeObserver === 'function') {
      const ro = new ResizeObserver(reach);
      ro.observe(band);
      ro.observe(document.documentElement);
    } else {
      window.addEventListener('resize', reach);
    }
    if (typeof MutationObserver === 'function') {
      new MutationObserver(reach).observe(band, { childList: true });
    }
  }

  if (carrier && carrier.register) carrier.register(reach);
  if (document.readyState === 'loading') document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', reach);
  else reach();
})();

/* ── columns ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
/* ── sideways, made legible ───────────────────────────────────────────────
   The stylesheet turned the three laned boards into a carousel. This is the
   part CSS cannot do: say which column you are in, and let you get to one
   without swiping through the five in front of it.

   Two pieces of chrome, both the page's and neither the file's. A strip of
   segments above the board — one per column, lit for the one being read,
   tappable. And a pill that surfaces while the board is moving and names the
   column it has landed on, because the lane header cannot stay in view: the
   pool is its own scrollport and it does not scroll vertically, so there is
   nothing for a sticky header to stick to.

   Everything here is derived from where the board is scrolled to and from the
   lane elements already in the file. Nothing is stored, nothing is filed, and
   every use of the carrier is guarded — with no host at all this still works,
   it just forgets nothing, because there was never anything to remember. */
(() => {
  const stage = document.querySelector('.stage');
  const pool = document.querySelector('.pool');
  if (!stage || !pool) return;

  const LANED = ['form', 'when', 'term'];
  const reduced = matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');

  /* The breakpoint is written down once, in the container query, and read
     back from here. A script that measures the window for itself is a second
     copy of that decision, and the two go out of step the first time a host
     docks a panel beside the document. */
  const phone = () =>
    getComputedStyle(pool).getPropertyValue('--rv-phone').trim() === '1';

  const arrange = () => stage.getAttribute('data-arrange');
  const carousel = () => phone() && LANED.includes(arrange());

  const lanes = () =>
    [...pool.children].filter(
      (el) => el.classList.contains('lane') && el.getClientRects().length > 0,
    );

  // ── the chrome ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  let strip = null;
  let count = null;
  let track = null;
  let hud = null;

  function ensureChrome() {
    if (!strip || !strip.isConnected) {
      strip = document.createElement('nav');
      strip.className = 'rv-cols';
      strip.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
      strip.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Columns');

      count = document.createElement('span');
      count.className = 'rv-cols-n';
      strip.append(count);

      track = document.createElement('span');
      track.className = 'rv-cols-track';
      strip.append(track);

      builtFor = '';
      pool.parentElement.insertBefore(strip, pool);
    }
    if (!hud || !hud.isConnected) {
      hud = document.createElement('div');
      hud.className = 'rv-hud';
      hud.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
      // The strip's buttons carry aria-current, which is the accessible
      // answer to the same question. The pill is the visual echo of it, and
      // announcing both would only say it twice.
      hud.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      document.body.append(hud);
    }
  }

  /* One button per lane. Rebuilt only when the set of lanes changes — which
     is a view switch, not a scroll — so a swipe touches nothing but two
     attributes and a string. */
  let builtFor = '';
  function buildTrack(list) {
    const key = arrange() + ':' + list.length;
    if (key === builtFor) return;
    builtFor = key;
    track.textContent = '';
    list.forEach((lane, i) => {
      const dot = document.createElement('button');
      dot.type = 'button';
      dot.className = 'rv-dot';
      dot.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
      const head = lane.querySelector('h4');
      dot.setAttribute('aria-label', (head && head.textContent.trim()) || 'Column ' + (i + 1));
      dot.addEventListener('click', () => {
        lane.scrollIntoView({
          inline: 'start',
          block: 'nearest',
          behavior: reduced.matches ? 'auto' : 'smooth',
        });
        // The pill is the receipt for a tap as much as for a swipe: the board
        // is about to move under a finger that is not on it.
        flash();
      });
      track.append(dot);
    });
  }

  // ── which column ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  /* Measured against the snapport rather than by arithmetic on scrollLeft:
     the column width is a min() of two units and the trailing padding is a
     calc() of a third, so the browser's own numbers are the only ones worth
     trusting here. */
  function currentIndex(list) {
    const left = pool.getBoundingClientRect().left;
    const pad = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(pool).scrollPaddingLeft) || 0;
    let best = 0;
    let nearest = Infinity;
    list.forEach((lane, i) => {
      const d = Math.abs(lane.getBoundingClientRect().left - left - pad);
      if (d < nearest) { nearest = d; best = i; }
    });
    return best;
  }

  let shown = -1;
  function sync() {
    ensureChrome();
    if (!carousel()) {
      builtFor = '';
      shown = -1;
      hud.removeAttribute('data-rv-on');
      hud.classList.remove('rv-lit');
      return;
    }
    const list = lanes();
    if (!list.length) return;
    buildTrack(list);
    hud.setAttribute('data-rv-on', '');

    const at = currentIndex(list);
    if (at === shown) return;
    shown = at;

    const place = (at + 1) + ' / ' + list.length;
    count.textContent = place;
    [...track.children].forEach((dot, i) => {
      if (i === at) dot.setAttribute('aria-current', 'true');
      else dot.removeAttribute('aria-current');
    });

    const head = list[at].querySelector('h4');
    hud.textContent = '';
    const n = document.createElement('b');
    n.textContent = place;
    hud.append(n, document.createTextNode((head && head.textContent.trim()) || ''));
  }

  // ── the pill's life ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  let fade = 0;
  function flash() {
    if (!carousel()) return;
    hud.classList.add('rv-lit');
    clearTimeout(fade);
    fade = setTimeout(() => hud.classList.remove('rv-lit'), 1100);
  }

  /* A scroll fires far faster than anything on screen can change, so the work
     is coalesced onto one frame and the listener itself does nothing but ask
     for that frame. */
  let queued = false;
  pool.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
    if (queued) return;
    queued = true;
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      queued = false;
      sync();
      flash();
    });
  }, { passive: true });

  // ── the full bleed ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  /* Everything that scrolls sideways on a phone — the board and each framing
     — is pulled out to the screen edges, so what cuts the next column off is
     the screen and not a text margin. How far out is the stage's own side
     padding, which is not a number this file gets to assume: it is measured.

     The answer is published through a page-only stylesheet rather than as an
     inline style on the pool, because the pool is an element in the file and
     the framings are in a different part of it. One derived declaration on
     <body>, inherited by both, and nothing addressable is touched. */
  let sheet = null;
  let bled = '';
  function bleed() {
    if (!sheet || !sheet.isConnected) {
      sheet = document.createElement('style');
      sheet.setAttribute('data-marble-transient', '');
      // In the body rather than the head, because that is where a carrier
      // looks for what belongs to the page. If it goes missing anyway the
      // two lengths fall back to the ones written in the stylesheet, which
      // are the ones this document has today — so the worst case is that the
      // board stops keeping up with a padding change, not that it breaks.
      document.body.append(sheet);
      bled = '';
    }
    const host = pool.parentElement;
    if (!host) return;
    const box = host.getBoundingClientRect();
    const cs = getComputedStyle(host);
    const l = box.left + parseFloat(cs.borderLeftWidth) + parseFloat(cs.paddingLeft);
    const r = document.documentElement.clientWidth
      - (box.right - parseFloat(cs.borderRightWidth) - parseFloat(cs.paddingRight));
    const next = 'body{--rv-bleed-l:' + Math.max(0, Math.round(l)) + 'px;'
      + '--rv-bleed-r:' + Math.max(0, Math.round(r)) + 'px}';
    if (next === bled) return;
    bled = next;
    sheet.textContent = next;
  }

  // ── the map, on a phone ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  /* The map arrangement switches the pool's canvas affordance on, because on
     a wide screen the whole point of the view is moving cards around. At this
     width the stylesheet has stacked those cards in file order, so a drag
     would file coordinates describing a layout nobody can see — and the
     inverse of that op is another set of coordinates, so undo would not even
     make it right.

     The affordance itself is left alone: turning it off is a fact about the
     document, it would file an op, and it would still be off when the same
     file opened on a laptop tomorrow. What is suppressed is the gesture, and
     only at this width. A listener in the capture phase on the pool runs
     before the pool's own pointerdown handler, which is where a drag begins.
     Clicks, taps and typing are separate events and are untouched. */
  pool.addEventListener('pointerdown', (event) => {
    if (!phone() || arrange() !== 'map') return;
    event.stopPropagation();
  }, true);

  // ── keeping up ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  /* A view switch changes which lanes exist and how many of them. It also
     leaves the board scrolled to wherever the last view was left, which on a
     narrower board is a column that is no longer there — so every switch
     opens on the first column, the way arriving at a board should. */
  new MutationObserver(() => {
    bleed();
    if (carousel()) pool.scrollTo({ left: 0, behavior: 'auto' });
    shown = -1;
    builtFor = '';
    sync();
  }).observe(stage, { attributes: true, attributeFilter: ['data-arrange'] });

  new ResizeObserver(() => {
    bleed();
    shown = -1;
    sync();
  }).observe(document.documentElement);

  /* A reconcile against the file is exactly the moment a page-only node has
     just been lost and a derived value has just gone stale, so the carrier is
     asked to say when that happens. With no host it never does, and the call
     below is the whole of it. */
  function adopt(marble) {
    if (!marble || !marble.register) return;
    marble.register(() => { ensureChrome(); bleed(); shown = -1; sync(); });
  }
  if (window.marble) adopt(window.marble);
  else addEventListener('marble:ready', (e) => adopt(e.detail), { once: true });

  ensureChrome();
  bleed();
  sync();
})();

</script>
</body>
</html>
